{"id": "218294", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218294", "title": "Guadalajara International Airport", "text": "Guadalajara International Airport () is in the western state of Jalisco, Mexico. The airport serves the greater Guadalajara area and is Mexico's third busiest airport. It is a hub for Volaris, which flies to about 25 Mexican destinations from Guadalajara. The airport is served by most major North American airlines. \nUnderstand.\nThe airport's long formal name is \"Aeropuerto Internacional de Guadalajara Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla\". In 2025, the airport served 18.7 million passengers. \nTerminals.\nThe airport has two terminals, but the vast majority of flights use Terminal 1 (the main terminal), Terminal 1 is divided into separate zones, designated 1A and 1C. 1A is gates for most domestic flights. 1C is gates for most international flights. Terminal 2 is a much smaller terminal with limited traveler services. It is used by Viva Aerobus so their passengers never forget that they're on a no-frills, low-cost carrier.\nThere are 43 gates in Terminal 1. Gates preceded by an \"A\" are airside walk-up gates on the lower level. You will board by having your boarding pass scanned at a door directly onto the tarmac where you'll climb steps onto the aircraft. Gates preceded by a \"B\" or \"C\" are standard jetways onto the aircraft. Gates preceded by a \"D\" will board a bus that will take you to the aircraft, parked elsewhere on the tarmac, away from the terminal building. \nExpect frequent roadway closures and service interruptions as the airport undergoes a major expansion beginning in 2026. The expansion will replace the small Terminal 2 with a huge new replacement designed to be a zero carbon, eco friendly facility that will increase the airport's passenger capacity to 40 million per year.\nFlights.\nAirlines serving GDL include Mexican carriers AeroMexico, Viva Aerobus, and Volaris. U.S. carriers include Alaska Airlines, American Airlines, Delta, and United, with direct flights from Los Angeles, Dallas, Miami, Atlanta, Houston and Denver. Canadian carriers include Air Canada, Flair, and WestJet with flights from Toronto, Vancouver and Calgary. Panamanian carrier Copa offers direct flights from Panama City. Aeromexico offers direct flights from Madrid and from Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport.\nGround transportation.\nTaxis are the most flexible way to get from the airport to any part of the metropolitan area (but also the most expensive). At the airport, always buy the taxi chit from the booth before exiting the terminal, then present it to the licensed driver. Many hotels offer airport pickups that can be cheaper.\nUber and other ride-hailing app drivers are not allowed to pick up passengers at the airport. A common way to get around this limitation is to walk outside the airport a few minutes away to the nearby UPS distribution center (labeled as \"Centro de Envios UPS\" on Google maps). Set your Uber pick up location at this UPS center, which is outside the airport's restrictions and a driver will pick you up there.\nBy bus.\nThe MiMacro BRT buses serve the airport using Line 5 to the Aeropuerto station. The MiMacro station is open daily from 03:30 to 22:30. It costs M$11 (about US$0.60) per ride. Fares must be paid using a \"Mi Movilidad\" prepaid card, which can be purchased in the stations. Destinations served by the Line 5 Mi Macro include popular traveler spots like Parque Agua Azul (downtown), Expo Guadalajara, and Estadio Akron. The app Moovit might find a better bus connection from the airport to your accommodation than Google Maps (as of Dec 2021). \nMi Transporte El\u00e9ctrico route C98 picks up at Terminal 1 near the FedEx facility. Buses are air conditioned and have racks for luggage. Buses go to the Periferico Norte station. Fares cost M$10 and are paid in coins to the driver.\nThere is not a dedicated long-distance bus station, but some bus companies pick up at the airport. There are frequent pickups by the Chapala bus line offering regional service to towns around Lake Chapala or to the Central Vieja (old bus station). To use these buses, follow signs in the terminal to \"Transportes Terrestres\". If you plan to take an onward long-distance bus, be aware that most of those buses depart from the Central Camionera (new bus station), not from Central Vieja (which has mostly regional buses).\nGet around.\nGuadalajara International Airport is one of the least complicated airports to navigate. Most airlines operate out of a single terminal and all gates are essentially along a single (very) long corridor. Signage is ample and excellent with both Spanish and English. There are no complicated trams, no twisty labyrinths, and no need to go up and down escalators or stairways.\nWait.\nSeating areas are available at all gates, but like at most airports, they get crowded close to flight boarding time. All seating clusters have charging areas for electronic devices with 110 volt AC plugs (same as used in the US) as well as USB ports. Outlets and ports are typically located directly below the seat. Seats close to the charging stations tend to be the most popular, so get to the gate early if you want to charge your phone.\nLounges.\nThe airport has three lounges. VIP Lounge East and West both sell daily daily admission passes for M$600 (about US$30). The Aeromexico Salon Premier is limited to the airline's elite frequent fliers and first class passengers. The two VIP lounges are open 24 hours.\nEat and drink.\nGuadalajara International Airport (GDL) has a mix of dining options ranging from fast food grease pits to sit-down bistros. Casual dining spots occupy a mid-level spot on the quality spectrum. All of the restaurants are in the main concourse of Terminal 1 (inside security). If you need a place outside security, Fruty's is outside, near the main entrance. \nSome of the better eateries in the terminal include:\nFamily-friendly options:\nGrab and go:\nBuy.\nGuadalajara International Airport has a variety of shops selling overpriced things you don't need anyway. \nDuty-free stores are in both the international departures and arrivals areas. They sell alcohol, fragrances, and tobacco. \nMost stores and shops are in the main concourse of Terminal 1 in the departures area (after security). Many are just predictable international brands with many others being your typical airport newsstand selling trinkets, magazines, and candy, (though there is an Oxxo store that has some better quality junk food for you to stock up on).\nThere are also a couple of interesting stores:\nConnect.\nWiFi internet service is available in all parts of the main terminal. From your network settings, select \"GAP FREE\". You will be asked to agree to terms and conditions.\nOutside the airport, 5G service is ubiquitous in all parts of Guadalajara. The major carrier is Telcel.\nCope.\nThe airport has a number of facilities to make life easier on the traveling public. These include:\nSleep.\nThere is one hotel on the airport premises: a Hilton Garden Inn. There are also several hotels within a couple miles of the airport, most offering free shuttle service to and from the main terminal. The closest is the Hangar Inn, across the highway from the airport entrance. If you have a longer layover, the Tlaqapaque area is nearby, has some good boutique hotels and is delightfully scenic. "}
{"id": "218329", "revid": "2257405", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218329", "title": "Maccheroncini di Campofilone Trail", "text": "The Maccheroncini di Campofilone Trail is the second tourist and food and wine itinerary created within the WikiM.A.R.C.A project. The route winds through the area around Campofilone, famous for its artisanal production of egg pasta, and particularly the Maccheroncini di Campofilone. \nThe tour takes visitors on a journey to discover the artisanal tradition of Maccheroncini di Campofilone, with the involvement of local producers and the community. This trail offers an opportunity to learn about the techniques used to make egg pasta, explore the history of the product and discover the local producers, providing an authentic experience rooted in the region\u2019s food and wine traditions.\nThis itinerary is aimed at food lovers and those who wish to combine a seaside holiday with exploring the inland areas and local traditions, particularly those linked to pasta-making, for a more authentic experience. It is also ideal for those interested in the artisan food production carried out by local producers.\nUnderstand.\nJust 3 km from the Adriatic coast, nestled in the hills of the Val d\u2019Aso, lies the medieval village of Campofilone, which grew up around the Abbey of St Bartholomew the Apostle. The historic centre still retains the charm of its ancient walls, and it overlooks landscape that stretches from the Sibillini Mountains, across the cultivated hills, al the way to the sea. \nCampofilone is one of Italy\u2019s most important egg pasta-producing districts and a key player in the \u2019s food and wine industry. Different types of egg pasta are produced here, fresh and dry, among which the Maccheroncini di Campofilone stands out: a product of excellence with Protected Geographical Indication (PGI) status from the Marche region and a symbol of local identity, that plays a central role in this food and wine tour. Its significance is not merely gastronomic, but forms part of a broader effort to promote the food culture of the Piceno area, linked to the principles of the Mediterranean diet.\nIn the Fermo area, the Piceno Mediterranean Diet Laboratory Association is actively involved in promoting a dietary lifestyle inspired by local traditions. Within this context, Maccheroncini di Campofilone represent a significant example of how traditional food products embody the cultural identity of the region. \nTheir international success has made them one of the most renowned culinary specialties of the Piceno area. In 1998, the Marche Region officially recognized them as a Traditional Agri-food Product (Prodotto Tipico Tradizionale), and in 2013 they were awarded PGI (Protected Geographical Indication) status, becoming the first Italian egg pasta to be protected by this designation.\nMaccheroncini di Campofilone are an egg pasta produced in the municipality area of Campofilone. They are characterized by an exceptionally thin pasta sheet and a high proportion of fresh eggs in the dough, significantly greater than that used in most other pasta varieties. The same thin sheet is also used to prepare other egg pasta shapes, both long varieties such as fettuccine and short varieties such as penne, as well as filled pasta types including tortellini.\nAccording to local tradition, Maccheroncini were already known in the 15th century, when they were referred to as \"maccheroncini fini fini\" (\"very fine macaroni\") or \"capelli d'angelo\" (\"angel hair\"). The pasta is also associated with, who included it among his favourite dishes in the famous list of \"49 foods\" recorded in his writings.\nThe traditional recipe, passed down through generations, uses only soft wheat flour and fresh eggs, with no water added. The dough is rolled into very thin sheets, cut into fine strands, and left to dry using traditional methods. This process gives the pasta a distinctive porosity, making it particularly well suited to absorbing a variety of sauces, from the classic meat rag\u00f9 to fish and vegetable-based sauces.\nIn 2023, the pasta makers of Campofilone united to form the Consortium for the Protection of PGI Maccheroncini di Campofilone, an organization established to safeguard and promote the product while generating economic and tourism benefits for the Aso Valley.\nThe itinerary follows the Aso Valley to the historic centre of Campofilone, including visits to pasta producers and their retail outlets, where visitors can purchase the product. Travelling by car is recommended, although the route is also suitable for cycling enthusiasts.\nPrepare.\nYou can do this itinerary throughout the year, as pasta factory in the area are generally open to visitors and the climate is favourable in most seasons. \nWinter tends to be quieter and there are fewer events, while spring and summer offer a livelier atmosphere and greener landscapes. \nThe summer is a particularly good time to visit, thanks to the town\u2019s proximity to the Adriatic coast, which allows you to combine a food and wine tour with days spent at the beach. \nFurthermore, in the first ten days of August, the Campofilone Pro Loco organises the National Festival of Maccheroncini di Campofilone, where visitors can sample the maccheroncini produced by the six pasta makers of the Consortium for the Protection of Campofilone Maccheroncini PGI, made according to the traditional recipe with a rich meat sauce.\nSpring and early autumn offer ideal conditions for visiting the area, with mild temperatures and picturesque landscapes along the Aso river valley, which are also perfect for cycling routes.\nGet in.\nThe best way to reach the itinerary stops is using the car: Whether you're coming from the north or the south, take the Pedaso exit on the A14 motorway. From there, take the Val d'Aso state road (SS 433) towards Campofilone.\nThe itinerary can also be completed by bicycle and is particularly suitable for cyclists wishing to explore the Aso Valley and the surrounding countryside. While it can be started from any point along the route, a convenient access point is the Adriatic coast, with railway stations in towns such as Civitanova Marche, San Benedetto del Tronto, or Pedaso providing connections to the area. From the coast, cyclists can follow local and provincial roads inland toward Campofilone and the Aso Valley. Traffic is generally light, and the route combines gentle valley sections with some rolling hills typical of the Marche countryside.\nGo.\nThis route winds through the Campofilone area, crossing the Aso valley to the historic centre of the village. The itinerary includes visits to maccheroncini producers and local shops, offering the chance to experience the tradition of Campofilone egg pasta at first hand.\nThe route is best covered by car and can be completed in a day.\nCampofilone.\nLeave the A14 at Pedaso, follow Provincial Road 238 for about 2 km, then turn right to reach the first stop: the pasta factory. It is one of the historic businesses specialising in the production, according to the traditional recipe, of Campofilone IGP maccheroncini, and continues to uphold the local tradition of egg pasta through a production process that combines artisanal methods with modern techniques. The company also organises guided tours and activities designed to explore the various stages of pasta production, offering visitors the chance to gain a deeper understanding of Campofilone\u2019s culinary heritage. At the company\u2019s shop, you can purchase typical local products made in-house, such as stuffed olives, cremini and breaded mozzarella. \nJust a stone's throw away (1-minute drive) is the pasta factory which produces fresh egg pasta and dried egg pasta (including maccheroncini), as well as other products such as stuffed olives, fried cremini mushrooms, breaded mozzarella balls, tortellini and ravioli. Its main outlets are located in Porto San Giorgio (FM). \nContinue along Provincial Road 238 and, after 4 minutes, you will reach the third stop on this route on your left:. La Campofilone is the only farm-based pasta factory in the Campofilone district and produces pasta using eggs from its own farm. The company has deep roots in the history of the village and was founded by the visionary housewife Adorna Albanesi, a central figure in the tradition of the famous Maccheroncini di Campofilone PGI. Over the years, La Campofilone has built an integrated model based on agricultural practices, developing an internal supply chain that starts with rearing hens and extends all the way to pasta production. In the shop, you can buy both La Campofilone pasta and the farm\u2019s excellent eggs, which are also used daily in the pasta factory\u2019s production.\nIn 5 minutes, still on Provincial Road 238, you will arrive at the pasta factory, the fourth stop on the itinerary. Thanks to the tradition handed down by Grandma Adelina, the Marcozzi family has made Campofilone egg pasta the heart of their business. Starting out as a small artisan workshop in the Campofilone area, the company has evolved over time into a well-structured, modern business. Today, the Marcozzi pasta factory in Campofilone represents a meeting point between tradition and innovation: artisanal pasta-making is combined with up-to-date production processes, whilst keeping the quality and identity of this typical local product intact.\nHead back along the SP 238, turn right onto Contrada Valdaso, continue straight on to the SP 10, turn right again onto Via Santa Maria, and you will find the fifth stop on your right:. Founded in 1958 and specialising in the production of egg pasta according to local tradition, the company is one of the most established expressions of the village\u2019s gastronomic culture, carrying on artisanal expertise handed down through the generations within the family. Now in its third generation, the business passed from the aunts to the mother of the current owners, eventually transforming into an artisan workshop specialising in pasta production, where 100% Italian durum wheat semolina and pasteurised eggs from the Marche region are used.\nFor the sixth stop, continue along Via Santa Maria, then take the SP10 and then Via XX Settembre until you reach on your left: since as early as 1850, the Carassai family has been processing grain in the historic watermill at Monte San Martino, a local landmark for flour production. In 2010, Leonardo Carassai took over one of the village\u2019s historic pasta factories, breathing new life into the local culinary heritage through a balance of craftsmanship, supply chain and innovation. The range includes various types of dried egg pasta typical of Campofilone, including Maccheroncini di Campofilone PGI, chitarra, linguine, fettuccine, tagliatelle and pappardelle, as well as special lines inspired by the traditions of the Marche region. Among the main products are the \u201cL\u2019Oro di Campofilone\u201d range, made with the ancient Senatore Cappelli wheat variety prized for its aromatic profile, nutritional qualities and superior digestibility, and \u201cDeci\u2019Ova\u201d, characterised by the use of ten eggs per kilogramme of flour and flour sourced from Molino Carassai. The company also produces traditional straw-paper packaging for the gift sector. A significant proportion of the production is destined for export, with distribution across various European and American countries. The pasta factory also organises guided tours (in Italian, English and Spanish) by appointment, as well as tastings dedicated to the tradition of Maccheroncini di Campofilone. \nAlmost opposite the Carassai pasta factory (within walking distance) lies the penultimate stop on the itinerary:. The Corona pasta factory was founded in the 19th century amongst the aristocratic circles of Naples, where Duke Doria De Serris commissioned a special pasta to be made for his guests. The dough, rolled out extremely thinly using traditional methods, soon became renowned in the aristocratic circles of the time. Production was then entrusted to a small pasta factory linked to the Corona family, marking the start of a tradition that has been handed down through the generations. In the early 20th century, the family moved to the Marche region, settling in Campofilone, where egg pasta is still produced today according to the ancient local recipe.\nFinally, a one-minute drive or a five-minute walk will take you to the final stop on the route: the pasta factory. Take the Borgo S. Patrizio/SP10 road and turn right. The company was founded in 1933 by Nello Spinosi, who began taking the homemade maccheroncini made by his wife Maria on his trips to Rome, introducing them to and gaining appreciation for them beyond the local area. From this idea, the first artisan workshop dedicated to the production and sale of Campofilone pasta was born, still visible today in its original premises opposite the Abbey of San Bartolomeo. Over time, the family business has grown across three generations, always keeping artisanal production and the quality of raw materials at the heart of its operations, until it became a nationally and internationally recognised benchmark for egg pasta.\nStay safe.\nPlease observe the Highway Code, paying attention to speed limits and taking care when driving on narrower or winding sections, which are typical of hilly roads.\nThe route follows a county road that is generally in good condition and easily accessible by car. The route passes through a hilly landscape, alternating between flatter sections, with traffic generally flowing smoothly and usually at a moderate level.\nGo next.\nIn addition to \"Maccheroncini di Campofilone\", the Aso Valley is particularly renowned for its fruit production and is considered one of the most important fresh-fruit growing areas in the Marche region. The Val d'Aso peach, in particular, is one of the Piceno area's leading horticultural products and has been awarded the QM quality mark. Other fruits, including apricots, cherries, apples, pears, plums, and damsons, are also cultivated using traditional methods and sold through local supply chains.\nFruit growing is complemented by a strong olive-growing tradition, with high-quality extra virgin olive oil produced from the numerous olive groves that characterize the valley's rolling landscape.\nThe area is also dotted with well-tended vineyards that produce some of the region's most important wines, including Rosso Piceno DOC, Pecorino, and Falerio DOC, making the valley an attractive destination for food and wine tourism.\nSeveral seaside destinations can be found along the Adriatic coast near Campofilone, starting with Torre di Palme, a picturesque medieval village perched above the coast, and continuing to Pedaso, a small town at the mouth of the Aso River where the sea and hills meet in a tranquil and authentic setting. Further south lie Cupra Marittima and Grottammare, two well-known resorts of the Riviera delle Palme, noted for their long sandy beaches and historic centres overlooking the sea.\nInland, visitors will find historic hill towns offering panoramic views and a rich cultural heritage, including Ripatransone, famous for claiming Italy's narrowest alley (43 cm wide) and for its sweeping coastal vistas; Montefiore dell'Aso, a charming fortified village surrounded by rolling hills; and Acquaviva Picena, dominated by its impressive medieval fortress and a remarkably well-preserved historic centre."}
{"id": "218330", "revid": "2257405", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218330", "title": "Campofilone", "text": "Campofilone is a town in the Marche region, situated on the coast of the province of Fermo. It is a tourist destination that can be enjoyed all year round thanks to its mild climate, due to its proximity to the coast. During the summer, this town in the Val d'Aso is also a high-quality seaside resort, as demonstrated by the Blue Flag it has held since 2025.\nUnderstand.\nSituated on a hill close to the coastline, Campofilone is a municipality in the Val d'Aso. It borders the municipalities of Pedaso, Altidona, Lapedona, Massignano (AP) and Montefiore dell'Aso (AP).\nHistory.\nThe history of Campofilone has its roots in antiquity, as evidenced by the Picene and Roman artefacts preserved in the local parish archaeological museum. The present-day historic centre of Campofilone dates back to the 11th century, having developed around the Benedictine Abbey of San Bartolomeo, built in 1066 on the ruins of a Roman villa.\nIn the 13th century, the castle of Campofilone was declared a free commune and remained so until the 14th century, when the city of Fermo brought it under its jurisdiction. During the period of Fermo\u2019s rule, the imposing walls surrounding the present-day historic centre were constructed.\nMaccheroncini di Campofilone.\nOne of the region\u2019s main attractions is the Maccheroncini di Campofilone PGI, a product that symbolises the local identity. Its significance is not merely gastronomic, but forms part of a broader effort to promote the food culture of the Piceno area, linked to the principles of the Mediterranean diet.\nIn the Fermo area, the Piceno Laboratory of the Mediterranean Diet Association is also active, committed to promoting a dietary style inspired by local tradition. In this context, Maccheroncini di Campofilone represent a significant example of how traditional products are an expression of the cultural identity of the region.\nMaccheroncini di Campofilone is an egg pasta produced in the municipality of the same name, characterised by a very thin sheet of dough and a high proportion of fresh eggs in the mixture, higher than in other types of pasta. The same dough is also used to make other shapes of egg pasta, both long (fettuccine) and short (penne), as well as filled varieties such as tortellini.\nAccording to local tradition, maccheroncini have been known since the 15th century and were referred to as \u201cmaccheroncini fini fini\u201d or \u201ccapelli d\u2019angelo\u201d. Furthermore, maccheroncini are linked to the figure of, who included them among his favourite dishes in the famous list of \u201c49 foods\u201d noted in his writings.\nThe recipe, handed down through the generations, uses only soft wheat flour and fresh eggs, with no added water. The dough is rolled out into thin sheets, cut into very fine strands and left to dry using traditional methods, which give the product a distinctive porous texture, ideal for holding various types of sauce, from classic meat sauce to fish or vegetable sauces.\nRecognised as a Traditional Local Product by the Marche Region in 1998, Maccheroncini di Campofilone have held the PGI (Protected Geographical Indication) designation since 2013, safeguarding their origin and production methods. In 2023, the pasta makers of Campofilone came together to form the Consortium for the Protection of Maccheroncini di Campofilone PGI, an organisation established with the aim of protecting and promoting the maccheroncini and ensuring economic and tourism benefits for the Val d\u2019Aso.\nCultural events and festivals.\nIn the first ten days of August, the Pro Loco of Campofilone organises the National Festival of Maccheroncini di Campofilone, where visitors can sample the maccheroncini produced by the pasta makers of the Consorzio di tutela dei Maccheroncini di Campofilone PGI, made according to the traditional recipe with a rich meat sauce.\nSpring and early autumn offer ideal conditions for visiting the area, with mild temperatures and picturesque landscapes along the Aso river valley, which are also perfect for cycling routes.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nTo reach Campofilone by car, take the A14 motorway and exit at Pedaso. From there, take the Val d'Aso state road 433 (SS 433) towards Campofilone.\nBy train.\nTo reach Campofilone by train, we recommend taking a regional train to Porto San Giorgio. From there, head to the bus stops on the SS16 and take the Fermo\u2013Val d\u2019Aso\u2013Montalto bus towards Montalto. Get off after seven stops at Campofilone (towards Montefiore).\nBy bus.\nYou can reach Campofilone by public transport from Fermo. From the terminal at the large car park, take the Fermo\u2013Val D\u2019Aso\u2013Montalto bus towards Montalto and get off at the Campofilone (towards Montefiore) stop (20 stops). Journey time: approximately 30 minutes.\nBy plane.\nThere is no airport. The most convenient airport is, formerly Ancona Falconara Airport.\nGet around.\nThe best way to explore the Campofilone is by car, as the itinerary extends throughout the Aso Valley and includes several villages, pasta producers, and rural attractions. However, it can also be enjoyed by bicycle, particularly by those wishing to discover the countryside at a slower pace. \nBy foot.\nWalking is practical within the historic centres of Campofilone and the other villages along the route, where most attractions are located within a short distance of one another. However, the full itinerary is too extensive to be completed entirely on foot.\nBy bike.\nThe itinerary can be completed by bicycle and is well suited to cyclists interested in exploring the landscapes of the Aso Valley. Quiet local roads connect Campofilone with neighbouring villages, farms, vineyards, and fruit-growing areas. While much of the route follows gentle terrain along the valley floor, some sections include rolling hills typical of the Marche countryside. Railway stations along the Adriatic coast, such as Pedaso, provide convenient access for cyclists arriving by train.\nBy car.\nA car is the most practical means of transport for completing the itinerary, allowing visitors to easily reach the various pasta producers, shops, villages, and points of interest scattered throughout the valley. Roads are generally quiet and well maintained, making travel between destinations straightforward.\nSee.\nDespite its small size, Campofilone offers a number of points of interest, including historic churches, small museums and traditional buildings that reflect the town\u2019s cultural and rural heritage. \nBuy.\nCampofilone is one of Italy's most renowned egg-pasta production districts and an important centre within the Marche region's food and wine sector. A variety of fresh and dried egg pasta is produced here, most notably Maccheroncini di Campofilone, a PGI-certified specialty and one of Marche's best-known gastronomic products. Since 2023, local pasta makers have been united under the Consortium for the Protection of PGI Maccheroncini di Campofilone, established to safeguard and promote this traditional product.\nConnect.\nTelephone.\nThe local area code is 0734; international code is +39 if calling from outside Italy (you keep the 0 when dialing a number; i.e. +390341...). Mobile operators active in the area are Tim, Tre (roaming) and Vodafone. 3G with Tim appears to be the one working at best."}
{"id": "218346", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218346", "title": "Dharmasthala", "text": "Dharmasthala (\u0ca7\u0cb0\u0ccd\u0cae\u0cb8\u0ccd\u0ca5\u0cb3), literally a \"religious place\", is a temple town in Dakshina Kannada, on the banks of the Netravati River. While the town has earned headlines in late 2025 due to the disturbing allegations of mass graves of raped women and children, Dharmasthala is otherwise home to a popular Hindu temple and two museums."}
{"id": "218389", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218389", "title": "Monterrey International Airport", "text": "Monterrey International Airport () is the main airport for the city of Monterrey in Northern Mexico. It is the main operational hub for Viva Aerobus and is a second hub for Aeromexico. The airport provides connections between most Mexican destinations and major cities throughout North America as well as Madrid and Paris in Europe and Tokyo and Seoul in Asia.\nUnderstand.\nThe airport is the largest in Northern Mexico. It is formally named \"General Mariano Escobedo International Airport\", but nobody has ever actually called it that. It has a projected capacity of 18 million passengers. It handled 15.76 million passengers in 2025 and is expected to exceed its capacity in 2026. It handles about 160 departures per day.\nTerminals.\nThe airport has three terminal buildings, designated A, B, and C. Unlike most airports, all three terminals have customs and immigration services and can handle arriving and departure flights from foreign destinations. Terminal B is the newest (but smallest) terminal and is used by Aeromexico and its SkyTeam partners (particularly Delta Airlines). Terminal C is used exclusively Viva Aerobus. Terminal A is the original terminal building. It handles flights from all airlines other than Aeromexico and Viva (which also sometimes have departures going out of Terminal A). Terminal A has an odd arrangement with departure gates and restaurants in a midfield terminal building and checkin counters and baggage claim in a landslide building, which also houses several shops and some food outlets. The two Terminal A buildings are connected by an underground tunnel that passes below the apron.\nOnce you pass through security and enter the boarding areas, you cannot access the post-security areas of other terminals. The boarding areas of each terminal are not interconnected.\nFlights.\nAirlines serving Monterrey include:\nInternational destinations with direct service to and from Monterrey include:\nGet around.\nA free shuttle bus operates between terminals. It departs every 10 minutes throughout the day.\nWait.\nWaiting areas at all departure gates have comfortable seating, if you get there with ample time before boarding. There are limited outlets for charging electronic devices. VIP lounges for certain passengers can be found in all three terminals. Access to OMA Premium Lounges available to Priority pass, LoungeKey, or by purchasing a day pass for prices from US$29 to $46. Access to Centurion Lounges is for American Express Platinum and Centurion card members. Amenities in all lounges include comfortable seating, Wi-Fi internet connections, televisions, drinks, and snacks or buffet food.\nEat and drink.\nFast food options are widely available in all three terminals, which also have sit-down restaurants for better quality food in a more relaxed environment. There are also grab-and-go snacks. There are restaurants in both the pre-security public areas as well as the post-security boarding areas.\nAlcoholic drinks are served in all of the sit-down restaurants and in the VIP lounges. There are also a couple of standalone bars in both Terminal A and Terminal B. \nBuy.\nThe Monterrey International Airport has a variety of stores and shops in all terminals. \nDuty-free shops are in Terminal A (in the secured area for both Departures and for Arrivals). Terminal B has one duty-free shop in the Departures area. The shops are all operated by Dufry and offer the same selection of liquor, tobacco, and perfumes. \nThere are newsstands selling magazines, gum and candy in all terminals, but only Terminal A has any unique stores:\nBenavidez Pharmacies are available in all three terminals. The pharmacies are open from 06:00 - 22:00. In Terminals A and B, the pharmacies are in the public (landside). In Terminal C, the pharmacy is past security near the boarding gates.\nConnect.\nFree WiFi internet connections are available in all areas of Terminals A, B, and C. The service is provided by Sitwifi. To connect to the WiFi, open the Settings and select \"_Vinci Airports Wifi\". You will be prompted to agree to conditions and a browser will open. Click \"Navegar\" to begin browsing the web.\nCope.\nThe airport has several facilities to help families Travelling with children.\nSleep.\nA Hilton Garden Inn is on the airport premises between Terminal A and Terminal B. From either terminal there is a covered walkway to the hotel. There are also at least a half dozen hotels within a half mile of the terminals, all offering free airport shuttle vans on demand. These are listed in Monterrey/Eastern suburbs."}
{"id": "218392", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218392", "title": "Butwal", "text": "Butwal is in Western Tarai.\nUnderstand.\nButwal is a rapidly growing city in the Western Tarai plain and is considered the commercial hub and gateway to western Nepal. Aside from a developing presence of hotels and restaurants, the city has some unique attractions that include religious and historical military and political sites. Plus it is at the center of a number of nearby attractions like the birthplace of Gautama Buddha (Lumbini) and Ramgram Stupa for which Lumbini Province is known. \nGet in.\nButwal is at the crossroads of the Mahendra Highway (East-West Highway) and the Siddhartha Highway, such that motor transit from Kathmandu or India is possible. It also is only 22km from Siddharthanagar where Nepal's second international airport, Gautam Buddha International Airport, is located."}
{"id": "218410", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218410", "title": "Brussels-Midi/Zuid", "text": "Bruxelles-Midi (French) or Brussel-Zuid (Dutch) () is the hub of Belgium's train network in the south of Brussels (by Molenbeek). The station lacks a single common English term, and it's usually common to see both native names used interchangeably.\nUnderstand.\nStation layout.\nBrussels-Midi/Zuid can be roughly divided into three sections. \nServices.\nInternational.\nAll long-distance international train services to the UK, France, Luxembourg, Germany and the Netherlands, plus the night trains to Austria and Czechia, as listed in Rail travel in Belgium \u00a7 International, serve Midi/Zuid. Most will use platforms 1\u20136, but some, particularly to the Netherlands and sometimes Germany, can use the rest of the station.\nDomestic.\nMidi/Zuid, being the centre of the Belgian rail network, has trains to almost the entire country. Almost all of the greater Brussels region can be reached from this station, and the station has multiple departures an hour to other major Belgian cities including Antwerp, Bruges, Charleroi, Ghent and Li\u00e8ge.\nLocal transport.\nMetro.\nZuidstation / Gare du Midi (the metro for the station) sits in the middle of the southern ring portion of metro lines 2 and 6. This means that from most of the inner city, as well as Heysel / Heizel, it is only a single line away. From lines 1 and 5, change at Gare de l'Ouest from the west end of Brussels or Arts-Loi / Kunst-Wet from the east. From the centre, take either the S-Train from Brussels-Central or change for tram/premetro lines 4 and 10 at De Brouckere and head south.\nTram.\nTram lines 4 and 10 are the main premetro routes that run north-south passing the station. However, various other lines, including the 37, 48, 49, 50, 73, 81 and 96, either pass by or terminate at Gare du Midi / Zuidstation.\nGet around.\nThe station has a single main concourse which connects all of the platforms. All shops and restaurants are in this area. There's another small walkway close to Rue de l'Instruction/Rue de France, but this is merely a connecting passageway.\nSignage is usually clear and bilingual in French and Dutch, though it isn't hard for an English speaker to understand. However, English announcements are provided.\nUnlike stations in the Netherlands, there are no fare gates at the station; you do \"not\" need to scan your ticket to enter the station. However, there \"are\" fare gates to enter the metro station.\nWait.\nFor a station of its size, seating availability is rather limited. There are a few seats scattered around throughout the main concourse and a larger waiting area near the Eurostar/HSL concourse, but you are unlikely to find a seat when the station is busy.\nFor travellers using Eurostar Premier, there are two lounges. The main lounge, is at Rue de l'Instruction 158 (outside the station), for travellers using Eurostar Red (i.e. intra-Schengen destinations). For travellers heading to the UK via the Channel Tunnel, there is a separate lounge after you've passed immigration and security.\nLockers.\n can be found between the Eurostar/high-speed platforms and platforms 7\u20138. There are a range of different sizes (M, L, XL and XXL) and can be rented for up to 7 days.\nEat and drink.\nFood court.\nThere is a small food court and dining area beneath platforms 15\u201320. Variety isn't exceptional, and the dining area isn't the cleanest either, but it will suffice if you're after a quick bite. \nSleep.\nThe Pullman Brussels Midi (an upscale business-oriented hotel) is opposite the station building. For other accommodation in the area, see Brussels/Molenbeek and surroundings and Brussels/Pentagon.\nConnect.\nWi-Fi is provided for free by SNCB. Information on connecting to the network can be found on the SNCB-NMBS website.\nStay safe.\nPut simply, the area surrounding the station and the station itself are spots notorious for pickpockets, theft and antisocial behaviour. Keep your valuables and your luggage safe with you at all times.\nNearby.\nSee Brussels/Molenbeek and surroundings for info on the surrounding district."}
{"id": "218415", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218415", "title": "Sonpur (Odisha)", "text": "Sonpur or Sonepur (\u0b38\u0b4b\u0b28\u0b2a\u0b41\u0b30 \"Sonapura\"), also known as Subarnapur (\u0b38\u0b41\u0b2c\u0b30\u0b4d\u0b23\u0b2a\u0b41\u0b30 \"Subar\u1e47apura\"), is a temple town in western Odisha. Once a capital of a princely state, Sonpur is often called the twin of Varanasi for its cluster of architecturally significant temples, as well as the twin of Prayagraj for the confluence of the Mahanadi and Tel rivers."}
{"id": "218416", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218416", "title": "Rayagada", "text": "Rayagada is a town in South Odisha, close to Andhra Pradesh. It is one of Odisha's many hilly spots."}
{"id": "218417", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218417", "title": "Torrelavega", "text": "Torrelavega is a town in Cantabria near the north coast of Spain. It's 28 km southwest of the regional capital Santander, so it's a commuter town for the city and is mostly industrial, with a population in 2025 of 52,000. It's named for its 13th century founder Garci Lasso de la Vega I, whose descendants built the tower - \"torre\". That's long gone, and the most prominent towers today are those of the chemical works north side of town. \nUnderstand.\nSeveral towns along the north coast of Spain were centres for the coal, iron and steel industries from the 19th century. Torrelavega was among these, but specialised in making soda ash, sodium carbonate. You need this alkali to produce soap, glass and several textiles, and traditionally in Spain it was obtained by burning halophytic plants such as \"barrilla\", or elsewhere kelp. By 1800 demand outstripped supply and the Leblanc process was devised: it ramped up production but was costly and belched out acid pollution. A better process was invented in 1811 by Fresnel (best known for his lighthouse lenses) but forgotten until re-invented by Ernest Solvay in the 1860s. Carbon dioxide is bubbled through ammonia and brine, and sodium bicarbonate precipitates out. This is reacted with calcium oxide (quicklime) and the product heated to 200 \u00b0C: \"viola!\" The process has evolved over the last 150 years but the basic chemistry is unchanged. Solvay Quimica is the large industrial complex in the north suburbs that continues to make soda ash, which is taken to the port at Santander for worldwide distribution.\nGet in.\nTorrelavega is at the junction of A-8 coastal highway with A-67 from Valladolid to Santander. By road from Madrid is 455 km, follow A-6 north to Valladolid to join A-67.\nRenfe trains from Madrid Chamartin stop at Torrelavega on their way to Santander. The fastest take 4 hours via Segovia Guiomar, Valladolid and Palencia.\nA regional train also trundles through the mountains from Palencia, with multiple stops including Reinosa and Torrelavega. \n is the station for mainline trains, 2 km south of town centre. \nCercan\u00edas commuter train C2 runs from Santander every 30 min, taking 20 min. It continues west to Puente de Miguel and Cabez\u00f3n de la Sal. \n is the Cercan\u00edas railway station, north side of town. \nAltamira is a platform halt 500 m further west that you're unlikely to use. It's nowhere near the famous Altamira Cave.\nAlsa buses from Madrid stop at Torrelavega on their way to Santander. There are six a day, taking 5 hr 30 min via Madrid airport and Burgos. \nAlsa buses from Gij\u00f3n run four times a day via Oviedo, Llanes and San Vincente de la Barquera to Torrelavega and Santander; faster buses bypass Torrelavega. \nAlsa buses from Santander run every 15 min and take 40 min. From Santillana del Mar they run hourly and take 20 min.\nCantabria buses run four times a day from San Vicente de la Barquera via Comillas and Santillana del Mar to Torrelavega and Santander. \n is the bus station, central in town.\nGet around.\nThe town sprawls, but the main sights are in a compact walkable area.\nBuy.\nLots of small supermarkets. Lupa has three branches in town, open M-Sa 09:00-21:00.\nEat.\nA dozen places are ranged around Virgen Grande, including Villa de Santillana, La Patrona, Nolula, La Fonda de Clint, Casa Te\u00f3fila, Malquerida Tasca Nipona, Mundo Canalla, 2 Valdeses[dead link], Casa Mar\u00eda and Mes\u00f3n La Taberna.\nDrink.\nA dozen town centre bars, mostly serving tapas.\nCider is the main drink here and throughout the north coast of Spain.\nConnect.\nAs of June 2026, Torrelavega and its approach roads have 5G from all Spanish carriers."}
{"id": "218419", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218419", "title": "Capo di Ponte", "text": "Capo di Ponte is the cultural heart of in Val Camonica, a wide Alpine valley in Lombardy, northern Italy. This small, quiet town is one of Europe\u2019s most extraordinary prehistoric sites: it sits at the centre of the Val Camonica Rock Art, a vast collection of over 140,000 petroglyphs carved into sandstone over thousands of years. These carvings\u2014depicting hunters, warriors, animals, farming scenes, ritual symbols, and even early maps\u2014span from the Mesolithic period through the Iron Age and into Roman times.\nUnderstand.\nThe people of Val Camonica, known as the Camunni, left behind an unparalleled visual record of their world. Unlike painted cave art elsewhere in Europe, these figures were hammered and scratched into exposed rock surfaces, meaning they are best viewed in natural light\u2014often at certain angles where shadows bring the carvings to life.\nThe engravings show a transition from hunter-gatherer societies to more complex agricultural and warrior cultures. You\u2019ll see stylised deer, duelling figures, ploughing scenes, solar symbols, and mysterious geometric shapes. Some panels appear almost like proto-maps, especially at Bedolina, hinting at early spatial awareness and land organisation.\nGet in.\nCapo di Ponte lies about 100 km northeast of Brescia.\nBy train.\nRegional trains run up the valley from Brescia (line to Edolo).\nBy car.\nFollow the SS42 road through Val Camonica.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are in Milan (Linate, Malpensa) and Bergamo (Orio al Serio)."}
{"id": "218442", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218442", "title": "Ogimi", "text": "Ogimi (\u5927\u5b9c\u5473\u6751, \"\u014cgimi-son\") is a small village in the northern part of Okinawa Island, Japan, known as part of the Yanbaru region (Northern Okinawa) \u2014 a UNESCO World Natural Heritage site. The village sits between lush subtropical forest and the East China Sea, and is famous for its longevity: it has one of the highest concentrations of centenarians in the world.\nUnderstand.\nOgimi is part of the Yambaru (\u3084\u3093\u3070\u308b) wilderness area, which became a UNESCO World Natural Heritage site in 2021. The village is quiet and rural, far from the tourist crowds of southern Okinawa. Its population is small and the pace of life is slow \u2014 exactly why many visitors come here.\nThe area is known for:\nGet in.\nOgimi is about 80 km north of Naha, roughly 1.5\u20132 hours by car via National Route 58.\nGet around.\nA rental car is essentially required. Public buses are infrequent. The village roads are narrow and scenic.\nEat.\nDining options are limited in the village itself. The roadside station has a small caf\u00e9. Most visitors eat at their accommodation or drive to nearby Nago (approximately 30 minutes south).\nConnect.\nMobile coverage (au, SoftBank, Docomo) is generally available along the main roads. Some areas deep in the forest may have weak signal."}
