{"id": "218473", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218473", "title": "Dhanushkodi", "text": "Dhanushkodi (\u0ba4\u0ba9\u0bc1\u0bb7\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bcb\u0b9f\u0bbf \"Tanushk\u014d\u1e0di\") is an interesting ghost town in Tamil Nadu, at the southern tip of Pamban Island. This strip of land is about 1 km (0.62 mi) wide and 18 km (11 mi) long on the eastern end of Pamban Island.\nUnderstand.\nThe name \"Dhanushkodi\" (\u0927\u0928\u0941\u0937\u094d\u0915\u094b\u091f\u093f \"Dhanushko\u1e6di\") means the \"tip of a bow\" in Sanskrit, referencing the bow of Rama in the Ramayana.\nDhanushkodi was completely washed away by the 1964 cyclone after which the city was deserted. It seems to revive between the ruins: many wood bungalows are around the old harbour (fishermen) and others with handicrafts along the road. A primary school is also active. You can see the post office, railway station, church and rail tracks in ruins. On one side are the waters of the Indian Ocean and on the other is the Bay of Bengal. The two seas, otherwise known as Ratnakaran and Mahodadhi, with their confluence, are shaped like a bow while the strip of land resembles an arrow poised for release. Devout Hindus consider the Adi Sethu, or the arrowhead, a sacred place to perform religious rites. It is also a place worth visiting for its scenic beauty and bird life.\nGet in.\nThe only way to reach Dhanushkodi is by the fully paved road from Rameswaram to the tip of India towards the Adam's Bridge (Rama Setu). Buses #3 and #2a from Rameswaram bring you there for \u20b930. Both buses start a bit north of the temple east gate at the seashore.\nOn the trip, you see the sea on both sides: on one side the Bay of Bengal and on the other the Indian Ocean, the Bay of Bengal side is relatively calm and hence goes by the name (Penn Kadal, Lady Sea) and the Indian Ocean is rough with waves lashing and is called (Aaan Kadal, Male Sea). It's best to go in the evening or early morning to avoid the scorching sun, the bus starts at early as 6AM and till sunset 6PM. You might experience the travel with the former mini-truck instead of the bus."}
{"id": "218475", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218475", "title": "Ramanathapuram", "text": "Ramanathapuram (\u0b87\u0bb0\u0bbe\u0bae\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bb0\u0bae\u0bcd \"Ir\u0101man\u0101daburam\") or Ramnad, a town in southern Tamil Nadu, is the gateway to Rameswaram and Dhanushkodi."}
{"id": "218476", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218476", "title": "Ramanathapuram (district)", "text": "Ramanathapuram is a district of Tamil Nadu.\nUnderstand.\nRamanathapuram district combines pilgrimage, coastal landscapes, maritime history and marine biodiversity. The district is particularly known for Rameswaram, Dhanushkodi, the Ramanathaswamy Temple, Pamban Island and the Gulf of Mannar.\nThe eastern part of the district is dominated by the Palk Strait and Gulf of Mannar. The region contains numerous islands, shallow coastal waters, coral reefs and marine habitats.\nHistory.\nRamanathapuram has a long history associated with the Sethupathi rulers, maritime trade and pilgrimage to Rameswaram. The region's location between mainland India and Sri Lanka has given it considerable historical and cultural importance.\nRameswaram has been an important pilgrimage centre for centuries, attracting visitors from different parts of India.\nLanguage.\nTamil is the principal language spoken in the district. English is understood in tourist centres, hotels and larger commercial establishments. Malayalam may also be understood by some people working in tourism and trade.\nClimate.\nThe district is generally hot and dry for much of the year. Coastal areas can be humid and exposed to strong winds.\nVisitors should carry light clothing, sun protection and sufficient drinking water, particularly when travelling to exposed coastal areas such as Dhanushkodi.\nReligion and culture.\nRamanathapuram district is an important pilgrimage destination, particularly because of Rameswaram and the Ramanathaswamy Temple. The district also has a diverse cultural heritage, including Hindu, Muslim and Christian communities.\nKilakarai is particularly known for its historic Muslim maritime heritage.\nWhen to visit.\nThe cooler months from October to March are generally more comfortable for sightseeing.\nRameswaram is busiest during major Hindu festivals and pilgrimage seasons. Visitors planning to stay in Rameswaram during major festivals should arrange accommodation in advance.\nThe coastal areas may experience strong winds and occasional severe weather during periods of cyclonic activity. Visitors should check local weather conditions before travelling to exposed coastal destinations.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe district has no major commercial airport. The nearest airports are Madurai Airport and Tiruchirappalli International Airport. Madurai Airport is generally the more convenient option for travellers heading to Ramanathapuram or Rameswaram.\nFrom the airports, onward travel is possible by taxi, bus or train.\nBy train.\nRamanathapuram, Mandapam, Pamban and Rameswaram are connected to the Indian railway network.\nRameswaram railway station is the principal railway station for visitors travelling to Rameswaram. Regular trains connect the district with Madurai, Chennai and other major cities in Tamil Nadu.\nBy bus.\nGovernment and private buses connect the district with Madurai, Sivaganga, Paramakudi, Ramanathapuram, Rameswaram and other towns in Tamil Nadu.\nLocal and regional buses also operate between Rameswaram, Mandapam, Pamban and Dhanushkodi.\nBy car.\nRamanathapuram district is connected by road with Madurai and other major towns in Tamil Nadu.\nThe road journey to Rameswaram is particularly scenic around Pamban, as the route crosses towards Pamban Island.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nLocal and regional buses connect the main towns and villages. Buses are inexpensive but may be less frequent in remote coastal areas.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis are convenient for visiting several attractions in a single day, especially around Rameswaram, Dhanushkodi and the Gulf of Mannar coast.\nHiring a car with a driver can be useful for travellers visiting several destinations in the district.\nBy auto-rickshaw.\nAuto-rickshaws are available in Ramanathapuram, Rameswaram and other larger towns. They are useful for short distances.\nBy bicycle.\nCycling can be an enjoyable way to explore some parts of Rameswaram and the smaller coastal settlements, although visitors should be prepared for hot weather and strong coastal winds.\nDo.\nPilgrimage.\nRameswaram is one of the major Hindu pilgrimage destinations in India. Visitors can explore Ramanathaswamy Temple and the traditional pilgrimage sites around the town.\nExplore Dhanushkodi.\nTravel to Dhanushkodi to see the abandoned settlement, coastal landscape and historic ruins.\nVisit beaches.\nAriyaman Beach and other coastal areas around Rameswaram provide opportunities to relax by the sea.\nMarine activities.\nThe Gulf of Mannar region offers opportunities for marine-related tourism, subject to local regulations and environmental restrictions.\nPhotography.\nThe coastal landscapes around Pamban, Rameswaram and Dhanushkodi are particularly attractive for photography.\nBirdwatching.\nThe coastal wetlands, islands and shallow waters of the Gulf of Mannar attract numerous resident and migratory birds.\nExplore local culture.\nVisit the traditional streets, markets, fishing settlements and historic religious buildings of towns such as Kilakarai and Ramanathapuram.\nEat.\nRamanathapuram district has a mixture of Tamil, Chettinad-influenced, coastal and Muslim cuisines.\nLocal food.\nCommon foods include:\nSeafood is widely available in coastal towns, although availability varies by season.\nRameswaram has many vegetarian restaurants catering to pilgrims.\nDrink.\nTea and coffee are widely available in towns throughout the district.\nFresh fruit juices and tender coconut water are particularly suitable in the hot coastal climate.\nPackaged drinking water is widely available.\nVisitors should use clean and reliable sources of drinking water, particularly when travelling to remote coastal areas.\nStay safe.\nRamanathapuram district is generally suitable for tourists, but normal precautions should be followed.\nFor emergencies in India, dial **112**.\nCope.\nClimate.\nThe district has a hot tropical climate. Daytime temperatures can be high, particularly during the summer.\nCarry:\nThe coast can be windy, especially around Pamban and Dhanushkodi.\nMoney.\nIndian rupees (\u20b9) are used throughout the district.\nATMs are available in major towns, particularly Ramanathapuram and Rameswaram. Smaller villages may have limited banking facilities.\nDigital payments, including UPI, are increasingly accepted."}
