{"id": "218500", "revid": "2258681", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218500", "title": "Osaka/Hirano", "text": "HIrano (\u5e73\u91ce) is a city district of Osaka.\nUnderstand.\nOn the southeast side of Osaka, Hirano used to be independent from Osaka. It was a moated, commercial city, nationally known as Cotton City. Today, the Hirano Old City (\u5e73\u91ce\u65e7\u5e02\u8857), also called as Hirano-g\u014d (\u5e73\u91ce\u90f7) area is known as one of the oldest urban areas in Osaka, and preserved old townscape. The city name of Hirano originates from Sakanoe no Hironomaro (\u5742\u4e0a\u5e83\u91ce\u9ebb\u5442), a nobleman who developed the area.\nNow Hirano is known as the festival city. Hirano-g\u014d Summer Festival (\u5e73\u91ce\u90f7\u590f\u307e\u3064\u308a) which was begun over 1,300 years ago, is the largest \"danjiri\" festival in Osaka, so it attracts as many as 300,000 visitors from across Osaka.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nTanimachi Line and Yamatoji Line cross through this district. Osaka Metro Hirano Station and JR Hirano Station are completely separate stations about 1.5 kilometers apart. \nFrom Kansai International Airport, please take a JR Haruka or Kansai Airport Rapid service to Tennoji, and change to either of the two lines via Hirano. The routes from Shin-Osaka Station are shown below.\nMetro lines.\nThe total journey takes approximately 35 to 40 minutes and costs \u00a5340.\nJR Lines.\nFrom Shin-Osaka Station, two primary routes are available and the total travel time ranges from 35 to 45 minutes, and the standard fare is \u00a5320."}
{"id": "218502", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218502", "title": "Athens Eleftherios Venizelos International Airport", "text": "Athens Eleftherios Venizelos International Airport () is located approximately 27 km east of Athens city centre, near the town of Spata.\nUnderstand.\nOpened in 2001 as part of the infrastructure improvements in preparation for the Olympics. It is now one of the more attractive and efficient major European airports, though some old Athenian hands say they miss the messy atmosphere of the old Hellenikon.\nFlights.\nThe airport is designed to handle approximately 16 million passengers and is served by major European airlines, including Lufthansa, Air France, Austrian Airlines, and Swiss.\nThe airport is the hub of the Greek airlines Olympic Air and Aegean Airlines.\nTerminal.\nThe airport has a single terminal, which on the landside consists of two connected check-in halls. Access to the security checkpoints is located between the two check-in areas and is divided into an area on the left, used exclusively for Schengen flights, and an area on the right, used only for non-Schengen departures. In between are two boarding gates that can be used for both European and non-European flights and are accessible via a separate entrance.\nDepartures.\nThe departures hall is located to the left of the main entrance and has approximately 150 counters. There are electronic check-in options and staff are usually available.\nArrival.\nThe arrivals area is located on the level below the check-in desks.\nGround transportation.\nBy car.\nThe A6 motorway (toll) leads from Athens in an arc around a hill to the airport, the last leg of the journey from Koropi to the airport is the A62.\nParking.\nThere are short-term parking spaces directly at the terminal entrance. The first 20 minutes are free, after which you'll pay \u20ac3.80 for the first hour. A little off the beaten path are long-term parking lots with the same rates as the first day: a week costs \u20ac50.00, and each additional day costs \u20ac5.00 (September 2011).\nCar rentals.\nAll international and local suppliers have their counters in the arrivals area. There are no shuttle buses to the parking lots.\nThe vehicle is returned to a small parking lot at the end of the access road. In any case, use the first entrance, even if your \"own\" company is not listed there. This is the only way to get there, and another attempt is only possible after a very large round.\nBy bus.\nThere are several bus lines to the airport, including the X95 from Syntagma Square, the X93 from Kifissos, the X96 from Piraeus, and the X97 from Dafni Metro Station. There are also \"KTEL\" intercity buses.\nSee.\nThere is a small museum on the top floor with interesting history information about Athens, and a space for temporary exhibits.\nWait.\nLounge.\nThe airport has a total of 7 lounges, 3 of which are located in the non-Schengen arrivals area behind passport control.\nEat and drink.\nThere are numerous fast food chains and a restaurant on the ground and first floors. Most are open 24 hours a day.\nBuy.\nThe vast majority of shops are located after security in the Schengen departures area and can only be accessed with a valid boarding pass.\nDuty-free shops are available, but they aren't very attractive to passengers from EU countries. Parallel to the highway, you can walk from the terminal to the IKEA furniture store towards the city (about 1-1.5 km), but this is also possible by bus for the full price of \u20ac3.50 (Emporiko Kentro Aerodromiou stop).\nConnect.\nFree Wi-Fi hotspots are located at various points in the departure lounge. You can charge your mobile phone (including those from other manufacturers) for free at the Samsung Point."}
{"id": "218508", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218508", "title": "San Vicente de la Barquera", "text": "San Vicente de la Barquera is a town in Cantabria on the north coast of Spain. With a population of 4000 in 2025, it's a beach resort and has an old centre, but its main attraction is the coastlands of Oyambre nature park.\nGet in.\nSan Vicente is just off coastal motorway A-8. Driving from Madrid, follow A-6 north to Valladolid then A-67 towards Santander to join A-8 at Torrelavega.\nAlsa buses from Santander run four times a day via Torrelavega, taking 65 min, and continue to Llanes, Oviedo and Gij\u00f3n. \nCantabria buses also run four times a day from Santander, via Torrelavega, Santillana del Mar and Comillas.\n is the bus station, central in town.\nSan Vicente railway station was 2 km south of town at La Acebosa, but it's disused. This was on the metre-gauge FEVE line, but taken over by RENFE who neglected it. Standard-gauge Cercan\u00edas trains from Santander run via Torrelavega only as far west as Cabez\u00f3n de la Sal.\nGet around.\nTown is small and walkable, and the closest beaches are 2 km east.\nHalf a dozen taxi firms ply in town.\n Golf Shangri-La is in the hills 10 km south. \nBuy.\nLupa is the main supermarket, on Padre \u00c1ngel in Old Town, open M-Sa 09:00-21:30, Su 09:00-15:00.\nEat.\nOver a dozen cafes and restaurants line the main street through Old Town, serving trad fare.\nDrink.\nBars serve \"tapas\". \nCantabria produces a small amount of wine, but the main drink is cider.\nConnect.\nAs of June 2026, San Vicente and its approach roads have 4G from Digi and Movistar, and 5G from MasOrange and Vodaphone."}
{"id": "218515", "revid": "413428", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218515", "title": "Dhubri", "text": "Dhubri (Assamese: \u09a7\u09c1\u09ac\u09c1\u09f0\u09c0 \"Dhubur\u012b\", Bengali: \u09a7\u09c1\u09ac\u09a1\u09bc\u09c0 \"Dhub\u1e5b\u012b\") is a commercial centre in Lower Assam."}
{"id": "218520", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218520", "title": "Northern Hooghly", "text": "Northern Hooghly (\u0989\u09a4\u09cd\u09a4\u09b0 \u09b9\u09c1\u0997\u09b2\u09bf \"Uttor Hugli\"), officially Chinsurah Sadar subdivision, covers the northern part of the Hooghly district of West Bengal."}
{"id": "218521", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218521", "title": "Warsaw Chopin Airport", "text": "Warsaw Chopin Airport () is 8 km south of Warsaw city centre in the west side of the city.\nUnderstand.\nWith over 24 million passengers by 2025, it's the largest airport in Poland.\nFlights.\nThis was upgraded in 2015 and now has a single Terminal A. It has both budget and full-service flights to most major European cities. Intercontinental destinations are New York JFK and Newark, Chicago O'Hare, Toronto, Seoul, Beijing, Tel Aviv and the Gulf states. Domestic flights are reducing as the trains speed up, but include Pozna\u0144, Krak\u00f3w, Katowice, Rzesz\u00f3w, Gda\u0144sk and Wroc\u0142aw.\nGround transportation.\nBy train.\n is next to Arrivals. \"Szybka Kolej Miejska\" (\"SKM\") train Line S2 runs downtown every 30 min, taking 25 min via S\u0142u\u017cewiec, Zachodnia (West) station, \u015ar\u00f3dmie\u015bcie station (for Centralna bus and railway stations), Stadion, Wschodnia (East) station and Sulej\u00f3wek. It runs to the city 06:00-23:30 and outward 05:00-23:30. Line S3 runs downtown hourly through Warszawa Zachodnia (West), Warszawa Centralna and Warszawa Wschodnia (East) to Legionowo Piaski / Radzymin.\nRegular Warsaw transport tickets issued by ZTM (the local transport operator which runs city buses, trams and the metro) are valid on SKM train services between the airport and Warsaw. The airport is in fare zone 1 so a single adult ride is 3.40 z\u0142. If you need a bus at the other end, get a one-transfer ticket (valid 75 min) for 4.40 z\u0142. Buy tickets from the machines near the platforms - you shouldn't need to validate them as they're stamped on issue.\nKoleje Mazowieckie (Mazovian Railways or KML, the provincial local train operator) also operates direct connections (RL) between Modlin, Warszawa Centralna and Chopin Airport hourly. Single transfer tickets and timed tickets of ZTM are also accepted on RL airport trains operated by KM on the section Warszawa P\u0142udy \u2013 Warszawa Lotnisko Chopina.\nBy bus.\nFour daytime bus lines and one night bus ply to and from the airport. Single-fare tickets for the bus cost 4.40 z\u0142 (as of May 2023) from any kiosk. There is a ticket machine (which accepts coins, notes and credit cards) near the bus stop. You need to validate the ticket when you board the bus. There are also ticket machines in most of the buses, please note that they only accept cards. Drivers do not sell tickets anymore. \n - Bus 175 runs from Plac Pi\u0142sudskiego near Old Town via Centralna station and Metro Centrum, Nowy \u015awiat and the University. It runs 04:30-23:00 every 20 min and takes 30-45 min.\n - Bus 188 runs from Goc\u0142awek Wschodni in Praga Po\u0142udnie, via \u015ar\u00f3dmie\u015bcie and Politechnika metro station. It runs 05:00-23:00. \n - Bus 148 runs from Rondo Wiatraczna in Praga Po\u0142udnie, east side of the city, via Ursyn\u00f3w, Mokot\u00f3w and Imielin metro station.\n - Bus 331 only runs in peak hours from Wilanowska metro station in Mokot\u00f3w, where many inter-city buses stop.\n - Night Bus N32 runs from Centralna bus station, 23:00-05:00. \nBy taxi.\nThe airport recommends Ele Taxi +48 22 811 11 11; a reasonable fare from city centre might be 40 z\u0142. There's a taxi kiosk within Arrivals. Other operators can only pick you up from outside Departures. Have nothing to do with the touts and sharks lurking outside Arrivals: see Warsaw#Taxis for more info.\nGet around.\nAt the airport we can easily get around on foot.\nEat and drink.\nThe airport has several catering facilities, including bars, restaurants, and fast food.\nBuy.\nThe airport has several shops and some duty-free shops.\nSleep.\nThe hotels closest to the airport are:"}
{"id": "218522", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218522", "title": "Southern Hooghly", "text": "Southern Hooghly (\u09a6\u0995\u09cd\u09b7\u09bf\u09a3 \u09b9\u09c1\u0997\u09b2\u09bf \"Dokhyi\u1e47 Hugli\") covers the southern part of the Hooghly district of West Bengal, corresponding to the district's Chandannagar and Serampore subdivisions."}
{"id": "218529", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218529", "title": "Western North 24 Parganas", "text": "Western North 24 Parganas covers the western part of the North 24 Parganas district of West Bengal, corresponding to much of the district's Barrackpore subdivision. The southern parts of Barrackpore subdivision are covered under the Northern Suburbs of Kolkata."}
{"id": "218530", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218530", "title": "Central North 24 Parganas", "text": "Central North 24 Parganas covers the central part of the North 24 Parganas district of West Bengal, corresponding to Barasat Sadar subdivision."}
{"id": "218531", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218531", "title": "Eastern North 24 Parganas", "text": "Eastern North 24 Parganas covers the eastern part of the North 24 Parganas district of West Bengal, along the Ichhamati River. It covers Bangaon and Basirhat subdivisions."}
{"id": "218544", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218544", "title": "Rudraprayag", "text": "Rudraprayag (\u0930\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0930\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u093e\u0917 \"Rudrapray\u0101g\") is the town in upper Garhwal where the Alaknanda and Mandakini rivers meet. It's also home to the Rudranath Temple, one of the holy \"Panch Ked\u0101r\" sites."}
{"id": "218588", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218588", "title": "Bandipur", "text": "There is more than one place called Bandipur:"}
{"id": "218592", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218592", "title": "Granvin", "text": "Granvin is a former municipality in Hardanger. Granvin stretches along both sides of the Granvin Fjord (a small arm off of the main Hardangerfjorden) and the valley extending inland from the end of the fjord, in the region of Hardanger\nUnderstand.\nThe area is now part of Voss Municipality in the traditional district of Hardanger in Vestland county. In 2019, it was home to fewer than 1,000 people.\nThe large lake Granvinsvatnet sits in the centre of the valley, just north of the village of Eide. On both sides of the fjord and valley, there are high mountains. Espeland Falls are found in the Espelandsdalen valley near the border with Ulvik Municipality. \nGranvin is a major setting in Moe Cidaly's short story \"Summer Episode\".\nGet in.\nNational Road 13 enters Granvin via the Tunsberg Tunnel, which runs through the high mountains to the northwest. The highway then runs through Granvin before entering the Vallavik Tunnel, which goes through the high mountains to the southeast. The Vallavik Tunnel connects with the Hardanger Bridge in Ulvik Municipality, which crosses the Hardangerfjorden.\nA car ferry service connects Kvanndal (in southwest Granvin) with the villages of Utne and Kinsarvik (in Ullensvang Municipality) on the south side of the Hardangerfjord."}
{"id": "218616", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218616", "title": "Koml\u00f3ska", "text": "Koml\u00f3ska (Rusyn: \"\u041a\u043e\u043c\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0448\u043a\u0430\") is a village in Borsod-Aba\u00faj-Zempl\u00e9n County in northeastern Hungary, close to the border with Slovakia, 70 km from Miskolc.\nUnderstand.\nThe name Koml\u00f3ska was first mentioned in historical documents in 1396 as \"Koml\u00f3s\" and later in 1397 as \"Koml\u00f3stelek\".\nRusyn settlers from modern-day Slovakia arrived in the village in the 1720s. To this day, the village has preserved its unique Rusyn traditions and Greek Catholic faith.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nThere are direct trains from Budapest to the village of V\u00e1mos\u00fajfalu, from where you can take a bus to Koml\u00f3ska. The name of the train station in V\u00e1mos\u00fajfalu is \"Olaszliszka-Tolcsva\". The entire trip from Budapest takes about 3 and a half hours and costs around.\nBy bus.\nBus routes 3891 and 3884 run every 1\u20132 hours from the V\u00e1mos\u00fajfalu Train Station to Koml\u00f3ska. The earliest departure is at 4:50 AM, and the latest is at 5:35 PM. The journey takes about 20 minutes and costs. There are two bus stops in Koml\u00f3ska: \"Koml\u00f3ska, Erd\u00e9szh\u00e1z\" at the S\u00f3lyomv\u00e1r-Lak hotel and \"Koml\u00f3ska, K\u00f6zs\u00e9gi k\u00fat\" in the center of the village, in front of the Municipal Office.\nConnect.\nKoml\u00f3ska's postal code is H-3937 and its telephone area code is 47."}
{"id": "218624", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218624", "title": "Woolgoolga", "text": "Woolgoolga (often called \"Woopi\") is a relaxed coastal town on the Mid-North Coast of New South Wales. Known for its long sandy beaches, strong Sikh heritage, and laid-back holiday vibe, it\u2019s a popular stop along the Pacific Highway and a gateway to quieter coastal stretches.\nGet in.\nWoolgoolga sits just off the Pacific Highway about 25 km north of Coffs Harbour. It and is easily reached by car from Coffs Harbour (around 20 minutes). The nearest major station is at Coffs Harbour and local bus services operate north to Woolgoolga, though services can be infrequent. The nearest airport is Coffs Harbour Airport, with regular flights from Sydney and Brisbane."}
{"id": "218625", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218625", "title": "Siderno", "text": "Siderno is a lively seaside town on the Ionian coast of Calabria, known for its long beachfront promenade, summer atmosphere, and proximity to some of southern Italy\u2019s most atmospheric hill towns. Divided between the modern coastal settlement and the historic hillside village above, it offers both relaxed beach life and easy access to centuries of history.\nGet in.\nSiderno is served by the Ionian railway line, with regular regional trains connecting it to Reggio Calabria and Catanzaro. By car, it lies along the SS106 coastal highway. The nearest major airport is Reggio Calabria Airport, with additional options at Lamezia Terme Airport."}
{"id": "218626", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218626", "title": "Gerace", "text": "Gerace is a striking medieval hill town in the region of Calabria, perched on a sandstone plateau above the Ionian coast. Known for its remarkably preserved historic centre, Norman-era cathedral, and sweeping views over the Locride plain and sea, Gerace is one of southern Italy\u2019s most atmospheric small towns.\nGet in.\nBy train, the closest station is Locri on the Ionian coast line between Reggio Calabria and Catanzaro. Regional trains from Reggio Calabria and other coastal towns stop here frequently.\nGerace is about 10 km from Locri and there is a very limited local bus services.Schedules can be infrequent and may be limited on weekends and public holidays, try Autolinee Federico. You may need to take a taxi\nBy car, Gerace is reached via the SS106 coastal highway and then a steep inland road climbing up from Locri.\nSee.\nGerace\u2019s old town is a maze of stone-paved lanes, arches, and stairways, largely unchanged for centuries. Its elevated position offers constant views over the surrounding countryside and sea."}
{"id": "218627", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218627", "title": "Locri", "text": "Locri is a small coastal town on the Ionian Sea in the region of Calabria. Locri is best known for its proximity to the ancient Greek city of Locri Epizephyrii and its long archaeological heritage. Today it is a quiet, functional town serving as a gateway to nearby hill villages like Gerace, while also offering beaches, basic amenities, and a relaxed southern Italian atmosphere.\nGet in.\nLocri lies on the SS106 Ionian coastal highway and is the main rail stop for the surrounding area on the Reggio Calabria\u2013Catanzaro line. Regional trains connect it south to Reggio Calabria and north toward Catanzaro."}
{"id": "218629", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218629", "title": "Digboi", "text": "Digboi (Assamese: \u09a6\u09bf\u0997\u09cd\u09ac\u0987), a town in Upper Assam, is where Asia's first oil well was drilled.\nUnderstand.\nThe origin of the name \"Digboi\" (Assamese: \"DEEG-boy\") is unclear. As the legend goes, the Assam Railways and Trading Company (AR&amp;TC) commissioned Italian engineers to build a railway line from Dibrugarh to Margherita (headquarters of AR&amp;TC) in 1867. As they accidentally discovered oil around 10 mi from Margherita, Canadian engineer W. L. Lake shouted \"Dig boy, dig\", which got corrupted to \"Digboi\" in Assamese."}
{"id": "218630", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218630", "title": "Goalpara", "text": "Goalpara (Assamese: \u0997\u09cb\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09b2\u09aa\u09be\u09f0\u09be \"G\u014dy\u0101lp\u0101r\u0101\", Bengali: \u0997\u09cb\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09b2\u09aa\u09be\u09a1\u09bc\u09be \"G\u014dy\u0101lp\u0101\u1e5b\u0101\") is a town in Lower Assam, along the Brahmaputra."}
{"id": "218631", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218631", "title": "Diphu", "text": "Diphu is a tribal town on the hills of of Assam."}
{"id": "218632", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218632", "title": "Maibang", "text": "Maibang or Maibong (Bengali and Assamese: \u09ae\u09be\u0987\u09ac\u0982 \"M\u0101ibong\"), a town on the hills of Assam, is an ancient capital of the Dimasa kingdom. The Stone House near the river is the only intact relic of the kingdom there, though other artifacts have been discovered during construction and are on display."}
{"id": "218635", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218635", "title": "Badarpur", "text": "Badarpur (\u09ac\u09a6\u09b0\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0 \"Bodorpur\") is a town in the Barak Valley region of Assam."}
{"id": "218637", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218637", "title": "Sribhumi", "text": "Sribhumi (\u09b6\u09cd\u09b0\u09c0\u09ad\u09c2\u09ae\u09bf \"Shr\u012bbh\u016bmi\"), formerly Karimganj (\u0995\u09b0\u09bf\u09ae\u0997\u099e\u09cd\u099c \"Korimgonj\"), is a border town in the Barak Valley region of Assam.\nUnderstand.\nSribhumi is on the Kushiyara River (\u0995\u09c1\u09b6\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09b0\u09be \"Kushiy\u0101r\u0101\"), opposite Zakiganj (\u099c\u0995\u09bf\u0997\u099e\u09cd\u099c \"Jokigonj\") in Bangladesh."}
{"id": "218646", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218646", "title": "Amfissa", "text": "Amfissa is in Central Greece.\nUnderstand.\nKnown in antiquity as Amphissa, the city was an important regional centre of the Ozolian Locrians. Its strategic position near Delphi ensured continued relevance through classical and Roman times. In the Middle Ages, it became a fortified settlement under Frankish and later Ottoman rule, leaving behind a castle and traces of its defensive past.\nToday, Amfissa is a working Greek town surrounded by one of the oldest cultivated olive landscapes in the Mediterranean\u2014an atmospheric setting that defines the region."}
{"id": "218660", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218660", "title": "Station articles", "text": "Wikivoyage has articles for some of the world's largest and most-complicated transport stations, including bus and train stations. They are designed to help you navigate safely and comfortably around them, and provide essential knowledge such as information on eating and sleeping options in the station, and onward travel advice. This article lists our current station articles by continent."}
{"id": "218664", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218664", "title": "Camino del Mayab", "text": "Camino del Mayab is a 110-km (68 mile) hiking and biking trail through the heart of historic Maya country in the jungles of Yucat\u00e1n. The itinerary follows ancient Maya roadways, called \"sacbeob\" and passes near traditional Maya villages, deep cenotes will clear, cold water where you can swim and get a break from the jungle heat, as well as historic henequen haciendas that recall Mexico's colonial past. It is intended as a \"slow travel\" itinerary for travelers to experience Maya traditions and the natural wonders of the Yucatan.\nUnderstand.\nThe trail first opened in 2020 as an 86-km route (53 miles) but has expanded over the years, including an extension that takes it all the way into Merida (in the past, hikers started in a nature reserve outside the city). As of 2026, it is a 110-km route, mostly on historic Maya sacbeob, but with sections that are rural dirt backroads. Although surface roughness varies by section, the entire route is flat with no hills, so the biggest challenge is potentially high temperatures and very high humidity. \nIt is a tropical climate with hot temperatures all year-round. Even in winter months, day time highs can reach 40\u00b0C. The hottest month is May with average high temperatures of 43\u00b0C. Very high humidity is normal and rains particularly common in the summer months (June to October). Most days in the summer will experience a period of thunderstorms with heavy rain, but rarely will the rains persist.\nThe best time of year to hike or bike the Camino del Mayab is the winter period, which is referred to as the \"dry season\" in the Yucatan. Temperatures are usually a few degrees cooler in the winter, but cold temperatures should not be expected.\nThe trail is inland so does not experience coastal breezes, but most of the route passes through dense forest with shade to provide some relief from the hot sun.\nInformation.\nCosts vary depending on length of your trip and what services you require. The most basic passport to the Camino costs about M$1000 (US$50). Most travelers will opt for guide services and additional support services. A 5-day \"all in\" package can cost up to M$15,000 (about US$800) and is the best deal for travelers seeking an authentic, meaningful experience with few logistical worries. The money supports the Maya communities.\nThe camino is managed by a Maya community collective. They operate an information office in Merida and can provide guides (recommended), arrange overnight stays (not always camping), transport luggage between overnight stops, and arrange meal stops in Maya villages. They can also arrange rental mountain bikes with fat tires for visitors who would rather ride than walk.\nOffice: Calle 64A #530 Colonia Centro, M\u00e9rida\nWebsite: Camino del Mayab\nPrepare.\nSome hikers choose not to use the services of the Camino del Mayab community. Not doing so will make for a much more grueling experience because you'll have to pack camping supplies, food and large quantities of water and walking up to 25 km per day with 100 kg of gear on your back in a climate where 95% humidity and 95\u00b0F temperatures (35\u00b0C) is a recipe for trouble unless you are very fit and have trained for difficult conditions.\nA traveler on one of the guided treks will carry only a small daypack because the heavy supplies are transported each day by van to the next campsite and local indigenous communities will supply meals along the way, avoiding the need to carry extensive supplies.\nHikers can prepare themselves by starting a heat-acclimatization and cardio regimen several weeks before the hike. Pack lightweight, quick-drying long clothes, at least 4 liters of water daily, a LifeStraw or similar filtration device can purify shaky water sources (like water from a cenote), and an old fashioned printed map would be wide as GPS navigation can be uncertain when you get away from urban areas with good cell coverage. Hikers using the guide services should follow the guide's advice about what to carry (but should still prepare themselves for the prospect of sweating profusely each day).\nGet in.\nThe nearest commercial airport is Merida International Airport (MID). \"See M\u00e9rida for more info about transportation options\".\nWalk.\nMost days begin with walking a section of the trail. Sections average about 20 km. Hiking begins early in the morning before the sun gets too hot and will typically include activities that involve natural explorations like a bird-watching walk or swimming in a cenote, or sometimes cultural immersion, like learning to cook typical Mayan dishes under the practiced eye of a village matron. \nThe route is intended to culminate in a visit to Mayapan, but due to an ongoing legal battle, the site is temporarily closed and an alternative nearby endpoint is a Mayan ceremony in an underground cenote.\nStay safe.\nAdequate hydration will be critical in Hot weather, so prepare to carry several litres of water. and avoid getting dehydrated.\nYou will be in the shade of jungle foliage during much of the day, but there will inevitably be places where you are exposed to direct sun. Wear hats or sunblock to protect yourself from the sun. "}
{"id": "218686", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218686", "title": "Elassona", "text": "Elassona is is a small town in Larissa in northern Thessaly, at the foothills of the southern slopes of Mount Olympus. It acts as a quiet regional centre for surrounding farming and pastoral communities, and is best known as a gateway to the monasteries and hiking routes of the Olympus range. \nUnderstand.\nElassona has a long history, with ancient settlement in the wider region and a Byzantine and Ottoman past reflected in scattered churches, monasteries, and stone bridges.\nToday it is a relaxed provincial town with agriculture, livestock farming, and small-scale trade forming the backbone of the local economy. It is particularly associated with sheep and goat herding, and local dairy production\u2014especially feta cheese\u2014remains an important tradition.\nGet in.\nElassona is accessible by road from:\nThere is no railway station in Elassona; the nearest major rail hub is Larissa, with bus connections onward. Regional buses connect Elassona with nearby towns and villages, though services are not frequent.\nSee.\nThe modern town is compact and easy to walk. Key sights include small squares, local churches, and traditional neighbourhoods. While not a major monument town, Elassona offers a sense of everyday Thessalian life framed by impressive mountain scenery."}
{"id": "218689", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218689", "title": "Feres", "text": "Feres is in Western Thrace. It has the most significant Byzantine monument in Thrace: the Monastery of Panagia Kosmosoteira.\nUnderstand.\nHistorically, the area formed part of a strategic frontier zone between Byzantium and later Ottoman territories. Today it remains lightly visited, with agriculture\u2014especially crops and livestock\u2014forming the backbone of the local economy.\nGet in.\nFeres is located on the main road between Alexandroupolis (about 25 km west) and the Turkish border crossing at Kipi.\nBy car.\nFeres is easily reached via the Egnatia Odos and regional roads.\nBy bus.\nRegular services connect Alexandroupolis with Feres and border villages.\nBy plane.\nThe near airport is Alexandroupolis International Airport."}
{"id": "218690", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218690", "title": "Old Malda", "text": "Old Malda (\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0\u09be\u09a4\u09a8 \u09ae\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09b9 \"Pur\u0101ton M\u0101ldoho\") is a small heritage town near Malda in North Bengal, at the confluence of the Mahananda and Kalindi rivers. Most of the town is developed along parallel streets lined up by old homes.\nUnderstand.\nOld Malda was originally called simply \"Malda\" (\"M\u0101ldoho\" or \"M\u0101ld\u0101\"). It's uncertain why the town was called \"Malda\" in the first place. Some locals say that it is named after the grave of Sufi saint \"M\u0101lod P\u012br\". Other claim that it is named after the Mal tribe. It is also not known when Malda was established.\nWhatever the origin of the town, Old Malda, like many towns in medieval Bengal, was developed due to river trade. The town was also Muslim-majority, as it is evident with the presence of multiple Islamic graves beneath the homes. But somehow, the town eventually became Hindu-majority. Due to the establishment of English Bazar by the 17th century, Old Malda declined significantly. Despite this, the Old Malda Municipality was established in 1869, one of the oldest in West Bengal."}
{"id": "218695", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218695", "title": "Geraki Laconias", "text": "Geraki Laconias is a small historic village in Laconia in the Peloponnese, best known for its atmospheric Byzantine ruins and castle. Set on the slopes of Mount Parnon, it offers a quiet, off-the-beaten-path experience combining history, views, and traditional village life.\nUnderstand.\nGeraki was an important medieval settlement, flourishing under Frankish and later Byzantine control. Its hilltop castle and the scattered remains of churches and houses from the Byzantine town create one of the most evocative historic landscapes in Laconia\u2014often compared, on a smaller scale, to Mystras. Today, the modern village sits below the ruins, surrounded by olive groves and farmland, and retains a peaceful, rural character.\nGet in.\nGeraki lies inland, roughly midway between Sparta and the eastern coast of Laconia."}
{"id": "218696", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218696", "title": "Katni", "text": "Katni is a city in Eastern Madhya Pradesh.\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport is in Jabalpur."}
{"id": "218697", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218697", "title": "Aparan", "text": "Aparan (Armenian: \u0531\u057a\u0561\u0580\u0561\u0576) is is a town at the eastern slopes of Mount Aragats and the northern slopes of Mount Ara, in the Aragatsotn Province of Central Armenia. \nUnderstand.\nIn 2022, the population of the town was 5,800.\nAparan has an Alpine climate in general with the influence of cold semi-arid climate. The town is characterized with snowy winters and mild humid summers. The average temperature is around \u22128 \u00b0C in winter and 17 \u00b0C in summer. \nGet in.\nAparan is about 50 km northwest of the capital Yerevan.\nAparan is located on the M-3 Motorway that connects the Armenian capital Yerevan with the Georgian capital Tbilisi, passing through Aparan on the way to Lori Province. A network of regional roads connects the town with the surrounding villages, as well as the provinces of Shirak and Armavir.\nSee.\nAparan has a palace of culture, a public library, a music school, and a school of art run by the municipality.\nThere are many places of cultural interest in the town:"}
{"id": "218704", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218704", "title": "Andritsaina", "text": "Andritsaina is a picturesque mountain village in western Peloponnese, perched on the slopes above the Alfeios valley in Elis. Andritsaina is known for its stone-built houses, shaded square, and historic library, which make it a convenient base for exploring some of the region\u2019s lesser-visited but highly rewarding archaeological sites and landscapes.\nUnderstand.\nAndritsaina prospered in the Ottoman and early modern periods, leaving behind elegant mansions and a strong intellectual tradition. The highlight is the renowned Nikolaos Papazoglou Library, one of Greece\u2019s most important provincial collections, reflecting the town\u2019s past as a centre of learning.\nGet in.\nAndritsaina is best reached by car. It lies 1.5\u20132 hours from Kalamata or Pyrgos, via winding but scenic mountain roads."}
{"id": "218707", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218707", "title": "Tambaram", "text": "Tambaram (\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb0\u0bae\u0bcd \"T\u0101mbaram\") is in Tamil Nadu.\nGet in.\nBy place.\nTambaram is adjacent to Chennai Airport ()."}
{"id": "218709", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218709", "title": "Mangadu", "text": "Mangadu is in Tamil Nadu."}
{"id": "218712", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218712", "title": "S\u0142awk\u00f3w", "text": "S\u0142awk\u00f3w is a small historic town in Silesian Voivodeship, southern Poland, in the eastern part of the Zag\u0142\u0119bie D\u0105browskie region. It lies on national road 94, between D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Olkusz and Katowice, near the Bia\u0142a Przemsza river.\nS\u0142awk\u00f3w has a medieval street layout, a compact market square, the wooden Austeria inn, remains of a castle of the bishops of Krak\u00f3w, historic religious buildings, and walking areas in the Bia\u0142a Przemsza valley. It is a good half-day or one-day trip from Katowice, Sosnowiec, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, B\u0119dzin or Olkusz.\nUnderstand.\nS\u0142awk\u00f3w is one of the older urban centres in this part of southern Poland. Its development was connected with the medieval trade route between Krak\u00f3w and Wroc\u0142aw, mining of lead and silver ores, and its long association with the bishops of Krak\u00f3w. The town still has a small-town character, with most of the main sights close to the market square.\nAllow 2\u20134 hours for the market square, Austeria, parish church, castle ruins and the Bia\u0142a Przemsza valley park. A longer visit makes sense if you want to walk along the river, cycle through the surrounding forests, or visit the owl education centre.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nS\u0142awk\u00f3w railway station is on Kolejowa Street, about 1.5 km west of the market square. Regional trains connect the town with Katowice, Sosnowiec, Bukowno, Wolbrom, S\u0119dzisz\u00f3w, Koz\u0142\u00f3w and Kielce. Check current departures before travelling, as intervals outside peak hours can be long.\nUseful timetables:\nFrom the station to the market square, walk east along Kolejowa Street and then through the town centre.\nBy bus.\nLocal and metropolitan public transport is organised by Transport GZM. The most useful stops for visitors are:\nBuses connect S\u0142awk\u00f3w with D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Strzemieszyce, B\u0119dzin and local districts. Always check the current timetable for the exact stop, as service frequency varies.\nBy car.\nS\u0142awk\u00f3w lies near national road 94, between D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza and Olkusz. From Katowice and Sosnowiec, the easiest approach is via DK94. From Krak\u00f3w, drive via Olkusz and Boles\u0142aw.\nParking is available around the market square and nearby streets, but it may fill up during events. For walks in the Bia\u0142a Przemsza valley, parking closer to M\u0142y\u0144ska Street and the valley park is more convenient.\nBy bicycle.\nS\u0142awk\u00f3w is a good destination for a cycling trip from D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Sosnowiec, Bukowno or Olkusz. Routes in the area pass through forests, the Bia\u0142a Przemsza valley and former industrial landscapes. Check the surface before riding, as some local sections are unpaved or forest tracks.\nGet around.\nThe historic centre is small and best explored on foot. The market square, Austeria, town hall, parish church, cultural centre, town park, remains of fortifications and the castle ruins are all close to one another.\nFor more distant places, such as Niwa, Sowiarnia, the youth hostel, parts of the Bia\u0142a Przemsza valley and the outskirts, use a bicycle, car or local GZM buses.\nBuy.\nSmall shops and services are available around the market square and the town centre. For larger shopping, go to D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Sosnowiec or Olkusz."}
{"id": "218714", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218714", "title": "B\u0119dzin", "text": "B\u0119dzin is a county town in Silesian Voivodeship, southern Poland, in the Zag\u0142\u0119bie D\u0105browskie region, on the Czarna Przemsza river. It is part of the G\u00f3rno\u015bl\u0105sko-Zag\u0142\u0119biowska Metropolis and lies between Sosnowiec, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Czelad\u017a, Wojkowice and Psary.\nB\u0119dzin is one of the oldest towns in the region and a historic centre of Zag\u0142\u0119bie D\u0105browskie. Its best-known landmark is the medieval royal castle on Castle Hill. The town also has important Jewish heritage sites, the Mieroszewski Palace, the B\u0119dzin Underground, remains of medieval town walls, riverside walking routes and industrial heritage in Grodziec.\nUnderstand.\nB\u0119dzin was a royal town of the Crown of the Kingdom of Poland and an important point on the historical borderlands between Lesser Poland and Silesia. The historic core is concentrated around Castle Hill, the old town, Holy Trinity Church, the remains of the medieval town walls and the Czarna Przemsza river.\nThe town has several distinct visitor areas:\nAllow at least half a day for the castle, Castle Hill, the Underground, Mieroszewski Palace and selected Jewish heritage sites. Add Grodziec, St Dorothy's Hill and industrial sites if you want a full-day itinerary.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Katowice Airport (KTW) in Pyrzowice, about 16\u201325 km from B\u0119dzin depending on the route. By car, the usual approaches use DK86, S1 or local roads through Siewierz and Psary.\nBy public transport, use Transport GZM buses and interchange in B\u0119dzin, Sosnowiec or Katowice. Check current connections in the Transport GZM journey planner.\nBy train.\nB\u0119dzin is on railway line 1, the main Warsaw\u2013Katowice corridor. The most useful station for visitors is B\u0119dzin Miasto, close to the centre, castle and B\u0119dzin Underground.\nUseful timetables:\nRegional trains connect B\u0119dzin with Katowice, Sosnowiec, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Zawiercie, Cz\u0119stochowa and Gliwice. Some long-distance trains may also stop at B\u0119dzin Miasto, depending on the timetable.\nBy car.\nImportant roads in and around B\u0119dzin include:\nParking is available on streets and municipal car parks in the centre. On event days near the castle, Castle Hill or B\u0119dzin Arena, arrive early or use public transport.\nBy bus and tram.\nPublic transport is organised by Transport GZM. B\u0119dzin has direct bus and tram connections with Sosnowiec, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Czelad\u017a, Katowice, Wojkowice, Psary, Siewierz, Siemianowice \u015al\u0105skie, Piekary \u015al\u0105skie and Bytom.\nUseful stops:\nUseful tram lines include:\nBy bicycle.\nB\u0119dzin is in the Metrorower public bicycle system of the GZM metropolitan area. Stations are located near the castle, stadium, railway station, Syberka and residential districts. Check the current station map and availability at Metrorower.\nA useful cycling route is B\u0119dzin Miasto \u2013 castle \u2013 Gzich\u00f3w \u2013 Osiedle Zamkowe \u2013 Grodziec \u2013 St Dorothy's Hill. Cycling is also practical for trips to Czelad\u017a, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Sosnowiec and the Pogoria lakes.\nGet around.\nThe centre, castle, Castle Hill, B\u0119dzin Underground, Mizrachi Prayer House, Cukerman Prayer House and B\u0119dzin Miasto station are walkable. Mieroszewski Palace can be reached on foot through Gzich\u00f3w or by bus. Grodziec, \u0141agisza and St Dorothy's Hill require a bus, bicycle or car.\nPlan sightseeing by area:\nSee.\nJewish heritage.\nB\u0119dzin was one of the important Jewish centres of Zag\u0142\u0119bie. Before World War II, Jews formed a large part of the town's population. Prayer houses, cemeteries, traces of the former Jewish quarter and memorial sites survive in the urban space.\nDo.\nTrails.\nWalking and cycling trails in and around B\u0119dzin include:\nBuy.\nMost shops and services are in the centre, around Aleja Ko\u0142\u0142\u0105taja, Ma\u0142achowskiego Street, Modrzejowska Street, local markets and roads out of town.\nEat.\nB\u0119dzin's food options are scattered between the centre, castle area, hotels and residential districts. Check current opening hours before coming specifically for a meal.\nStay safe.\nB\u0119dzin is generally straightforward to visit, but take normal urban precautions around railway stations, parks and quiet areas after dark. Do not enter fenced, derelict or industrial sites, especially around old industrial heritage in Grodziec. Around cemeteries and Jewish heritage sites, behave respectfully and avoid disturbing residents."}
{"id": "218719", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218719", "title": "Czelad\u017a", "text": "Czelad\u017a is a town in Silesian Voivodeship, southern Poland, in Zag\u0142\u0119bie D\u0105browskie, on the Brynica River. It is part of the G\u00f3rno\u015bl\u0105sko-Zag\u0142\u0119biowska Metropolis and lies between B\u0119dzin, Sosnowiec, Katowice, Siemianowice \u015al\u0105skie and Wojkowice.\nCzelad\u017a is one of the oldest towns in Zag\u0142\u0119bie D\u0105browskie. For visitors, the main sights are the historic market square, St Stanislaus Church, the former Saturn coal mine complex, Saturn Museum, Elektrownia Contemporary Art Gallery, Saturn Palace, Grabek Park and the workers' colonies in the Piaski district.\nUnderstand.\nCzelad\u017a has two distinct faces. The first is the old town, with the market square, parish church and traces of a medieval urban layout. The second is the industrial town that developed in the 19th and 20th centuries around coal mining, especially the Saturn and Czelad\u017a mines.\nThe townscape includes palaces and villas connected with former mining companies, workers' housing, industrial heritage buildings, parks and sites connected with Jewish history. Czelad\u017a has no active railway station, but it is well connected by buses, trams in the Piaski area and the GZM public bicycle system.\nAllow 3\u20135 hours for the market square, church, Grabek Park, Saturn Museum and Elektrownia Gallery. With Saturn Palace, Termy Rzymskie, Grabek Park and Piaski, Czelad\u017a can fill a full day.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Katowice Airport () in Pyrzowice, about 23 km from Czelad\u017a. By car, the usual route is via DK86 and local roads around B\u0119dzin or Siewierz.\nBy public transport, you will usually need to change in B\u0119dzin, Sosnowiec or Katowice. Check current connections in the Transport GZM journey planner.\nBy train.\nCzelad\u017a has no active railway station. The most useful railheads are:\nFrom B\u0119dzin, Sosnowiec and Katowice, continue by Transport GZM buses or, for Piaski and M1, by tram and bus. When planning a trip, combine Koleo for trains with Transport GZM for local public transport.\nBy bus and tram.\nPublic transport is organised by Transport GZM. Czelad\u017a has connections with Katowice, Sosnowiec, B\u0119dzin, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Siemianowice \u015al\u0105skie, Wojkowice, Bytom and Piekary \u015al\u0105skie.\nUseful stops:\nUseful lines include:\nBy car.\nCzelad\u017a is on DK94, the road connecting Wroc\u0142aw, Bytom, Czelad\u017a, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Olkusz and Krak\u00f3w. Near the borders with B\u0119dzin and Sosnowiec runs DK86/S86, the main road towards Katowice, Tychy and Siewierz. The A1 and A4 motorways are also accessible via neighbouring cities.\nParking is available around the market square and nearby streets, but it may fill up during events. Large car parks are available at M1 Czelad\u017a, Hotel Szafran and retail areas along DK94.\nBy bicycle.\nCzelad\u017a is a good short cycling destination from Sosnowiec, B\u0119dzin, D\u0105browa G\u00f3rnicza, Katowice and Siemianowice \u015al\u0105skie. The Metrorower public bicycle system operates in the town, with stations around Szpitalna Street, Grodziecka Street, Hallera Street, Saturn Museum, J\u00f3zef\u00f3w, Spacerowa Street, Zwyci\u0119stwa Street and 1 Maja Street. Check current availability at Metrorower.\nGet around.\nThe centre, market square, church, Grabek Park, Saturn Palace and Saturn Museum can be visited on foot. For Piaski, M1, the districts near B\u0119dzin and Siemianowice, use buses, tram, bicycle or car.\nUseful orientation points:\nEat.\nFood options are concentrated around hotels, Grabek Park, M1 and the centre. Opening hours change, so check before travelling specifically for a meal.\nStay safe.\nCzelad\u017a is generally straightforward to visit. Use normal urban precautions around bus stops, shopping areas and parks after dark. Do not enter derelict or fenced industrial sites. In workers' estates and residential colonies, stay on public streets and respect residents' privacy. At the Jewish cemetery, behave quietly and respectfully."}
{"id": "218720", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218720", "title": "Kalavryta", "text": "Kalavryta is a historic mountain town in Achaea in the northern Peloponnese, set at the foot of Mount Helmos. Kalavryta combines history, alpine scenery and outdoor activities, making it a year-round destination.\nUnderstand.\nIt is best known for its role in the Greek War of Independence and for the tragic events of World War II, when much of the town was destroyed. \nGet in.\nThe most scenic route is the narrow-gauge Odontotos Rack Railway from Diakopto, climbing through the dramatic Vouraikos Gorge.\nBy car:\nRoad access is straightforward but mountainous."}
{"id": "218725", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218725", "title": "Jagjivanpur", "text": "Jagjivanpur (\u099c\u0997\u099c\u09cd\u099c\u09c0\u09ac\u09a8\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0 \"Jogojj\u012bbonpur\"), an archaeological site in central West Bengal, is home to Nandadirghi Mahavihara, a Pala-era Buddhist university.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThere are infrequent private buses between Malda and Jagjivanpur.\nFees and permits.\nPermits can be obtained from the State Archaeological Museum in Kolkata."}
{"id": "218726", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218726", "title": "Karnasuvarna", "text": "Karnasuvarna (\u0995\u09b0\u09cd\u09a3\u09b8\u09c1\u09ac\u09b0\u09cd\u09a3 \"Kor\u1e47osubor\u1e47o\"), an archaeological site in Murshidabad, covers the ruins of the former capital of Bengal under Shashanka (\u09b6\u09b6\u09be\u0999\u09cd\u0995 \"Shosh\u0101nko\")."}
{"id": "218727", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218727", "title": "Shibnibas", "text": "Shibnibas or Shibnibash (\u09b6\u09bf\u09ac\u09a8\u09bf\u09ac\u09be\u09b8 \"Shibnib\u0101s\"), a village in the Nadia district of West Bengal, is home to three temples built by Krishnachandra Ray, the king of Nadia, on the Churni River."}
{"id": "218736", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218736", "title": "Karystos", "text": "Karystos is a seaside town on the southern tip of Evvia, looking across the narrow strait toward the Greek mainland. It serves as the gateway to Mount Ochi, a rugged and windswept massif known for its ancient drakospita (\u201cdragon houses\u201d). The town\u2019s small fortress and scattered ruins reflect its layered past, while its position makes it a natural stop for travellers arriving by ferry from Rafina.\nUnderstand.\nThe area has been inhabited since antiquity, later developing under Venetian and Ottoman rule. \nGet in.\nBy ferry.\nThe most common route is from Rafina (near Athens) to Marmari (about 1 hour). From Marmari, it\u2019s a 15 km drive or bus/taxi ride to Karystos.\nBy road.\nDrive from Athens via Chalkida (bridge to Evia) and continue south (approx. 3\u20134 hours total).\nBy bus.\nKTEL buses connect Athens to Karystos, often via Chalkida."}
{"id": "218737", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218737", "title": "Katakolo", "text": "Katakolo is a small seaside port in Elis in the western Peloponnese. It is best known as the cruise gateway to ancient Olympia. Once a modest fishing village, it now serves as a seasonal stop for cruise ships visiting the archaeological site of Olympia and nearby western Peloponnese beaches. Despite this, Katakolo retains a relaxed waterfront atmosphere with caf\u00e9s, tavernas, and views over the Ionian Sea.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nKatakolo is about 10 km west of Pyrgos and easily reached via local roads.\nBy train.\nSeasonal rail service connects Katakolo with Pyrgos and onward to Olympia.\nBy bus.\nKTEL buses run between Pyrgos and Katakolo."}
{"id": "218746", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218746", "title": "Cheyyur", "text": "Cheyyur (\u0b9a\u0bc6\u0baf\u0bcd\u0baf\u0bc2\u0bb0\u0bcd \"Cheyy\u016br\") is a town in northern Tamil Nadu."}
{"id": "218747", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218747", "title": "Kyparissia", "text": "Kyparissia is a coastal town in western Messenia, overlooking the Ionian Sea. Kyparissia is a relaxed and relatively untouristed destination, making it a good base for exploring western Messenia. Built on the slopes below a medieval castle, it is divided into two distinct parts: the atmospheric Upper Town (Ano Poli), with narrow lanes and traditional stone houses, and the modern Lower Town along the coast, centred around the harbour and beaches.\nUnderstand.\nKyparissia occupies the site of ancient Cyparissia, mentioned in classical sources, and later developed into an important Byzantine and medieval settlement. Its castle, likely of Byzantine origin and later strengthened under Frankish and Venetian rule, dominates the skyline and offers sweeping views of the coastline. \nGet in.\nKyparissia lies about 65 km north of Kalamata and can be reached by car via the coastal road or inland highways. Regular regional buses connect the town with Kalamata and other settlements in Messenia. The nearest airport is Kalamata International Airport."}
{"id": "218748", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218748", "title": "Megalopolis", "text": "Megalopolis is a small town in Arcadia, in the central Peloponnese, best known for the extensive ruins of the ancient city that once stood here. \nUnderstand.\nMegalopolis was founded in 371 BC as a political and military centre to counter the power of Sparta. Ancient Megalopolis was conceived on a grand scale\u2014its name literally means \u201cGreat City.\u201d Although the modern town is modest and shaped by industry, the surrounding landscape of the Alfeios valley preserves traces of this ambitious past.\nGet in.\nMegalopolis lies about 30 km southwest of Tripoli and around 50 km from Kalamata. It is easily reached by car via the A7 motorway (Moreas Motorway), with exits nearby. Regional buses connect Megalopolis with Tripoli, Kalamata, and other towns in Arcadia and Messenia."}
{"id": "218754", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218754", "title": "Latur", "text": "Latur (\u0932\u093e\u0924\u0942\u0930 \"L\u0101t\u016br\") is a town in central Maharashtra."}
{"id": "218763", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218763", "title": "Bingham", "text": "Bingham is a small town in the Kennebec Valley region of Maine, located along the Kennebec River in Somerset County. It's a popular base for whitewater rafting, ATV riding, snowmobiling, hunting, and fishing, and a gateway to the more remote wilderness of the Moosehead Lake region and the North Woods.\nUnderstand.\nBingham sits on the banks of the Kennebec River along Route 201, the Old Canada Road National Scenic Byway, a historic route that once carried Benedict Arnold's expedition toward Quebec in 1775. Today the town is best known as an outdoor recreation hub: the Kennebec and nearby Dead River offer some of the best dam-controlled whitewater rafting on the East Coast, and the surrounding forests connect to Maine's extensive ATV and snowmobile trail networks (ITS).\nThe town's economy historically centered on logging and the timber trade, and forestry remains important in the region, but tourism\u2014especially rafting and four-season outdoor recreation\u2014now drives much of the local economy. Bingham is a genuinely small, working Maine town rather than a resort village, so don't expect much in the way of boutiques or nightlife; its appeal is the river, the woods, and easy access to true wilderness.\nGet in.\nBingham lies along US Route 201, roughly 35 miles (56 km) north of Skowhegan and about 75 miles (120 km) northwest of Augusta. There is no commercial air or rail service; nearly everyone arrives by car.\nGet around.\nBingham is walkable, with Main Street (Route 201) forming the spine of the town. A car is essential for reaching rafting outfitters, trailheads, and nearby villages, though several outfitters provide shuttle transport for their guests.\nConnect.\nCell coverage can be spotty once you leave Route 201 and head into surrounding forest areas; don't rely on a signal in the backcountry. Most lodges and outfitters in town offer WiFi for guests."}
{"id": "218764", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218764", "title": "Pie Town", "text": "Pie Town is a small unincorporated community in Catron County, Southwest New Mexico, along the Continental Divide in the high desert. The town is famous \u2014 well out of proportion to its tiny population \u2014 for its pie shops. \nUnderstand.\nPie Town got its name from a bakery that opened in the 1920s to serve travelers passing through on the way to a nearby mining claim. It sits directly on the Continental Divide Trail, and many thru-hikers pass through here to resupply and reward themselves with a slice. Every September the town hosts the Pie Town Pie Festival, drawing visitors from across the region.\nGet in.\nPie Town lies along U.S. Route 60, roughly halfway between Socorro to the east and the Arizona state line to the west. There is no public transportation; a car is essentially required.\nBy car.\nPie Town is about 1.5\u20132 hours west of Socorro via US-60. The nearest larger towns with services are Socorro (east) and Springerville, Arizona (west).\nOn foot.\nPie Town is a resupply point on the Continental Divide Trail (CDT); northbound and southbound hikers pass directly through town.\nGet around.\nThe town is tiny and entirely walkable; everything of interest is along or just off US-60.\nBuy.\nLimited shopping; a small general store carries basic supplies.\nEat.\nPie is the main event. A couple of small caf\u00e9s and diners serve homemade pies (fruit, cream, and specialty flavors) along with basic diner fare \u2014 burgers, sandwiches, and coffee. Hours can be limited and seasonal, so it's wise to call ahead if driving out of your way.\nDrink.\nNo bars; coffee and soft drinks are available at the local caf\u00e9s.\nSleep.\nLodging is very limited. Some small guesthouses, cabins, or hiker hostels catering to CDT thru-hikers may be available seasonally; camping is common in the surrounding national forest land.\nConnect.\nCell phone coverage is spotty in this remote part of New Mexico. Some lodging and caf\u00e9s may offer Wi-Fi."}
{"id": "218770", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218770", "title": "Millburn", "text": "Millburn is a town in Essex County, New Jersey where South Mountain Reservation, Hobble Falls, and other natural attractions are found. \nUnderstand.\nMillburn was founded by James, the brother of Charles II who had been granted the land in 1664 with the rest of Nieuw Nederland. He later gave Lord Berkeley control of the land because they were fellow fighters in the English Civil War. Later, a Revolutionary War Battle was fought in Millburn, including points like Hessian House and Washington Rock.\nAnne Hathaway, Rachel Zoe, and Cathy Scorsese were raised in Millburn\u2019s residential neighborhood of Short Hills, with prominent banker John Stewart purchasing a home on Stewart Street there in the 1880s. The population of Millburn in 2020 was 21,710. Though founded as part of Union County, it was incorporated separately in 1867 as part of Essex County.\nGet in.\nMillburn and Short Hills Stations of the Morristown and Gladstone Lines are located in Millburn. NJ Transit Buses 70 and 873 have stops in Millburn. The nearest major airports are JFK and EWR in New York City and Newark, respectively. New Jersey Route 24 and Interstate 78 have exits in Millburn, with Interstates 287 and 80 offering connections.\nGet around.\nBus 70 and 873 go through the town, but Millburn is mostly a walkable city. The main business districts are Morris Turnpike, Short Hills Station, Upper Millburn Avenue, Downtown, and Wyoming Center. A map of Millburn is shown on the right.\nSee.\nMillburn is a town of natural beauty. Some attractions include:\nBuy.\nMillburn has both 5th Avenue-level shopping and cheap thrift and variety department stores."}
{"id": "218783", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218783", "title": "Kynopiastes", "text": "Kynopiastes is a traditional village in the Messi region (Central Corfu) that is known for its well-preserved character, the village features elegant mansions dating from the 17th to 19th centuries, narrow tiled lanes, and small leafy squares that reflect the refined rural life of historic Corfu.\nGet in.\nKynopiastes is about 10 km from Corfu Town and Corfu International Airport."}
{"id": "218784", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218784", "title": "Kassiopi", "text": "Kassiopi is a lively coastal village on the northeast coast of Corfu, around 35 km from Corfu Town. Once a small fishing settlement, it has grown into one of the island\u2019s most popular resort villages, while still retaining a distinct charm centred around its picturesque harbour. The village blends its layered past with a relaxed holiday atmosphere, offering a mix of traditional tavernas, scenic views across the channel toward Albania, and easy access to some of Corfu\u2019s clearest waters. The combination of history, beaches, and a sociable evening scene makes it a favourite base for visitors exploring the island\u2019s northeast.\nUnderstand.\nImportant since antiquity, Kassiopi was known in Roman times for a sanctuary dedicated to Zeus. "}
{"id": "218788", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218788", "title": "Chengalpattu (district)", "text": "Chengalpattu is a district of northern Tamil Nadu."}
{"id": "218790", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218790", "title": "Kanchipuram (district)", "text": "Kanchipuram is a district of northern Tamil Nadu.\nUnderstand.\nKanchipuram district is one of the 38 districts in the state of Tamil Nadu in India. The area comprising the present day Kancheepuram district was earlier a part of Chingleput district. The original Chingleput district was split in 1997 into form the present day Kanchipuram and Tiruvallur districts.\nKanchipuram district is one of the historically important areas of northern Tamil Nadu. The region was associated with the Pallava kingdom and later came under the influence of several South Indian dynasties.\nThe district is particularly famous for Kanchipuram, one of the traditional sacred cities of India. The city contains numerous Hindu temples representing both Shaivite and Vaishnavite traditions.\nSilk weaving is another important part of the area's identity. Kanchipuram silk sarees are traditionally handwoven and are widely known throughout India.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport is Chennai International Airport, approximately 60\u201370 km from Kanchipuram depending on the route.\nBy train.\nKanchipuram has a railway station with local and regional services. Trains connect the city with Chennai, Arakkonam, Tirupati and other destinations. \nBy bus.\nTamil Nadu state buses connect Kanchipuram with Chennai, Vellore, Tiruvannamalai, Pondicherry and other towns.\nBy car.\nKanchipuram is about 70 km from Chennai. Road connections include routes towards Chennai, Vellore, Chengalpattu and Bengaluru."}
{"id": "218793", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218793", "title": "Tiruvallur (district)", "text": "Tiruvallur is the northernmost district of Tamil Nadu."}
