{"id": "218809", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218809", "title": "Livadeia", "text": "Livadia is in West Central Greece. Livadia is a working Greek town with a strong local identity, combining historical layers with a pleasant natural setting. The town's defining feature is the Herkyna River, which flows through a small wooded gorge (the Krya area), creating waterfalls, stone bridges, and shady paths\u2014making it one of the most attractive urban river settings in mainland Greece.\nUnderstand.\nLivadia sits in the region of Boeotia, about 130 km northwest of Athens. In antiquity it was known for the oracle of Trophonius, once one of the most famous prophetic sites in Greece. \nGet in.\nLivadia lies just off the main highway linking Athens to Central Greece:"}
{"id": "218814", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218814", "title": "Assam Hills", "text": "The Assam Hills comprises a large hilly tribal region of Assam, separating the Assamese-speaking Brahmaputra Valley from the Bengali-speaking Barak Valley. Many areas are rugged and scenic, with rice cultivations in river valleys.\nUnderstand.\nThe Hills are known for its breathtaking natural beauty. A trip to the region, while rewarding, is somewhat adventurous. While the isolation of the Hills has helped preserve the tribal practices, it also means a lack of development.\nThe Hills consists of two hilly areas, each with their own tribes:\nTalk.\nIndigenous languages of the various tribal groups are most common. Many speak Hindi, Bengali, Assamese or English as a second language in urban areas. The region is home to Haflong Hindi, a Hindi-based pidgin.\nThere are daily commercial bus services from Guwahati and Shillong. The area can be reached by train from Guwahati, with a transfer in Lumding. A scenic local train to Silchar has stops in Langting and Haflong Hill.\nGet around.\nThree-wheeled taxis are ubiquitous in the towns: always make sure of your destination and fare before getting in. Inter-town transport is available in buses and large all-terrain vehicles that often carry ten or more passengers in a quite crowded cab.\nSee.\nGo to the very top of the hill in Haflong for a marvellous view of the Borail Range to the west of the town. Wander down from there into the market to see what might be available. Go to the Circuit House for a view of the Jatinga River. East of the main road is Haflong Lake.\nDo.\nHiking through the countryside would prove a treat for nature lovers. It would be unwise to do this without a local guide.\nDrink.\nThe Dimasa are proud of their traditional \"dju\", a rice beer.\nStay safe.\nThere has been a history of inter-tribal violence in the area. There are also a number of violent liberation groups. Obviously, it is good to check a knowledgeable travel agent for local conditions. Because of the violence, there is a heavy police and paramilitary presence in parts of the area. It is probably unwise to travel in the region without a local guide."}
{"id": "218818", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218818", "title": "Mani Peninsula", "text": "The Mani Peninsula is one of Greece\u2019s most stark and compelling regions \u2014 a land of stone tower villages, Byzantine churches, and rugged mountains plunging into the sea. Remote, fiercely independent, and largely untouched by mass tourism, Mani offers a raw, authentic slice of the Peloponnese where history, landscape, and tradition feel powerfully alive.\nUnderstand.\nThe Mani Peninsula is the central \u201cfinger\u201d of the Peloponnese in southern Greece, stretching between the Laconian Gulf to the east and the Messenian Gulf to the west. It is one of the most distinctive cultural landscapes in Greece, known for its stark mountains, fortified stone tower villages, and a long history of relative isolation that helped preserve strong local traditions.\nHistorically, Mani was never fully subdued by outside powers for long periods, including Byzantines, Venetians, and Ottomans. This independence shaped its architecture and identity: villages were often built like small fortresses, with family tower-houses designed for defence. The landscape remains sparse, rugged, and intensely atmospheric, with olive groves, rocky slopes, and dramatic sea views.\nGet in.\nMost visitors reach the Mani Peninsula by road from Kalamata or Sparta. The main approach follows the eastern side of the peninsula via Areopoli, with smaller, often scenic roads branching west toward the more remote coastal villages. A car is strongly recommended, as it allows you to explore the region\u2019s scattered sights and beaches at your own pace.\nPublic transport is available but limited. KTEL buses connect Kalamata and Sparta with Areopoli several times per day (typically around 3\u20135 services, depending on season), Day returns trips are usually possible but check the current timetable.\nGet around.\nGetting around the Mani Peninsula is part of the experience \u2014 and part of the challenge.\nBy car\nA car is by far the easiest and most practical way to explore. The peninsula is large, sparsely populated, and connected by narrow, winding roads; distances that look short on a map can take longer than expected. \nWalking\nMani is surprisingly rewarding on foot, especially around villages and short coastal stretches. Places like Areopoli, Limeni, Kardamyli, and the stone-built settlements of Inner Mani are best explored by wandering their narrow lanes and old mule paths. There are also longer walking opportunities \u2014 including coastal hikes and sections of old kalderimi (stone paths) linking villages \u2014 though signage can be limited.\nCycling\nCycling is possible and increasingly popular, particularly in the northern and western parts of Mani where roads are quieter and scenery is spectacular. However, be prepared for steep climbs, heat, and limited shade. Bike rental and guided cycling are available in the region.\nScooters / motorbikes\nScooter and small motorbike hire can sometimes be found in larger bases such as Areopoli, though options are limited compared to more touristy parts of Greece. \nSee.\nOther.\nCape Tainaron Lighthouse &amp; Sanctuary of Poseidon At the southernmost tip of mainland Greece, combining a historic lighthouse with the ruins of an ancient sanctuary."}
{"id": "218820", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218820", "title": "Sattal", "text": "Sattal (\u0938\u093e\u0924\u0924\u093e\u0932 \"S\u0101tt\u0101l\") is a cluster of seven interconnected freshwater lakes in the Kumaon region of Uttarakhand, India. Nestled at an altitude of 1,370\u202fm within dense oak and pine forests, it is a tranquil haven for birdwatchers, nature lovers, and those seeking a quiet alternative to the bustling hill stations of Nainital and Bhimtal.\nUnderstand.\nSattal, literally \u201cseven lakes,\u201d is a unique freshwater ecosystem formed by tectonic depressions filled by monsoon runoff and underground springs. The lakes \u2014 Purna Tal, Ram Tal, Sita Tal, Laxman Tal, Nal\u2011Damayanti Tal, Sukh Tal, and Garud Tal \u2014 are interconnected, varying in size and character. Unlike the more tourist\u2011heavy Nainital, Sattal remains largely unspoiled, visited primarily by ecotourists, birders, and families wanting a peaceful retreat.\nThe area falls within a designated Important Bird Area (IBA) and supports over 500 bird species, both resident and migratory. The forests are rich in oak, rhododendron, and pine, sheltering butterflies, barking deer, wild boar, and an occasional leopard. A Christian ashram and mission estate, established by E. Stanley Jones in the 1930s, adds a spiritual dimension to the lakes.\nClimate.\nBest visiting season is March\u2013May and October\u2013November. Summers are mild and pleasant; winters are cold, with occasional frost. Monsoon (June\u2013September) is heavy, transforming the forest into a lush paradise, though some trails become slippery and leech\u2011prone.\nGet in.\nSattal is about 25\u202fkm from Haldwani/Kathgodam and 4\u202fkm from Bhimtal. The nearest railhead is Kathgodam Railway Station (KGM).\nGet around.\nSattal is small enough to explore on foot. The main lake, Purna Tal, has a well\u2011trodden path connecting the shores. To reach the other lakes, forest trails branch off from the main road. A vehicle is only needed to reach Sattal from Bhimtal. Once you're within Sattal, walking is the best way to soak in the silence.\nBuy.\nSattal is not a shopping destination. There are no permanent markets; a few small stalls near the main lake sell:\nThere are no ATMs in Sattal. The nearest ATM is in Bhimtal (4\u202fkm). Carry sufficient cash.\nEat.\nDining in Sattal is simple and centred around family\u2011run eateries attached to guesthouses.\nSleep.\nAccommodation ranges from basic forest\u2011facing camps to comfortable guesthouses and the historic ashram.\nStay healthy.\nThe nearest hospital is in Bhimtal (4\u202fkm). Carry basic first\u2011aid; pharmacies are only in Bhimtal.\nRespect.\nRemove shoes at the small lakeside temples; modest dress is appreciated.\nConnect.\nMobile networks are weak in many lakeside areas; Airtel and Jio work best. Guesthouses usually have Wi\u2011Fi, but speeds are slow. The nearest post office is in Bhimtal (PIN 263136). Internet caf\u00e9s do not exist; plan to disconnect and unwind.\nGo next.\nAll destinations are connected by shared jeeps and taxis from Bhimtal."}
{"id": "218821", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218821", "title": "Kainchi Dham", "text": "Kainchi Dham (Hindi: \u0915\u0948\u0902\u091a\u0940 \u0927\u093e\u092e) is a revered ashram and temple complex dedicated to the saint Neem Karoli Baba, situated in the Kumaon hills of Uttarakhand, India. Nestled in a narrow valley 17\u202fkm from Nainital on the Bhowali\u2013Almora road, this spiritual haven is famed for its serene atmosphere, miraculous legends, and the thousands of devotees\u2014including global icons like Steve Jobs and Mark Zuckerberg\u2014who have found solace here.\nUnderstand.\nKainchi Dham (literally \u201cScissors Bend\u201d) gets its name from the two sharp hairpin curves on the mountain road that clamp the ashram like a pair of scissors. The ashram was established in 1964 by Neem Karoli Baba (Maharaj\u2011ji), a mystic saint whose unconditional love and simple teaching\u2014\"Love everyone, serve everyone, remember God\"\u2014drew seekers from around the globe. His devotees included Ram Dass (author of 'Be Here Now'), Bhagavan Das, and later, tech pioneers like Steve Jobs and Mark Zuckerberg, who famously visited in search of clarity.\nThe complex consists of a central temple housing Hanuman and Neem Karoli Baba\u2019s samadhi, a meditation hall, several smaller shrines, and a Dharmashala (pilgrim lodging). The stream beside the temple, fed by a perennial spring, is considered sacred and is used for ritual bathing. The entire site exudes a palpable quiet energy, amplified by the surrounding pine and oak forests and the constant murmur of water.\nKainchi Dham is particularly vibrant during the June 15 Bhandara (memorial feast), when tens of thousands of devotees gather to offer free food in Maharaj\u2011ji\u2019s honour, celebrating his love and service legacy.\nClimate.\nThe best time to visit is March\u2013June and September\u2013November. Summers are mild and pleasant; monsoons (July\u2013August) make the valley lush but can cause slippery paths. Winters are cold, with occasional frost, but the clear skies offer stunning Himalayan views.\nGet in.\nKainchi Dham is situated on the Nainital\u2013Almora highway, about 17\u202fkm from Nainital and 45\u202fkm from Haldwani/Kathgodam.\nGet around.\nKainchi Dham is compact and entirely walkable. The ashram premises include the temple, samadhi, meditation hall, and Dharmashala, all connected by short paved paths. The narrow road outside the ashram leads to a few trails into the surrounding forest, perfect for a meditative walk. No vehicles are allowed inside the complex.\nSee and do.\nLegend of Kainchi Dham.\nNeem Karoli Baba is said to have chosen the site after a divine vision; the name \u201cKainchi\u201d (scissors) is believed to symbolise cutting away of ego and worldly attachments. Many devotees recount miracles\u2014instant healings, visions, and materialisation of vibhuti (sacred ash) at the temple. The saint\u2019s famous teaching, \u201cLove everyone, serve everyone, remember God,\u201d permeates every stone. The ashram\u2019s guestbook holds signatures from world leaders, and a tree near the gate is rumoured to have grown from Maharaj\u2011ji\u2019s staff.\nBuy.\nKainchi Dham does not have a market. A few stalls outside the gate sell:\nThe nearest town with proper shops is Bhowali (4\u202fkm), known for its fruit market and local handicrafts.\nEat.\nEating options are limited, in keeping with the ashram\u2019s simplicity.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is basic, emphasising simplicity and devotion.\nConnect.\nMobile signals inside the valley are weak; BSNL and Jio provide intermittent connectivity. The ashram offers free Wi\u2011Fi in the Dharmashala for basic use. There is no post office within Kainchi Dham; the nearest is in Bhowali (PIN 263132). Internet caf\u00e9s do not exist; embrace the digital detox.\nGo next.\nAll destinations are accessible by shared jeeps and taxis from the Kainchi Dham crossroads or Bhowali."}
{"id": "218825", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218825", "title": "Igrejinha", "text": "Igrejinha is a town in Grande Porto Alegre in the state of Rio Grande do Sul. It draws many visitors during it Bavarian Oktoberfest beer festival. \nUnderstand.\nIgrejinha is a town of about 38,000 inhabitants in the Paranhana River valley. During the 19th century, German immigrants settled the colony of Santa Maria do Mundo Novo, which was split into an upper, middle and lower part. Today these correspond to the towns of Tr\u00eas Coroas, Igrejinha and Taquara.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airport is Aeroporto Salgado Filho serving the state capital Porto Alegre; it's about 80 km south of Igrejinha.\nBy bus.\nThere are bus services from nearby cities. The is at Rua da Rep\u00fablica, 132 in the middle of town and has toilets and a snack bar.\nBy car.\nThe main way in is state highway RS 115 connecting from Tr\u00eas Coroas, Gramado and Canela in the north, and Taquara, Novo Hamburgo (via RS 239) and Porto Alegre (via RS 020). The RS 020 also goes north to S\u00e3o Francisco de Paula. There are also many smaller local roads.\nGet around.\nWalk, drive or take a taxi/Uber.\nBuy.\nAlong the RS 115 there are shops selling shoes and leather goods.\nDrink.\nYou can try out local beers such as Tio Remi, Not\u00f3rios Bier and Bufalos Bier at the Oktoberfest or otherwise. There is also local wine."}
{"id": "218826", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218826", "title": "Tr\u00eas Coroas", "text": "Tr\u00eas Coroas is a city in Grande Porto Alegre, in the state of Rio Grande do Sul. The surrounding hills and the Paranhana River flowing through are good for rafting and other outdoor activities but the main sight here is something unusual in this part of the world - a Tibetan Buddhist temple.\nUnderstand.\nThe first recorded settlement in this hilly region was a lot of land (\"sesmaria\") granted by the Portuguese Crown to a certain Ant\u00f4nio Borges de Almeide Le\u00e3es in 1814, a few years before Brazil became independent. Since the mid-19th century German and later Italian immigrants settled here forming the colony of Santa Maria do Mundo Novo, divided in three parts that today make up the towns of Tr\u00eas Coroas, Igrejinha and Taquara. \nOver the next century the town was known by half a dozen of different names, but in 1944 the name of was established as Tr\u00eas Coroas, after an araucaria tree with three crowns that was used as a landmark. Starting out as a farming colony with the river as the only way in and out, Tr\u00eas Coroas is today a city of 24,000 inhabitants (as of 2024) and together with nearby towns a center of footwear industry.\nIn 1995 Chagdud Tulku Rinpoche, a teacher of Vajrayana Tibetan Buddhism, established the Khadro Ling Temple on a hill a few kilometers northeast of Tr\u00eas Coroas. While it was not intended as a tourist attraction, the beautiful complex is a major attraction in the region and visitors are welcome.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airports are Aeroporto Salgado Filho in Porto Alegre, and Aeroporto Hugo Cantergiani in Caxias do Sul. The former also has international flights.\nBy car.\nThe main way in is state highway BR-115 following the river valley. Starting from Porto Alegre, the straightest way is RS-020 to Taquara, a short bit RS-239 and join the BR-115 from there. The driving distance from Porto Alegre is around 100 km.\nBy bus.\nThe (\"Rodovi\u00e1ria de Tr\u00eas Coroas\") is at the intersection of Av. Santa Maria and R. Am\u00e9rica just east of the river and in practice just a bus stop outside a small commercial building. \nCitral operates a few buses a day from Porto Alegre (2h), Gramado (30 min) and Novo Hamburgo (2h 45min).\nGet around.\nDowntown is a little over 1 km across, and as such walkable (there are sidewalks along the streets). For locations further out, drive or take a taxi/Uber.\nDo.\nRafting.\nThere are several rafting parks up the Paranhana river:\nBuy.\nAs a small town, Tr\u00eas Coroas unsurprisingly isn't exactly a shopping destination. There are supermarkets, pharmacies, opticians, banks and similar everyday businesses.\nConnect.\nOperators Claro and TIM have 4G coverage, Vivo 5G coverage in and around Tr\u00eas Coroas. There may not be mobile coverage outside populated areas and main roads. In addition some accommodations offer free Wi-Fi."}
{"id": "218829", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218829", "title": "Jakusko", "text": "Jakusko is a local government in Yobe State, Nigeria. The town is known for farming, livestock rearing, and its role as a local commercial centre serving surrounding communities.\nUnderstand.\nJakusko has a hot climate, with a long dry season and a shorter rainy season. Farming and livestock rearing are the main occupations, while Hausa, Fulfulde, and English are commonly spoken.\nGet in.\nJakusko is accessible by road from major towns in Yobe State, like Potiskum, Gashua, and Nguru. Commercial buses, taxis, and motorcycles provide transportation within the area.\nGet around.\nThe most common means of transport within the town are motorcycles known as (achaba), tricycles also known as (keke napep), and taxis. \nWalking is also convenient for short distances."}
{"id": "218841", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218841", "title": "Ghatal", "text": "Ghatal (\u0998\u09be\u099f\u09be\u09b2 \"Gh\u0101\u1e6d\u0101l\") is a heritage town on the Shilabati River in West Bengal.\nUnderstand.\nThe culture of Ghatal is different from the rest of Paschim Medinipur, especially regarding rituals and customs. The Shitala worship is carried out with a procession of hundreds of dhaks, and open operas acting on the stage dragged and pushed by the men on pitch road running through the town.\nNearby Birsingha (\u09ac\u09c0\u09b0\u09b8\u09bf\u0982\u09b9 \"B\u012brsinho\") is the birthplace of social reformer Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nBy far the best way to get into Ghatal. The town is on NH 116A, which connects Ghatal to Arambagh and Panskura.\nBy train.\nGhatal is one of the few major towns in the region without a proper rail connectivity. Both Arambagh PC Sen\u00a0\u00a0HWH\u00a0 and Panskura\u00a0\u00a0SE\u00a0 are equally far away from Ghatal at about 32 km.\nStay safe.\nGhatal lies on the flood-prone Rupnarayan Valley."}
{"id": "218842", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218842", "title": "Chandrakona Road", "text": "Chandrakona Road (\u099a\u09a8\u09cd\u09a6\u09cd\u09b0\u0995\u09cb\u09a3\u09be \u09b0\u09cb\u09a1 \"Chondrok\u014d\u1e47\u0101 R\u014d\u1e0d\") is a town in Paschim Medinipur, West Bengal. It is named after the road to Chandrakona town that starts from here."}
{"id": "218843", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218843", "title": "Keshiary", "text": "Keshiary (\u0995\u09c7\u09b6\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09a1\u09bc\u09bf \"Keshiy\u0101\u1e5bi\") is in Paschim Medinipur."}
{"id": "218847", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218847", "title": "Totopara", "text": "Totopara (\u099f\u09cb\u099f\u09cb\u09aa\u09be\u09a1\u09bc\u09be \"\u1e6c\u014d\u1e6d\u014dp\u0101\u1e5b\u0101\"), a town in North Bengal, is home to the indigenous Toto people, on the border with Bhutan.\nUnderstand.\nThe Toto people, one of the world's smallest ethnic groups, were nearly extinct in the 1950s before being revived through recent measures. Their culture is completely unique to the tribe, and not related to any other neighbouring groups.\nGet in.\nTotopara Road connects Totopara to Madarihat. Whereas Trolly Line Road connects the town to Hasimara."}
{"id": "218848", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218848", "title": "Alipurduar (district)", "text": "Alipurduar (\u0986\u09b2\u09bf\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0\u09a6\u09c1\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09b0 \"\u0100lipurduy\u0101r\") is a district of North Bengal, at the foothills of the Himalayas.\nUnderstand.\nAlipurduar was once part of the Kamata Kingdom of the Koch dynasty, the same dynasty that ruled Cooch Behar, and acted as a trade route between Bengal-Assam and Bhutan. The region later came under the rule of Bhutan when the British captured it in 1865 after the Duar War under the command of Captain Hedayat Ali.\nAlipurduar is home to animal species like the famous one-horned rhino (\u0997\u09a3\u09cd\u09a1\u09be\u09b0 \"go\u1e47\u1e0d\u0101r\") and numerous other species of animals and birds. The region has an undulating span of tea gardens and rice plantation. Besides, it offers a pleasant environment, and is more relaxed than the nearby tourist destinations of Darjeeling and Gangtok.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:\nGet in.\nBy train.\nAlipurduar is well connected to all regions of India through the wide network of railways. There are many railway stations in the region providing easy mobility. Alipuduar Junction and New Alipuduar are the busiest ones.\nBy car.\nRoad travel is efficient in Alipurduar. There are many highways connecting the region to different parts of the country. Most road networks to North-Eastern India pass through this region. There are state highways that provide easy transport to places within the state of West Bengal. State Highway 12 (SH 12) connects most of the region. All major national highway systems connecting North-Eastern India with mainland India pass through this region.\nEat.\nThe cuisine in this region is mostly similar to that of the general cuisine in the entire Bengal region. In the northern cities of this region, like Siliguri, momo, a steamed filled dumpling, is quite popular along with its variations. Apart from this, bhapa pitha, a traditional rice cake, is quite popular. Chop, a potato and spice-stuffed deep-fried snack is also eaten and well-liked by locals. Chop can also be made with eggs, prawns and other variations.\nDrink.\nThere are bars in cities. In some rural areas, a fermented rice beverage is available, but it is hard to find. Drinking alcohol in public places is generally frowned upon. \nStay safe.\nThere is a low crime rate in the cities thanks to proactive police forces. Thefts like pickpockets can be seen in crowded areas. The weather in the summer is mostly clear. During monsoons, there are frequent heavy isolated thunderstorms, while in winters, heavy fog at night and early morning reduces visibility."}
{"id": "218858", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218858", "title": "Haldibari", "text": "Haldibari (\u09b9\u09b2\u09a6\u09bf\u09ac\u09be\u09a1\u09bc\u09bf \"Holdib\u0101\u1e5bi\") in Cooch Behar is the last station of North Bengal in West Bengal."}
{"id": "218873", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218873", "title": "Sealdah Station", "text": "Sealdah Station (Bengali: \u09b6\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09b9 \u09b8\u09cd\u099f\u09c7\u09b6\u09a8 \"Shiy\u0101ldoho S\u1e6deshon\", IR code: SDAH) is a major railway station of Kolkata in India. The core of the extensive Kolkata Suburban Railway, Sealdah is also served by over 70 long-distance trains from other parts of the country.\nUnderstand.\nSealdah (\"SHEE-ahl-dah\") is one of India's largest rail complexes with 21 platforms. It is also India's second-busiest station with more than 400 million people using it every year. The station is in North Kolkata amid the city's chaos.\nSealdah is a major hub of the Kolkata Suburban Railway across the eastern bank of the Hooghly River, from Lalgola to Namkhana. In addition to nearly 650 suburban trains, Sealdah is also served by over 50 express trains from different parts of India, including the famous \"Darjeeling Mail\" that used to be the go-to for North Bengal. Bridges like the Vivekananda Setu, the Jubilee Bridge (later the Sampreeti Setu) and the Nashipur Rail Bridge are the essential links that connect the station to the rest of India.\nHistory.\nThe name \"Sealdah\" is said to be a corruption of the name \"Shrig\u0101ldw\u012bp\" (\u09b6\u09c3\u0997\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09cd\u09ac\u09c0\u09aa), meaning \"Jackal Island\". Sealdah was one of the villages the East India Company (EIC) obtained from Mughal emperor Farrukhsiyar in 1717.\nSealdah Station was opened on 2 December 1862 as part of the Calcutta\u2013Kushtia railway line. At the time, Sealdah consisted of four platforms with a tin-roofed room. Other than Kushtia, the station also had trains to Canning and Ranaghat. The present building was designed by architect Walter Glanville, and was opened in 1869.\nIn the early 1940s, Sealdah was served by trains from different parts of East Bengal and Assam. In 1947, East Bengal became part of Pakistan, which led to a surge of trains full of Hindu refugees from East Bengal to Kolkata. After that, East Bengal (later East Pakistan) was served by three express trains from Sealdah till 1965: \"East Bengal Express\" to Goalundo Ghat, \"East Bengal Mail\" to Parbatipur and \"Barisal Express\" to Khulna.\nBy that time, Sealdah evolved from a major terminal of East Bengal and Assam trains to the core of the Kolkata Suburban Railway.\nSealdah was also served by horse-drawn trams, and later electric trams. However, the tram terminus was closed in 1981 due to the construction of the Vidyapati Setu flyover. Nonetheless, there were other tram services around Sealdah until 2019 in excuse of damaging the flyover.\nThe metro station was planned as early as 1971, and was opened to the public on 11 July 2022.\nServices.\nSealdah Station is served by five broad-gauge railway lines under the Eastern Railway (ER): Sealdah North, Sealdah Main, Sealdah South, the Calcutta Chord Link (CCL) and the Kolkata Circular Railway (KCR). The station is additionally served by the standard-gauge Kolkata Metro, which connects it to other parts of Kolkata.\nThe Sealdah-bound trains terminate at one of the following terminals, with platform numbers in brackets:\nServices.\nHowrah Station is served by two broad-gauge rail networks: the Eastern Railway (ER) and the South Eastern Railway (SER). The station is additionally served by the standard-gauge Kolkata Metro, which connects the station to other parts of Kolkata.\nThe Howrah-bound trains terminate at one of the two following terminals, with platform numbers in brackets:\nLocal transportation.\nBy car.\nThere are multiple ways to get into the station, but the easiest and the most common way is via Beleghata Main Road. Take Beleghata Main Road from AJC Bose Road or APC Road, and then take the station road identified with a mini Big Ben replica. The is at the end of the station lot.\nAn alternative way is from Bowbazar via Bepin Bihari Ganguly Road (BB Ganguly Road). However, the intersection at APC Road is often teemed by hawkers and vendors at peak time.\nBy taxi.\nSealdah's prepaid taxi stand is just outside the station's main entrance, covered by a large canopy. There used to be a queue at the taxi counter before it was effectively shut down in favour of the Yatri Sathi app, developed by West Bengal's IT department. The app is available on both Android and iOS.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nThe four parts of Sealdah Station are accessible within a single building. From Sealdah Main, walk northwest for \"Sealdah North\", and walk southeast for \"Sealdah South\". The metro station is at the Sealdah South side.\nBy BOV.\nYou can take a battery-operated vehicle (BOV) within Sealdah Station to avoid walking long distances. It is particularly useful for people with disabilities.\nWait.\nThere is a large, covered waiting area inside the station for passengers awaiting connecting trains.\nEat and drink.\nAlcohols are not permitted to sell here due to the Indian Railways' broader anti-alcohol policy.\nBuy.\nSeveral small stalls can be found around the station selling books. Like most Indian stations, there's a One Station One Product outlet at the station, selling local handicrafts.\nAutomatic teller machines (ATMs) of major banks are available at Sealdah Main and South terminals.\nConnect.\nLike most railway stations, Sealdah has a free Wi-Fi service named RailWire. There are also some private telecom operators providing free Wi-Fi at the station.\nSleep.\nThe nearest hotels are in Esplanade and North Kolkata districts.\nNearby.\nKolkata is the obvious place to visit for travellers trapped here. With a rich colonial history and a unique cosmopolitan culture, it is hard to be bored in Kolkata."}
{"id": "218877", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218877", "title": "Nafpaktos", "text": "Nafpaktos is a picturesque coastal town in West Central Greece, in the region of Aetolia-Acarnania, overlooking the northern shore of the Gulf of Corinth. Built amphitheatrically beneath a vast Venetian castle and centred around one of the most charming small harbours in Greece, it blends medieval atmosphere with a relaxed seaside vibe. Despite its rich history\u2014most famously linked to the Battle of Lepanto (1571)\u2014it remains pleasantly low-key compared to more famous Greek destinations.\nUnderstand.\nKnown in antiquity as Naupactus, the town has been inhabited since at least classical times and played a strategic role controlling access to the Gulf of Corinth. Its importance continued through Byzantine and Venetian periods, when the impressive castle and fortified harbour were constructed and expanded. Under Ottoman rule it became known as Lepanto, giving its name to the famous naval battle between the Holy League and Ottoman Empire.\nToday, Nafpaktos is a laid-back destination popular with Greek visitors, especially in summer. It offers a mix of history, scenic walks, and nearby beaches without the crowds of larger resorts. The old town, clustered around the harbour, is full of caf\u00e9s and tavernas, while pine-covered hills rise immediately behind.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nNafpaktos lies just north of the Rio\u2013Antirrio Bridge, about 3 hours from Athens, or 2.5 hours from Ioannina. The scenic coastal road along the Gulf of Corinth is particularly enjoyable.\nBy bus.\nKTEL buses connect Nafpaktos with Athens, Patras, and other regional towns.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Araxos Airport (near Patras), though most international travellers arrive via Athens."}
{"id": "218879", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218879", "title": "Kaushambi", "text": "Kaushambi (\u0915\u094c\u0936\u093e\u0902\u092c\u0940 \"Kaush\u0101mb\u012b\") is an archaeological site in southern Uttar Pradesh. It was once the capital of the Vatsa Kingdom and an ancient trading centre on the Indian Plains.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nLike all other district headquarters in Uttar Pradesh, Kaushambi is connected by a state highway (SH 95). SH 95 branches off from the Grand Trunk Road near Prayagraj."}
{"id": "218887", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218887", "title": "Monemvasia", "text": "Monemvasia is in Laconia.\nMonemvasia is one of Greece\u2019s most dramatic and atmospheric destinations \u2014 a medieval fortress town carved into a huge rock rising from the sea off the coast of Laconia in the southeastern Peloponnese. Often called the \u201cGibraltar of the East,\u201d it is connected to the mainland by a narrow causeway, hiding a remarkably preserved Byzantine and Venetian town within its walls.\nUnderstand.\nFounded in the 6th century as a refuge from invasions, Monemvasia (from \"moni emvasis\" \u2014 \u201csingle entrance\u201d) grew into a powerful fortified city controlling key sea routes. Over centuries it passed between Byzantine, Venetian, and Ottoman rule, each leaving visible marks in its architecture.\nToday, the Lower Town is a living settlement of stone houses, churches, and small squares, while the Upper Town \u2014 largely abandoned \u2014 crowns the summit with ruins and sweeping views. Despite increasing popularity, Monemvasia retains a timeless, almost cinematic atmosphere, especially outside peak summer.\nGet in.\nMonemvasia is reached via the modern mainland town of Gefyra, connected to the rock by a short causeway."}
{"id": "218889", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218889", "title": "Puslinch", "text": "Puslinch Township is in Welington County.\nUnderstand.\nPuslinch is made up of these communities: Aberfoyle, Aikensville, Arkell, Badenoch, Crieff, Glen Christie, Killean, Paddock's Corners, Morriston, Corwhin, Downey, Puslinch, and Puslinch Lake.\nThe township's economy is primarily agricultural, with spring water bottling and aggregate mining also contibuting to employment. \nGet in.\nBy road.\nHighway 6 goes through Morriston between Guelph and Hamilton. \nHighway 401 goes through the centre of it between London and Toronto.\nBy bus.\nGO Bus 25 and 48 go to Aberfoyle GO.\nGet around.\nExit 299 on the 401 goes to Morriston and Aberfoyle.\nGo next.\nGuelph, a beautiful city north of it.\nKitchener-Cambridge-Waterloo, a beautiful sprawling tech hub, west of it.\nMilton, a beautiful city east of it.\nHamilton, a beautiful waterfall-filled city southeast of it.\nBrantford, a beautiful riverside city southwest of it."}
{"id": "218890", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218890", "title": "Gytheio", "text": "Gytheio (also spelled Gythio) is a relaxed seaside town in Laconia on the eastern edge of the Mani Peninsula. Once the main port of ancient Sparta, today it\u2019s known for its colourful harbourfront, easy beaches, and a laid-back, local feel. It\u2019s also tied to legend: nearby Cranae Island is where Helen of Troy and Paris are said to have spent their first night\u2014sparking the Trojan War.\nUnderstand.\nGytheio grew as a maritime outlet for Sparta and later prospered under the Romans. The modern town climbs a low hillside above a busy little port, with neoclassical houses painted in soft pastels. It\u2019s a convenient base for exploring the northern Mani, combining history, beaches, and good food without the heavier crowds found elsewhere in the Peloponnese.\nSee.\nHarbour &amp; Old Town"}
{"id": "218892", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218892", "title": "Aschheim", "text": "Aschheim is a municipality in the district of Munich (M\u00fcnchen Land), Upper Bavaria. It is a suburb of the state capital Munich, located east-northeast of the city, and directly on the A99 motorway ring road. The district of Dornach also belongs to the municipality.\nUnderstand.\nAschheim has approximately 10,000 inhabitants and is located at an altitude of about 512 meters above sea level. \nArchaeological finds prove that the area was inhabited as early as the Neolithic period. In the south of the municipal area, excavations also uncovered several Roman farmsteads (villae rusticae) from the 1st century AD, including one with its own underfloor heating and bathroom. In the early Middle Ages, Aschheim was an important center: in 756\u2013757, the first Bavarian provincial synod took place here, and its resolutions also provide the oldest written mention of the place name. Examinations of grave goods from the 6th century also provided one of the earliest direct pieces of evidence of the plague-causing bacterium \"Yersinia pestis\" in Europe. \nThe originally independent district of Dornach was incorporated into Aschheim in 1978 as part of the municipal area reform.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nFly into the Munich Airport. From there, you can drive 24 km south on highway B301, or take the S8 commuter rail to Ismaning and then switch to one of the local buses for the last 7 km. The X202 and 230 routes both stop in Aschheim. \nBy train.\nAschheim does not have its own S-Bahn station; instead, the town is served by neighbouring S-Bahn stations and connecting buses. The most practical route from Munich city centre is the S2 (towards Erding or Markt Schwaben) from a central station (e.g.,\u00a0\u202fMarienplatz, Hauptbahnhof, Ostbahnhof) to M\u00fcnchen-Riem or Heimstetten, then change to MVV bus line 263 to Aschheim. MVV bus line 263 connects to the Riem-Dornach S-Bahn station, the Messestadt West U-Bahn station, and, to the east, the Heimstetten S2 station, with a 10-minute frequency Monday to Friday until approximately 21:00.\nOr, by U-Bahn, take the U2 to Messestadt West works, where you can also connect to bus line 263 to Aschheim. This is\u00a0\u202foften the more direct subway connection from the city centre (e.g., Marienplatz/Sendlinger Tor) before transferring to the bus.\u00a0Fare zone:\u00a0Aschheim is in MVV zone M, i.e., in the core area \u2013 a regular inner-city ticket is sufficient.\u00a0Total travel time:\u00a0approximately 30\u201340 minutes from Munich city centre, depending on your starting point and connections. For the exact connection, use the MVV timetable information or the MVGO app \u2013 there you can see live whether the S2/Riem or U2/Messestadt West is the faster option. Destination stops in Aschheim are \"Aschheim, Mitte\" or \"Aschheim, Erdinger Stra\u00dfe\" \u2013 depending on your destination.\nBy car.\nAschheim is on the motorway ring road\u00a0(exit\u00a014,\u00a0Aschheim/Ismaning,\u00a0or junction\u00a015,\u00a0Kirchheim near Munich,\u00a0depending on the direction of travel).\u00a0Travel time: the distance from the Kirchheim near Munich junction to the town centre is just over 5\u00a0minutes (approx.\u00a03\u20134\u00a0km).\u00a0\u00a0\nGet around.\nThe city bus network is integrated into the MVV (Munich Transport and Tariff Association)."}
{"id": "218944", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218944", "title": "New Delhi/Central", "text": "Central New Delhi (\u092e\u0927\u094d\u092f \u0928\u0908 \u0926\u093f\u0932\u094d\u0932\u0940 \"Madhya Na\u012b Dill\u012b\") contains the Kartavya Bhavan Area (\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0935\u094d\u092f \u092d\u0935\u0928 \u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947\u0924\u094d\u0930 \"Kartavya Bhavan Kshetra\"), the central administrative district of New Delhi. Formerly called Central Vista, the district was originally designed and constructed by Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker as the seat of the imperial government. It has been used as the seat of the three government branches of the Republic of India since 1950. It has seen a facelift in the 2020s as part of an effort to modernise the capital city.\nCentral New Delhi is especially nice in the evening and at night when the buildings are lit and the vendors come out to supply the many picnicking families.\nGet in.\nBy metro.\nCentral New Delhi is served by Central Secretariat\u00a0 and Seva Teerth\u00a0 metro stations.\nSee.\nThe Kartavya Bhavan Area can be neatly divided into four parts: the Raisina Hill, the Parliament Complex, the Kartavya Path and the India Gate Circle.\nRaisina Hill.\nThe Raisina Hill (\u0930\u093e\u092f\u0938\u0940\u0928\u093e \u0915\u0940 \u092a\u0939\u093e\u0921\u093c\u0940 \"R\u0101ys\u012bn\u0101 k\u012b Pah\u0101\u1e5b\u012b\") is the citadel of the Government of India, comprising the Rashtrapati Bhavan, the Central Secretariat and the newly-built Vice President's Enclave. It is often called the \"Acropolis of India\".\nSleep.\nThere's no hotel in the Kartavya Bhavan Area; all hotels nearby are listed under North or South New Delhi."}
{"id": "218947", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218947", "title": "New Delhi/North", "text": "North New Delhi (\u0909\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0930 \u0928\u0908 \u0926\u093f\u0932\u094d\u0932\u0940 \"Uttar Na\u012b Dill\u012b\") is the commercial enclave of New Delhi, north of the Kartavya Bhavan Complex. It consists of Connaught Place (CP), the city's commercial heart, as well as the Supreme Court, the symbol of India's judiciary.\nGet in.\nBy metro.\nNorth New Delhi is served by Rajiv Chowk\u00a0, Mandi House\u00a0, Barakhamba Road\u00a0, Janpath\u00a0, Patel Chowk\u00a0, Ramakrishna Ashram Marg\u00a0 and Supreme Court\u00a0 metro stations.\nBuy.\nThe Connaught Place (CP), officially the Rajiv Chowk, is a unique cross between a European shopping arcade, an Indian bazaar and an upmarket shopping mall. With all shops laid out in two circles, it's easy to get around and explore."}
{"id": "218948", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218948", "title": "New Delhi/South", "text": "South New Delhi (\u0926\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093f\u0923 \u0928\u0908 \u0926\u093f\u0932\u094d\u0932\u0940 \"Dakshi\u1e47 Na\u012b Dill\u012b\") is the city's residential and diplomatic enclave, south of the Kartavya Bhavan Complex.\nSee.\nChanakyapuri.\nChanakyapuri (\u091a\u093e\u0923\u0915\u094d\u092f\u092a\u0941\u0930\u0940 \"Ch\u0101\u1e47akyapur\u012b\"), literally the \"City of Chanakya\", is an affluent neighbourhood where many embassies and ministerial residences are located. Named after the famed prime minister of the Maurya Empire, Chanakyapuri was developed as the official residence for both politicians and civil servants of the Union Government. As such, it has numerous parks and open spaces and suffers few utility disruptions. It is served by local trains at Delhi Safdarjung\u00a0.\nLodi Gardens.\nThe Lodi Gardens is a huge urban open space with an interesting mix of nature and history. The park contains several historic monuments like Muhammad Shah's Tomb, Bada Gumbad, Sesha Gumbad, Sikandar Lodi's Tomb and Athpula.\nSleep.\nFor Hauz Khas, see Delhi/South#Sleep."}
{"id": "218949", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218949", "title": "Oristano", "text": "Oristano is in Sardinia and is the capital of the homonymous province."}
{"id": "218969", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218969", "title": "Garchumuk", "text": "Garchumuk (\u0997\u09a1\u09bc\u099a\u09c1\u09ae\u09c1\u0995 \"Go\u1e5bchumuk\") is a popular picnic spot in Howrah, at the confluence of Damodar and Hooghly rivers.\nGet in.\nGarchumuk is about 60 km from Kolkata.\nBy bus.\nGarchumuk is served by two Kolkata bus routes:\nBy train.\nLocal trains are available from Howrah to Uluberia. There are buses and three-wheelers from Uluberia to Garchumuk.\nBy car.\nFrom Kolkata, drive down NH 16 up to Uluberia and take Shyampur Road. Garchumuk is across the Damodar River."}
{"id": "218974", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218974", "title": "Berlin Hbf", "text": "Berlin Hauptbahnhof (lit. \"Berlin main station\") is the railway hub and main station of Berlin. Although the station isn't large, the multi-level station may appear confusing for first-time visitors.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBerlin Hauptbahnhof is the result of the reorganisation of long-distance rail services in the Berlin area after German reunification. Until World War II, Berlin had many terminal stations connected only by S-Bahn lines. In former East Berlin, the station now known as \"Ostbahnhof\" (lit \"East Station\") was temporarily renamed Hauptbahnhof, with Lichtenberg as another important hub. In West Berlin, Zoologischer Garten station was the main terminus for long-distance services. Following reunification, the reconstruction of the S-Bahn, the construction of the high-speed line to Hanover, the extension of services to Hamburg and Leipzig/Halle, and (still under construction) Dresden, long-distance services were consolidated. Following the \"mushroom concept,\" almost all long-distance trains reach the new underground station via a north-south tunnel. Depending on their direction, long-distance trains also stop at Berlin-S\u00fcdkreuz, Spandau, or Gesundbrunnen (also known as Nordkreuz). Trains from Poland travel via Ostbahnhof to the upper level. Today, almost exclusively S-Bahn and regional trains use the viaducts of the west-east Stadtbahn.\nThe current train station is on the site of the former Lehrter Bahnhof, which was destroyed during World War II. The Lehrter Stadtbahnhof, where the S-Bahn stopped, was demolished. The station is designed by the architectural firm Gerkhan Marg and Partners and was opened just in time for the 2006 FIFA World Cup. The construction of the main station relieved the previously notoriously overcrowded Berlin Stadtbahn (which sometimes suffered delays of several hours in the 1990s) and shortened the route for trains travelling north-south through Berlin, eliminating the need to route them via the Ringbahn and Stadtbahn to reach the Zoo station.\nLayout.\nBerlin Central Station may seem confusing at first glance, but its layout is quite simple. It's a so-called \"Turmbahnhof\" (roughly translates to tower station), where two lines intersect at 90\u00b0 angles on different levels. These are the north-south tunnel tracks on the lower level (platforms 1 to 8) and the east-west Stadtbahn (city railway) tracks on the upper level (platforms 11 to 14 and platforms 15/16 for the S-Bahn). Between these are three concourse levels. The lower concourse level allows passengers to change tracks between the tunnel platforms, and the upper concourse level allows passengers to change tracks between the Stadtbahn tracks. There is also a concourse level at street level, providing access to the station. All three concourse levels are lined with numerous shops and restaurants. An underground S-Bahn station is scheduled to open soon. The U5 U-bahn line terminates underground.\nLocal transport.\nU-Bahn.\nIn contrast to other German cities, the Hauptbahnhof is not well connected to Berlin's U-Bahn network, with the only U-Bahn line that connects the station being the U5. The U5 starts at Berlin Hbf, runs through the centre of Mitte, Alexanderplatz, Lichtenberg, Wuhletal and terminating at H\u00f6now.\nS-Bahn.\nThe S-Bahn network is considerably well-connected to Berlin Hbf \u2013 5 lines stop at the station:\nTram.\nTram routes M5, M8 and M10 stop by. The stop is on the northern end of the station. The three lines serve several important locations in central Berlin but also well connect the east. The tram extension towards Moabit (terminus Turmstra\u00dfe) opened in 2023. Further development of the western connection from the main station is planned.\nParking.\nThe carpark with direct access to the train station has over 800 parking spaces. Access is from the south directly from the Tiergarten Tunnel (B96), and from other directions via Invalidenstra\u00dfe/Clara-Jaschke-Stra\u00dfe. The price for parking is steep: it can cost \u20ac27 per day. Drop-off/pick-up is free for the first 15 minutes.\nThose who don't mind a short walk (10 to 15 minutes) can also try their luck with street parking, with varying chances of success depending on the time of day and day of the week. Opportunities are particularly plentiful towards Tiergarten.\nGet around.\nAll levels are connected by lifts \u2013 the station is fully accessible. Signage, lifts, and stair railings also include Braille labels.\nA mobility service centre can be reached at all times at \u260f.\nWait.\nThere is a public waiting area on the lower concourse level at the southern end, equipped with benches, a windshield, and a few heating elements. Officially, it is only open to passengers with a valid ticket, but the open gate system makes it practically unenforced. It's not particularly comfortable, nor is it meant to be; regular patrols by security and/or police ensure this.\nThe DB Lounge with a separate first-class area is located on the upper concourse level, west side, approximately below platforms 11 and 12. It's open daily from 06:00 to 22:00. The lounge is only accessible to passengers with a first-class ticket travelling outside the VBB network area (a destination outside the state of Brandenburg) or to BahnComfort customers.\nEat and drink.\nInside and outside the train station, there is a wide range of options, from bakeries, snack bars, kebab shops, and hamburger restaurants to more upscale restaurants with prices that are somewhat higher, as is typical for train stations.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels within the station, but there are some on Europaplatz and Washingtonplatz. See City West \u00a7 Sleep and Mitte \u00a7 Sleep for individual listings nearby."}
{"id": "218979", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218979", "title": "Cuglieri", "text": "Cuglieri is a town in Sardinia in the province of Oristano.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 Bosa-Oristano bus line takes you to Cuglieri. There are four bus stops along Corso Umberto I (SS 292 Nord Occidentale Sarda): the one with a shelter at coming from Bosa, Sennariolo, and Scano di Montiferro; the one, halfway between the pharmacy and the town newsstand; the one with a shelter, and the one.\nGet around.\nThe historic center of Cuglieri can be easily explored by foot.\nBuy.\nStreet market.\nEvery Saturday, the street market takes place in (Piazza Fra Paolo Perria) (along (Via Eleonora d'Arborea) when the square is occupied by the stage for musical events or other impediments), where it is possible to purchase food and other miscellaneous goods.\nEat.\n\"Panada Cuglieritana\" is a typical local dish consisting of a puff pastry calzone baked in the oven and filled with mixed meats, artichokes, fresh broad beans, peas, pitted olives in brine, and dried tomatoes."}
{"id": "218980", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218980", "title": "Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus", "text": "Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (Marathi: \u091b\u0924\u094d\u0930\u092a\u0924\u0940 \u0936\u093f\u0935\u093e\u091c\u0940 \u092e\u0939\u093e\u0930\u093e\u091c \u091f\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093f\u0928\u0938 \"Chhatrapat\u012b Shiv\u0101j\u012b Mah\u0101r\u0101j \u1e6carminas\", IR code: CSMT (mainline) or ST (suburban)), formerly Victoria Terminus (VT), is one of the main disembarking stations for suburban and long-distance trains in Mumbai, India, and a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nCSMT was established in 1853 as Bori Bunder Station. The first commercial railway service began on 16 April 1853 at 3:35PM on its first run between Bori Bunder and Thane. Bori Bunder was a small station, with a single pair of tracks and wooden buildings.\nIn 1877, the original station was demolished to make way for Victoria Terminus (VT) in 1888, taking ten years to build. The building was designed in the High Victorian Gothic style of architecture. It exhibits a fusion of influences from Victorian Italianate Gothic Revival architecture and traditional Indian architecture. The skyline, turrets, pointed arches, and eccentric ground plan is close to traditional Indian palace architecture.\nVT became the headquarters of the Great Indian Peninsular Railway (GIPR), which eventually became the Central Railway (CR).\nIn 1996, when Bombay became Mumbai, VT was also renamed to Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (CST) to honour the first Maratha emperor. It was renamed again in 2016 to add \"Maharaj\", thus becoming Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (CSMT). Despite this, the station continues to be known by the abbreviations \"VT\" and \"CST\" alongside \"CSMT\".\nIn 2008, the station was a target by terrorists during the 26/11 Mumbai attacks.\nArchitecture.\nThe wood carving, tiles, ornamental iron and brass railings, grills for the ticket offices, the balustrades for the grand staircases and other ornaments were the work of students at the Sir Jamsetjee Jeejebhoy School of Art. The station stands as an example of 19th-century railway architectural marvels for its advanced structural and technical solutions. The station was constructed using a high level of engineering both in terms of railway engineering and civil engineering. It is one of the first and is considered one of the finest products of the use of industrial revolution technology merged with the revival of the Gothic Revival style in India. The centrally domed office structure has a 150 m long platform connected to a 500 m long train shed, and its outline provides the skeleton plan for the building. Its dome of dovetailed ribs, built without centring, was considered as a novel achievement of the era. It has a C-shaped plan which is symmetrical on an east-west axis. All the sides of the building are given equal value in the design. It is crowned by a high central dome, which acts as the focal point. The dome is an octagonal ribbed structure with a colossal female figure symbolising progress, holding a torch pointing upwards in her right hand and a spoked wheel in her left hand. The side wings enclose the courtyard, which opens onto the street. The wings are anchored by monumental turrets at each of their four corners, which balance and frame the central dome. The fa\u00e7ades present the appearance of well-proportioned rows of windows and arches. The ornamentation in the form of statuary, bas-reliefs, and friezes is exuberant yet well-controlled. The columns of the entrance gates are crowned with figures of a lion (representing Great Britain) and a tiger (representing India). The main structure is built from a blend of Indian sandstone and limestone, while high-quality Italian marble was used for the key decorative elements.\nInterior.\nThe interior of the building was conceived as a series of large rooms with high ceilings. It is a utilitarian building and has had various changes required by the users, not always sympathetic. The main interiors are also decorated: the ground floor of the North Wing, known as the Star Chamber, which is still used as the booking office, is embellished with Italian marble and polished Indian blue stone. The stone arches are covered with carved foliage and grotesques. Internally, the ceiling of the booking hall was originally painted blue, gold and strong red on a ground of rich blue with gold stars. Its walls were lined with glazed tiles made by Maw &amp; Co of Britain. Outside, there are statues representing commerce, agriculture, engineering and science, with a statue representing progress on the central dome of the station. A statue of Queen Victoria beneath the central dome has been removed.\nServices.\nCSMT is served by both the broad-gauge Central Railway (CR) and the standard-gauge Mumbai Metro. The CR consists of two lines:\nCSMT consists of 18 platforms. The first 7 platforms are exclusively for suburban trains, and the remaining 11 for long-distance trains.\nLocal transportation.\nBy car.\nCSMT is on Dadabhai Naoroji Road at the west and P D'Mello Road at the east. The latter goes further up to Ballard Estate where a lot of corporate offices and Mumbai Port are situated.\nOn foot.\nUnlike most Indian stations, CSMT's footbridge starts right from the opposite side of Dadabhai Naoroji Road, bypassing the road traffic.\nWait.\nAttractions.\nThere are a few attractions within and around the station compound.\nSleep.\nThe nearest hotels are in Mumbai's Fort and SoBo districts.\nNearby.\nThe Fort district of Mumbai is worth a trip if you're stuck at CSMT."}
{"id": "218985", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218985", "title": "S'Archittu", "text": "S'Archittu is a coastal village in the Oristano province. The town takes its name from the natural rock arch (s'archittu in Sardinian) that dominates the cove immediately adjacent to the town. During the summer months, the arch is artificially lit after sunset, making it a popular tourist attraction. \nUnderstand.\nThe arch can be reached on foot via a paved pedestrian path created in 2002 that follows the original route of the previous dirt path. During the summer months, the arch is artificially lit after sunset, making it a popular tourist attraction. Both young and old alike dive from it; in 2001, the rear section of the rock arch hosted the World High Diving Championships. Approximately 15 metres high, it is the result of marine erosion of an ancient cave formed by limestone, marl, and fossil deposits. Due to its uniqueness, it is protected by a decree of the Ministry of the Environment, Land, and Sea, designated \"S'Archittu di Santa Caterina Natural Monument\".\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe SS 292 Bosa-Oristano allows you to reach S'archittu.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 bus line stops in S'Archittu.\nGet around.\nS'Archittu can be easily explored by foot.\nCope.\nPlease note that for safety reasons and to preserve the natural rock arch until 2026, two ordinances are in effect, one municipal and one from the port authority, which prohibit stopping, swimming, and navigation."}
{"id": "218986", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218986", "title": "Santa Caterina di Pittinuri", "text": "Santa Caterina di Pittinuri is a coastal village in Oristano province.\nUnderstand.\nSanta Caterina developed in the post-World War II period around the church of the same name, near one of Sardinia's coastal towers built by the Spanish.\nAt first, it consisted of only very rudimentary houses, almost certainly second homes used as shelter for shepherds. These were later demolished to make way for the first real dwellings, which arose in the 1950s and 1960s and were built without a town plan.\nThe village, although not very large, is divided into two parts by the Santa Caterina stream, which runs perpendicular to the coast and flows into the center of the bay.\nTo the north, we find mostly recently built homes, visible from the linear layout; to the south, however, we find the first homes built around the church.\nThe village has grown significantly thanks to tourism, and the demand for accommodation has led to the need to build even in the vacant areas of the old town.\nPrecisely because of the increase in tourists, it was decided in the late 1980s to build a Service Centre square, with an amphitheater structure, which was renovated in 2012.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe SS 292 Bosa-Oristano allows you to reach Santa Caterina di Pittinuri.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 bus line stops in Santa Caterina di Pittinuri."}
{"id": "218995", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218995", "title": "Torre del Pozzo", "text": "Torre del Pozzo is a coastal village in Oristano province.\nUnderstand.\nThe name derives from the presence of the characteristic Hispanic coastal tower, found in several Sardinian seaside towns, and from a natural well carved by erosion that runs from the promontory to the sea. The corridor cut into the rock reaches the open sea and is accessible to divers.\nThe village is characterized by a unique and uneven architecture, consisting of detached houses of moderate size. The village's first wooden building, \"La Capanna\" (a restaurant), is still in operation today and has retained its characteristic wooden exterior.\nFrom here, Is Arenas beach begins. The first stretch is rocky, almost reaching a kiosk that opens in the summer. From here, it's all sand until the end of the beach.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe SS 292 Bosa-Oristano allows you to reach Torre del Pozzo.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 bus line stops in Torre del Pozzo."}
{"id": "218999", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=218999", "title": "Putzu Idu", "text": "Putzu Idu is a coastal village of Sinis peninsula in Oristano province.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nSS 292 Bosa-Oristano: take Putzu Idu exit.\nBy bus.\n418 ARST bus line from Oristano."}
{"id": "219000", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219000", "title": "Macabebe", "text": "Macabebe is a municipality in the province of Pampanga, Philippines. It is a primarily commercial and fishing town at the edge of the Pampanga River delta. The guide also covers the municipality of Masantol, which is almost surrounded by Macabebe and is mainly accessible through it.\nUnderstand.\nMeaning \"surrounded by waters\" in Kapampangan, a reference to its location on the Pampanga River delta, Macabebe is one of the oldest towns in Pampanga, having hosted a community of indigenous warriors that resisted Spanish explorers during their conquest of Luzon in 1571 and was conquered by the Spanish shortly later. Paradoxically, Macabebe supplied loyal auxiliaries to the Spanish colonizers up to the Philippine Revolution. When the Philippine-American War broke out, the town was burned by Filipino republican forces as part of their scorched-earth retreat from the Americans and partially in revenge for the residents' alleged history of disloyalty. In retaliation, Macabebe supplied auxiliaries and scouts to US forces and contributed in the capture of President Emilio Aguinaldo and the collapse of the First Philippine Republic in 1901. They eventually had their own Scouts unit in the US Army during the American occupation. \nSince the 21st century, a combination of factors such as climate change, rising sea levels, typhoons, high tide, improper land conversion and excessive groundwater extraction have irreversibly submerged numerous barangays and triggered ground subsidence in other areas of town, resulting in an increase of abandoned homes and edifices surrounded by brackish estuarine water. Looking at maps of Macabebe and Masantol, one could be forgiven to think that both towns eerily resemble Venice, with thinly-stretched settlements gathered around spider web-like roads that double as dikes and fishpond fences stretching southwards into Manila Bay, where many barangays are in danger of disappearing altogether.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nThe main entry point into Macabebe and Masantol is a two-lane highway that begins from a junction with MacArthur Highway in downtown Apalit and continues south towards downtown Macabebe and Masantol. There are also secondary roads that connect the towns from Calumpit and Hagonoy in neighboring Bulacan province as well as from Minalin in Pampanga. From Manila, the nearest exits of the North Luzon Expressway (NLEX) to Macabebe via Apalit are San Simon and Pulilan exits.\nBoth Macabebe and Masantol are connected to the provincial capital San Fernando through jeepneys and minibuses. In Macabebe, the main terminal is in front of Macabebe Church, while the terminal in Masantol is at the back of Masantol Church. There is also a daily bus service to Masantol from Cubao in Metro Manila operated by First North Luzon Transit.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of land-based transportation between barangays, while outlying areas may require boats such as the western and southern areas of Macabebe and areas south of downtown Masantol. During times of flooding, specifically the rainy season and heavy thunderstorms coupled with high tide, boats are sometimes necessary even in both downtown Macabebe and Masantol.\nBuy.\nMacabebe and Masantol have lively estuarine and seafood markets on account of their location.\nStay safe.\nAs low-lying towns, both Macabebe and Masantol are regularly subjected to flooding from the Pampanga River during the rainy season, which is exacerbated by typhoons and high tide from Manila Bay.\nStay healthy.\nDue to chronic flooding, medication against leptospirosis (a disease carried by rat urine) is necessary after wading into floodwaters.\nRespect.\nBe respectful when discussing the chronic flooding situation, as many residents have had enough from the long-term disruptions and displacement. Since 2025, protests have erupted in both towns after it emerged that public funds meant for the construction of dikes and flood control measures have been embezzled by corrupt politicians, engineers and contractors.\nConnect.\nMacabebe's postal code is 2018, while that of Masantol is 2017."}
{"id": "219002", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219002", "title": "San Leonardo de Siete Fuentes", "text": "San Leonardo de Siete Fuentes is a mountain village in Oristano province.\nUnderstand.\nThe village takes its name from the church of the same name and from the seven fountains present here.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\n9210 ARST bus line from Macomer, Santu Lussurgiu, Scano di Montiferro, Cuglieri and Bosa.\nGet around.\nThe village can be explored on foot."}
{"id": "219003", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219003", "title": "Orani", "text": "Orani is a town in Bataan, Central Luzon, the Philippines. Situated at the eastern side of the Bataan peninsula, it has natural and historic attractions, including the only Roman Catholic basilica in Bataan. \nUnderstand.\nOrani is known to have existed as early as the 17th century, and became an independent town in 1714. Much of the town was destroyed in a fire in 1938 and was rebuilt, only to be devastated again during World War II in the Battle of Bataan in 1942. The Bataan Death March also passed through the town.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nOrani lies along Roman Superhighway (Route 301), the main six-lane transport artery through eastern Bataan which bypasses downtown Orani. Bataan National Road, a narrow two-lane thoroughfare, passes through the town center. The two highways are connected to each other by capillary roads.\nOrani is connected by minibuses to the provincial capital Balanga. From Metro Manila, Genesis and Victory Liner operate daily bus trips on their way to Balanga and Mariveles.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays. \nStay safe.\nThe lowland areas of Orani are prone to flooding during the rainy season.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2112."}
