{"id": "219028", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219028", "title": "Chiricahua Mountains", "text": "Chiricahua Mountains is in Southeast Arizona.\nUnderstand.\nThe range lies primarily within the Coronado National Forest. Chiricahua Peak, the highest point in the range, rises 9,759 feet (2,975 m) above sea level, about 6,000 feet (1,800 m) above the surrounding valleys. The mountains are named for the Chiricahua Apache, who historically lived in the region.\nThe earliest evidence of humans in the vicinity of the Chiricahua Mountains are Clovis archeological sites such as Double Adobe Site in the Whitewater Draw tributary of Rucker Creek north of Douglas. Subsequently, the Cochise culture another pre-ceramic based culture spanning 3000\u2013200 BCE was defined from sites around the Chiricahua Mountains, including Cave Creek Canyon.\nFollowing the transition to ceramics, artifacts characteristic of both Mogollon culture and its local variants, the Mimbres culture, are found. These relics span the period from 150 BCE \u2013 1450. The influx of other indigenous peoples, such as the Chiricahua Apaches, including the leaders Cochise and Geronimo occupied the area until forced removal in the late 19th century."}
{"id": "219030", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219030", "title": "Dragoon Mountains", "text": "The Dragoon Mountains are in Southeast Arizona. With very rugged terrain, the Dragoons provided refuge for the Chiricahua Apaches during the Apache Wars of the 19th century, and offer excellent hiking, rock climbing, horseback riding, and camping opportunities. The area is managed by the Coronado National Forest; maps and hiking information can be obtained from the Douglas Ranger District office.\nUnderstand.\nThey are a range of mountains about 25 mi (40 km) long, running on an axis extending south-south east through Willcox. \nMount Glenn (7,520 ft; 2,290 m) is the highest point in the range. The Little Dragoon Mountains are the continuation of the Dragoon Mountains north of Texas Canyon.\nFossilized horn coral has been discovered on exposed limestone rock. Ancient pictographs and matate dating to AD 1200 exist in the area and are attributed to the Mogollon people. The Apache people resided in this area beginning in the 15th century.\nThe warrior Cochise and his army defeated a small force of Confederate soldiers here at the First Battle of Dragoon Springs but was defeated at the Second Battle of Dragoon Springs a few days later. Cochise Stronghold Memorial Park lies near Mount Glenn on the eastern slope of the range and the historic town of Tombstone can be found at the southwestern portion of the range. There are also several ghost towns in the Dragoon Mountains including Gleeson and Courtland."}
{"id": "219031", "revid": "631289", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219031", "title": "Gascon phrasebook", "text": "Gascon (\"Gascon [gas'ku\u014b]\") is one of several dialects of Occitan spoken by a minority of people in southern France and northern Spain.\nIt is spoken in France roughly between the Garonne river and the Pyrenees mountains, and in Spain in the Val d'Aran valley where it is known as Aran\u00e9s. It used to be spoken as an educated language in the towns of the Basque country (Bayonne, Pamplona, etc) which leads to some disputes on attributions of some localities to Gascogne or Basque country. The reason for this language interference is due to the fact that Gascon is the result of the evolution of latin as adopted by proto-basque populations (called 'Aquitans' by Julius Caesar). Basque and Gascon share a number of features despite a very different lexical basis. Latin was adopted earlier in cities, this is why cities in the heart of the Basque country used to speak Gascon.\nPhrase list.\n\"Some in this still need to be translated. If you know anything about this language, you can by and.\""}
