{"id": "219058", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219058", "title": "Naha-Metzabok Biosphere Reserve", "text": "Naha-Metzabok Biosphere Reserve is a protected natural area in the Lacandon jungle in the southern state of Chiapas in Mexico. It is a designated UNESCO Biosphere Reserve that preserves tropical jungle habitats and cultural sites of the Lacandon Maya civilization.\nUnderstand.\nThe biosphere reserve covers an area of 43,362 hectares, in two distinct areas: \"Naha\" and \"Metzabok\". The reserve is managed by Mexico's National Commission of Protected Natural Areas (CONANP) in cooperation with local Lacondon indigenous communities. \nMetzabok is the name of the Lacandon Mayan rain God.\nHistory.\nThe reserve was established by UNESCO in 1998 to protect cloud forests and pine-oak woodlands in the Lacandon jungle and to preserve 24 crystal clear karst lakes formed by hydrologic erosion of the limestone subsurface. It is also home to several Lacandon Maya indigenous communities. Archaeological sites in the reserve include ancient cave dwellings with rock drawings that are more than 1000 years old.\nLandscape.\nMost of the terrain is typical of the \"Selva Lacandona\": dense jungle with areas that are commonly covered by dense clouds which provide moisture for plant life. Towering trees support a dense canopy that provides shade for the cool surface areas. The reserve is well-known for its 24 tranquil lakes set deep in the jungle. Some of the lakes feature a smooth, black surface that reflects clouds or nearby trees. \nFlora and fauna.\nHalf of all bird species known to inhabit Mexico can be spotted in the Lacandon jungles of Chiapas and this preserve offers some prime opportunities for Birdwatching. These include a variety of birds of prey and carrion species, like the white hawk, black hawk, and king vulture. The canopy is home to several brilliantly colored birds including the keel-billed toucan, resplendent quetzal, and great curassow.\nA number of reptiles and amphibians live in the park, particularly in or near lakes, rivers, or other wetlands. Some of the lakes are inhabited by crocodiles and freshwater turtles while more than 30 kinds of snake have been spotted in the jungle, including some venomous species like the bushmaster and the fer-de-lance, though its more likely that you'll see one of the many non-venomous species, like the small \"ocotera snake\", that commonly burrows under leaves or dirt piles. \nThe most commonly seen mammals are monkeys, swinging from branches and vines in the ranforest canopy. Bustling about on the ground are anteaters and other smaller animals. The park is a biological corridor for roaming cats, who travel great distances. Jungle cats that might be seen in the reserve include jaguars, ocelots, and jaguarundi. \nMore information about these animals is in Central and South American wildlife. \nPlant life varies depending on your location and its associated ecosystem. The rainforest and its canopy consist of old, enormous trees, including the big-leaf mahogony, chicle, and the ram\u00f3n tree. At the upper reaches of hills, pine, oak, and alder make up the forest vegetation. In the wetlands and rainforest floor you find epiphytes that can absorb moisture from the cloud cover along with orchids, bromeliads, and bright green mosses. \nClimate.\nThe jungles of southern Mexico are a hot tropical climate with high humidity and high rainfall all year. Because it is also in the Sierra Madre Sur mountain range, highland areas can be cooler and be less humid. Temperatures average about 30\u00b0C with the warmest months being April and May, when temperatures can reach 35\u00b0C, accompanied by near 100% humidity. During the coolest months (December and January) the temperature is closer to 25\u00b0C. June to October is the \"rainy season\" when downpours (including torrential rains) can be expected. The wettest month is September with a daily average rainfall of about 11 mm. Visiting the reserve in September will likely be a wet and muddy experience, but the vegetation is lush and the lakes are full and rivers and streams rushing.\nGet in.\nThe Naha-Metzabok reserve is about 95 km southeast of Palenque. The closest airport with commercial service is in Villahermosa. The best way to get to the park is to rent a car as it is in a remote area without major towns or bus stations.\nFees and permits.\nThe mandatory entrance fee charged by CONANP is M$95 per person, per day. Senior citizens and children under 12 can enter the park free of charge.\nAdditional fees paid to the Lacandon Maya communities include nightly camping fees of M$100 to M$200, fees to rent canoes range from M$300 to M$600, and fees to hire local Maya guides (required for some areas or types of activity) will vary and are negotiated directly with the guide but generally start at about M$200.\nGet around.\nHiking is the best way to get around most of the park though canoes are commonly used on the lakes, many of which are interconnected and can be used to travel long distances. You can also arrange one-way trips to cross large lakes by canoes and begin or continue your hike on the opposite shore.\nBuy, eat and drink.\nThere are no stores or restaurants in or near the park. Bring what you will need and pack out any trash when you leave.\nSleep.\nCamping is allowed in designated camping areas and in backcountry areas. Campers are required to register at the park entrances and to pay daily camping fees. Campsites are primitive tent-only sites with composting toilet restrooms, small palapa-style shelters, and little else. Leave-no-trace practices are required in backcountry areas including packing out any trash that you bring into the park.\nThe designated camping areas are in the Lacandon Maya communities, not on public park land. Communities charge camping fees that vary considerably (but are never expensive)"}
{"id": "219060", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219060", "title": "Okushiri Island", "text": "Okushiri Island (\u5965\u5c3b\u5cf6 \"Okushiri-t\u014d\") is an island off the southwest coast of Hokkaido, Japan.\nUnderstand.\nOkushiri is a rugged, deeply forested remote island famous for its crystal-clear \"Okushiri Blue\" waters and striking coastal rock formations. The name originates from the Ainu \"Ikusunsir\", \"Other Side Island\", somewhat unfortunately phonetically rendered with Chinese characters meaning \"Deep Butt\".\nHistory.\nThe island was devastated by a massive earthquake in 1993 that triggered a tsunami, landslides and fires, killing 198 people. The island has since been completely rebuilt with extensive coastal defenses, but it never really recovered and the population has continued to shrink, with only around 3,000 people left as of 2025.\nClimate.\nThe island experiences a humid continental climate with cool summer ocean breezes and heavy snowfall during the winter months.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\n () is served by JAL subsidiary Hokkaido Air System (HAC) with flights from Hakodate and Sapporo-Okadama.\nBy ferry.\nHeartland Ferry runs a daily ferry from Esashi (2 hours 20 minutes, \u00a53,460 in 2nd class) to.\nGet around.\nThe island's main population centers are Aonae (\u9752\u82d7), at the southern tip of the island, and Okushiri Town, near the port on the east coast.\nOkushiri Town operates an infrequent bus service. There are two routes from Okushiri Port, one south towards Aonae and back up the west coast to Kamuiwaki Onsen, the other north towards Cape Inaho. Fares are a flat \u00a5200.\nRenting a car or a bicycle is highly recommended to fully explore the island's coastal roads. Rentals are available at the airport and ferry terminal.\nDo.\nRent a sea kayak or stand-up paddleboard to explore the exceptionally clear coastal waters.\nEat.\nThe island is renowned throughout Japan for its incredibly fresh seafood, particularly its sweet sea urchin (uni) and crunchy Ezo abalone.\nDrink.\nPick up a bottle of unique wine from the Okushiri Winery.\nSleep.\nLodging.\nSeveral family-run minshuku and small ryokan are clustered around the main ferry port and the southern Aonae area.\nCamping.\nBudget travelers can take advantage of free, basic campsites located at coastal spots like Kitaoimisaki Park.\nBackcountry.\nAll trails on the island, including the hike up Mt. Tamashima, are short enough for day trips, making backcountry camping unnecessary.\nStay safe.\nDue to the region's seismic history, always familiarize yourself with the well-marked tsunami evacuation routes located throughout the island's coastal areas.\nGo next.\nThis is about as deep as it gets in Japan, the only way out is back to the mainland."}
{"id": "219070", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219070", "title": "Sunndal", "text": "Sunndal is a municipality in M\u00f8re og Romsdal county in Norway.\nIn the southern part of the municipality lies the Dovrefjell\u2013Sunndalsfjella National Park, home to a plentiful amount of impressive muskox. In the northern part is the Trollheimen and Innerdalen landscape protection areas.\nUnderstand.\nThe administrative centre of the municipality is the village of Sunndals\u00f8ra. Other villages include Gj\u00f8ra, Gr\u00f8a, Holssanden, Jordalsgrenda, Romfo, \u00c5lvund, \u00c5lvundeidet, and \u00d8ksendal.\nGet in and around.\nBy car.\nCounty road 62 runs along the west side of the fjord to Sunndals\u00f8ra from Molde. National road 70 comes down Sunndalen from Oppdal, and continues on the east side of Sunndalsfjorden in the direction of Kristiansund."}
{"id": "219074", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219074", "title": "Latin for travellers", "text": "Latin (\"lingua lat\u012bna\"), the language of ancient Rome, has greatly influenced many languages. The Romance languages (chiefly French, Spanish, Portuguese, Italian, Catalan and Romanian) are direct descendants of Latin, and Latin has had some influence on all the other modern European languages. Most European languages use the Latin alphabet, and even those that don't have inherited some Latin words.\nUnderstand.\nLatin was the sole liturgical language in the Roman Catholic Church until the second half of the 20th century and is still sometimes used. It is still an official language of the Holy See and Vatican City, and is used for official announcements and documents \u2013 and it is the language of much of older Christian music.\nLatin was a \"lingua franca\" for educated people across Europe throughout the Middle Ages and for much of the post-Renaissance period. In English and many other languages, much of the terminology in law, medicine and other sciences is derived from Latin. Often there are two ways to say something; where many people would use words from Anglo-Saxon like \"He broke his leg\", a doctor might use Latin-derived terms as in \"He fractured his tibia\".\nEven though Latin is still widely taught in high schools and universities, the language is rarely used to communicate person to person. However, travellers often encounter \"written\" Latin on inscriptions at historic sites, tombstones and old monuments. Latin abbreviations are also commonly used in university degrees. Not only that, but scientific names of organisms are written in Latin, so knowing Latin can be useful in botanical and wildlife tourism.\nGrammar.\nSuffixes (called declensions) are used extensively to specify the grammatical use of nouns, pronouns and adjectives, and others (called conjugations) show the grammatical use of verbs. They specify grammatical gender (masculine, feminine or neuter), number, and case (there are seven of them) in adjectives, nouns, and pronouns, and person, number, tense, voice, mood and aspect of verbs. Word order is relatively free, especially in poetry. Furthermore, Classical Latin did not separate words from each other or have punctuation. The result can therefore be ambiguous, so that you may need to understand the context in order to decode a phrase. However, Latin from the medieval period and later typically does put spaces or other demarcations between words and use punctuation.\nWhile Latin declensions and conjugations are much more complex than that of English, modern Latin use is often limited to set phrases, which can be recognised as such.\nPronunciation and spelling.\nAfter the fall of the Roman Empire, Latin was mostly a written language of people who acquired it in adulthood or schooling. This led to it developing local pronunciations in school and ordinary usage. Those pronunciations diverge \u2013 sometimes widely \u2013 from the pronunciation in Roman times.\nThere are many pronunciation standards for Latin today. The traditional English pronunciation of Latin, used by English speakers until the early 20th century, is nowadays rejected by purists, but still prevalent in the fields of biology, law, and medicine, and survives to some extent in the Anglican Church, as well as the universities of Oxford and Cambridge for ceremonial purposes. Classical Latin is a reconstruction by linguists of how Latin was most likely to have been spoken by upper class Romans around the time of Julius Caesar and Augustus. Ecclesiastical Latin is the pronunciation officially used by the Roman Catholic Church, based on Italian pronunciation.\nThe Latin alphabet had only 23 letters during Roman times, lacking the letters J, U and W. These letters originated in medieval times, with J originating as a variant of I, and U and W originating as variants of V. The letter C was originally used to represent both the C and G sounds, but a separate letter G was created around the 3rd century B.C. by modifying the letter C so the two sounds could be distinguished in writing.\nClassical Latin distinguished between long and short vowels. In modern texts for Latin learners, a macron (e.g. \"\u0101\") indicates that the vowel is supposed to be long. However, vowel length was often not indicated at all in Roman-era texts, and those that did so used the apex (a long acute accent) instead of the macron. This distinction in vowel length has largely been lost in Ecclesiastical Latin.\nPhrase list.\nLatin is commonly used on inscriptions at historic sites, tombstones and pre-modern monuments, especially in the West, and also in many churches and sacred works of Christian art. Common Latin expressions include:\n Lamb of God, referring to Jesus, whose sacrifice takes away the sins of the world (according to Christianity).\n In the year of the Lord. Refers to the common era, after the birth of Jesus.\n Blessed\n Chair. In modern Christian contexts, refers to the bishop's throne in a cathedral. In an academic context it signifies an official professorial post, or a specialized field of study.\n I believe in one God\n Grant us peace.\n \"Everyone out.\" Used to order all non-Cardinals to leave the Sistine Chapel before a Papal Conclave commences.\n Glory to God in the highest\n \"We have a Pope.\" Used to announce the election of a new Pope.\n Have mercy on us.\n \"In the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit\", said by Catholics when making the sign of the cross\n Shout joyfully for God\n Pray for us. Seen in many inscriptions on monuments in churches and the like.\n Our father. The name of a very common prayer, where it refers to God.\n Greatest priest. Used to refer to the Pope in his role as the supreme leader of the Roman Catholic Church, and formerly to the chief high priest of pre-Christian Rome.\n First among equals. Often used to describe the role of the Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople as the leader of the Eastern Orthodox churches, the Archbishop of Canterbury as the leading clergyman of the Anglican Church or archbishops in general among bishops: they have a leading role, but little formal power above that of their peers. Also used to describe similar roles elsewhere, e.g., of a dean among professors of the faculty.\n The King or Queen. Refers to the monarch or the government of a monarchy,\n May he/she rest in peace. Used as a blessing that the deceased is at peace.\n Grant them eternal rest. On gravestones you might encounter the singular form \"Requiem aeternam d\u014dna e\u012b\".\n Holy\n Empty seat. Today used to describe a period when a bishop or archbishop position (most commonly that of the Pope, who is Bishop of Rome) is vacant. \n Thee, Oh God, we praise\n Peace. Often used to indicate periods of relative peace brought about by submission to the dominance of a single hegemon (e.g. \"P\u0101x R\u014dm\u0101na\", \"P\u0101x Britannica\", \"P\u0101x Americ\u0101na\").\n For the fatherland. In patriotic contexts, including on memorials for soldiers perished in wars.\n \"I came, I saw, I conquered\", popularly attributed to Julius Caesar\n \"Wine is life\", used as a motto by many wine brands\n By the fact itself\n My fault\n And others\n Person not acceptable\n Take this. Used on prescriptions.\n For the sake of an example\n And the other things\n That is\n Something for something.\n In the year of the founding of the city. Refers to Rome's legendary establishment by Romulus.\n Before \"G\" was recognized as a separate letter, the letter \"C\" was used for that sound instead. Gaius was a common male personal name in Ancient Rome.\n Another common male personal name in Ancient Rome.\n Here is buried.\n The first day of a month, used in dates within the previous month.\n The senate and people of Rome. It was commonly used to identify the citizens of the Roman Empire, and is still used by the municipal government of modern Rome.\nPatriotism and municipal pride.\nSome countries, regions, cities and institutions (schools, societies, sports clubs etc.) have adopted Latin phrases as their mottos, after Rome's example of SPQR. You will likely spot these on coats of arms, seals, war memorials, banknotes, coins, or even street furniture such as lampposts and bins. A few examples:\n \"From sea to sea\" - the Canadian national motto\n \u201cWell-being through harmony\u201d - motto of Montreal\n \"Out of many, one\" - a traditional motto of the United States\n \"No-one provokes me with impunity\" - Scotland's national motto\n \"Ever upward\" - state motto of New York\n \"Oh Lord, guide us\" - motto of the City of London\n \"City in a Garden.\" - motto of Chicago\n \"United so that they may benefit all.\" - motto of Adelaide\nLatin as a \"neutral\" language.\nIn a multilingual or international context, especially in Europe, Latin is sometimes used to avoid the appearance of favouring one language or country over another. As a \"dead\" language with no more native speakers but which many Europeans can claim partial heritage from, Latin is useful when neutrality or unity are needed:\n The Swiss Confederation. Having a Latin official name avoids favouring any of the four official languages of Switzerland. You'll most commonly see the letters CH on Swiss-registered vehicles' licence plates abroad and in the URLs of Swiss websites. Swiss postage stamps bear the Latin name \"Helvetia\".\n The country's English name comes directly from Latin. \"Belgium\" is used on the logos and kits of the Belgian national sports teams, rather than Dutch \"Belgi\u00eb\" or French \"Belgique\", as language politics in the country can be divisive.\nThe European Union has 27 official languages, and occasionally uses Latin where cross-border clarity demands it, but use of one of the widely-understood official languages would seem prejudicial:\n \"United in diversity\", the EU's motto\n \"European Company\", for a commercial entity registered under EU corporate law. Examples include Airbus SE and Allianz SE.\nSome EU institution domain names also use Latin: https://consilium.europa.eu/ leads to the European Council's website, while https://curia.europa.eu/ is the European Court of Justice.\nNames of organisms.\nTaxonomy (Ancient Greek for \"method of arrangement\") is the science of naming and grouping biological organisms, traditionally written in Latin but using a lot of Greek terms; the study of these, from sources such as the Wikipedia list of Latin and Greek words commonly used in systematic names, will benefit the science-minded traveller.\nTaxonomy employs binomial nomenclature. Each species is given a name that consists of two parts: the generic name and the specific name. The generic name identifies the genus under which the species belongs, whereas the specific name distinguishes the species within the genus. For example:\n Yarrow. Mentioned often in the \"Illiad\" as used as a blood-staunching balm by protagonist Achilles, hence \"Achillea\"; \"millefolium\" means \"of a thousand leaves\".\n Horse. Both the genus name \"Equus\" and the specifying \"caballus\" part refer to horses in Latin.\n Modern humans. \"Homo\" means man in Latin, and the \"sapiens\" part, which indicates the modern species under it, means discerning or wise.\n Domestic apple. \"Malus\" means apple tree and \"domestica\" means domestic (of the house / \"domus\").\n Lettuce. \"Lactuca\" is the vegetable's Roman name, after its white, \"milky\" (\"lactea\") sap. \"Sativa\" means \"cultivated\".\n House sparrow. \"Passer\" is the bird's Roman name; it is the type species (\"species typica\") of its genus, which is the type genus (\"genus typica\") of its family \"Passeridae\", the \"familia typica\" of the enormous order \"Passeriformes\", which contains more than half of all bird species.\n Date palm. \"Phoenix\" derives from the Greek word for \"date palm\", \"phoinik\u00f3s\"; \"dactylifera\" means \"date-bearing\", from \"dactyl\" (Greek for \"finger\" = date) + \"foros\", \"to bear\".\n Pomegranate. \"Punica\" means \"from Carthage\" (its Roman name was \"malus punica\"); \"granatum\" means \"grainy\", i. e. \"seeded\". \n Brown bear. The Latin \"ursus\" and Ancient Greek \"arctos\" both mean \"bear\".\nTo indicate the individual subspecies within a species, a \"subspecific name\" is added after the binomial name of the parent species. This is called trinomial nomenclature. For example:\n Panama yellow-headed parrot. \"Amazona\" reflects the bird's fame as a symbol of the Amazon Jungle; \"ochrocephala\" is Greek for \"yellow-headed\" and \"panamensis\" means \"from Panama\".\n Dog. This is an obsolete classification, as a subspecies of \"Canis lupus\" the wolf. Nowadays, \"Canis familiaris\" is the more prevalent name.\n Western lowland gorillas. The first \"Gorilla\" indicates the gorilla genus, the second \"gorilla\" indicates the lowland gorilla species, and the third \"gorilla\" indicates the particular subspecies under it. Quite often, type species have such seemingly redundant names.\nThe whole scientific hierarchy of species is based on Latinised names. For example, \"Panthera pardus\" (the leopard) and \"Panthera onca\" (the jaguar) belong to the genus \"Panthera\", subfamily Pantherinae, family Felidae (from \"f\u0113l\u0113s\", \"cat\"), suborder Feliformia, order Carnivora (from \"carnivorus\", \"flesh-eating\"), infraclass Placentalia (from \"placenta uter\u012b\", \"placenta\"), class Mammalia (from \"mamma\", \"breast\"), phylum Chordata (from \"corda\", \"cord\"), and finally kingdom Animalia (from \"animal\", \"animal\", \"living\")."}
{"id": "219104", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219104", "title": "Saraikela", "text": "Saraikela or Seraikella (Hindi: \u0938\u0930\u093e\u0908\u0915\u0947\u0932\u093e \"Sar\u0101ikel\u0101\") in South Jharkhand served as the capital of the Singh Deo dynasty. The town is known for the Saraikela version of the famous Chhau dance, a unique style of masked tribal dance.\nUnderstand.\nThe town was founded in 1620 by Kumar Bikram Singh I 0f Sing Deo dynasty. The town still hosts the royal palace known as Seraikela Rajbari. The town is the birthplace of the Seraikela style of Chhau, an internationally acclaimed semi-classical dance form combining martial, tribal, and folk traditions. Nearby is the Chandil Dam, on the Subarnarekha River, known for its scenic beauty.\nGet around.\nAutos are widely available. There are no fixed rate. Fix up a rate before you start the tour and bargain. For places outside the town, a car is recommended. Hotels can arrange for cars."}
{"id": "219112", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219112", "title": "New Delhi/Cantonment", "text": "Delhi Cantonment, or Delhi Cantt for short, is the military enclave of New Delhi. It is home to the Indira Gandhi International Airport, formerly Palam Air Force Station."}
{"id": "219121", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219121", "title": "Los Tuxtlas Biosphere Reserve", "text": "Los Tuxtlas Biosphere Reserve is a protected natural area along the Gulf of Mexico coast in Veracruz, Mexico. The reserve protects areas of coastal mangrove swamp but is best known for its tropical forest ecosystem on the slopes of seven volcanoes within the park. The mountainous area includes highland lakes that are a winter haven for migratory ducks and geese.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a large reserve covering an area of more than 155,000 hectares. \nHistory.\nThe reserve was established by presidential decree in 1998. It was declared a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve in 2006 and is administered for the federal government by the National Commission of Natural Protected Areas (CONANP), \nThe reserve was established following a wake-up call sounded by environmental researchers at UNAM (Mexico's largest university), who became alarmed at the destruction of the rain forests for logging and cattle ranching. Several of the volcanic peaks had been completely deforested since the 1960s. Illegal foresting remains a problem, but growing awareness of the value of ecotourism is helping to turn local opinion against irresponsible natural exploitation.\nThe Olmec past.\nThe Olmec indigenous culture is known for their enormous sculptures of rounded heads created from 1500 BC to 900 AD. Archaeologists have determined that the Olmecs operated quarries in the areas of today's biosphere reserve. They cut massive blocks of basalt from the base of the Cerro El Vig\u00eda volcano, then loaded the stone onto rafts where they could be floated down the Papaloapan river to Tres Zapotes where where they would be carved. \nTravelers today can visit Tres Zapotes where some of the oldest Olmec heads are displayed (including the first ever discovered). A site museum explains the story of Olmec civilization and its role as the \"mother civilization\" of Mesoamerica. \nLandscape.\nThe park is large with various different landscapes, including sandy beaches or steep rocky cliffs on the shores of the Gulf of Mexico. Most of the park is jungle covered volcanic peaks with steep hillsides. Numerous mountains streams and rivers flow through the park with several waterfalls from as low as a few meters in height to dramatic falls that plunge over 50 meters. Lake Catemaco is a large freshwater lake with dense jungles along most of its shorelines but increasingly cleared land near the town of Catemaco.\nFlora and fauna.\nLos Tuxtlas is considered a biological hotspot because of the very high numbers of plant and animal species found here. It is an area where you might encounter animals that are common to North America as well as animals more commonly found in South America. The reserve boasts some 2,600 plant species and more than 800 vertebrates. Interestingly, more than 300 species of ant have been identified in the reserve. \nBirdwatchers will enjoy trying to spot all of 560 species of birds. Some are common residents of the tropics, like the toucans, eagles, and vultures, but there are hundreds of songbirds that can only be spotted during winter months. \nMore than 140 mammal species live in the reserve. Some of the animals are most commonly found in South America, but they're perfectly happy in this part of Mexico. These include \"Tayras\", and \"Kinkajous\", both of which are small animals about the size of a raccoon or a coatamundi. Up in the jungle canopy, you can find large numbers of primates, like the mantled howler monkey, having a swinging time. \nThe park is filled with dense vegetation with huge kapok trees supporting the canopy. Down below are numerous mosses, epiphytes and colorful blooms of Bernoullia flammea, spiky pochote, and Ficus aurea (also known as golden fig trees).\nClimate.\nLos Tuxtlas has a tropical climate with very warm but stable temperatures and high humidity. Temperatures are typically between 25\u00b0C and 30\u00b0C. Rain is frequent at almost any time of year and higher altitudes receive almost twice as much rainfall as lowlands. The wettest months are September to November when heavy tropical storms bring torrential downpours. March to May are the driest months and are a good time to visit.\nGet in.\nThe nearest city with commercial airline flights is in Veracruz, about 3-1/2 hours north by car or bus. \nFrom Veracruz, frequent bus service is available to the nearby town of Catemaco, which is an excellent base from which to explore the various parts of the reserve. ADO operates four buses daily between Veracruz and Catemaco. AU operates buses to Catemaco from Mexico City's TAPO bus terminal and from Puebla's CAPU bus terminal. \"See Bus travel in Mexico.\"\nIn Catemaco, you can hire a local guide who can take you into remote parts of the reserve and who knows the backcountry trails and the different access points into the reserve.\nFees and permits.\nDaily admission fees to the reserve cost M$95 per person. Children under 12, senior citizens and disabled persons are admitted free. Discounted admission is offered to students and teachers.\nGet around.\nYou'll probably end up doing a lot of hiking into the jungles, but it's a big reserve and walking long distances in sweltering weather gets tired fast. You will probably use Catemaco or San Andr\u00e9s Tuxtla as your base and then make day trips to different points of interest in the park. The two main means of getting around larger distances are by truck (\"mixto\") or by boat. Boats can be arranged in Catemaco to take you across the lake to various trailheads, as well as to points of interest like Monkey Island. The \"mixto\" is kind of like a jungle version of a \"combi\"\u02d0 it carries passengers and cargo along dirt roads (usually mud roads) to remote villages and coastal communities. The mixto is typically a flatbed truck with benches along the sides in back and a canvas awning to protect passengers from either the beating sun or the beating torrential rains. In Catemaco, you can find mixtos as the marketplace, on Calle Carranza, and on Carretera a Playa Azul at the edge of town. Use the location where the mixtos are heading in the direction you want to go. Fares are cheap. Schedules are erratic.\nDo.\nVolcanoes.\nThe reserve has 7 volcanoes within its boundaries. None of these have lakes in their calderas, but have scenic overlooks which you can reach via a steep, rocky hike to the summit. Many of the trails are poorly marked and are frequently overgrown by the encroaching jungle vegetation. A local guide is needed to safely hike up the volcanoes. \nBuy.\nThere are several small villages within the biosphere reserve and some larger towns adjacent or nearby. These typically have some small stores where you can buy supplies as well as restaurants and hotels. Towns include Catemaco, San Andr\u00e9s Tuxtla, and Santiago Tuxtla. Catemaco has the best tourism infrastructure and tour guides can be hired to take you to remote sights within the preserve.\nSleep.\nNearby towns include Catemaco, San Andr\u00e9s Tuxtla, and Santiago Tuxtla, all of which have hotels or hostels where you can find inexpensive lodging. The seaside village of Montepio has a number of comfortable places to stay and is close to several waterfalls.\nBackcountry.\nBackcountry camping is allowed. Inquire with local tour guides or the CONANP ranger stations."}
{"id": "219122", "revid": "2196717", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219122", "title": "Philipsburg (Montana)", "text": "Philipsburg is a town of about 800 people (2020) in southwestern Montana, originally formed around an ore smelter in 1867. The surrounding county is home to more than 24 ghost towns. Since the 1990s, numerous buildings in the town's historic district have been restored and filled with rock and jewelry shops, art galleries and candy stores.\nGet in.\nState Route 1 (MT-1) passes north-south along the west edge of Philipsburg, between Drummond and Anaconda.\nState Route 38 (MT-38) is a scenic primitive seasonal road over Skalkaho Pass to Skalkaho Falls and Hamilton. No RVs or trailers. There are no guardrails on this narrow, mostly gravel mountain road.\nGet around.\nMost businesses are on Broadway St."}
{"id": "219151", "revid": "50919", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219151", "title": "Lothal", "text": "Lothal in Central Gujarat is one of India's Indus Valley sites.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nDating back to around 2400 BCE, Lothal is considered as one of the world's earliest known artificial dockyards, which connected the city to an ancient course of the Sabarmati River and trade routes across the Arabian Sea to Mesopotamia and Persia. Discovered in 1954 and excavated by the Archaeological Survey of India between 1955 and 1960, it was a thriving trade hub during the Harappan era.\nGet around.\nThe entire archaeological site and museum complex is best explored on foot. Wear comfortable walking shoes, carry a hat or umbrella, and stay hydrated, as there is limited shade across the excavated ruins. The new National Maritime Heritage Complex is bit far away so should be reached by a taxi or a rickshaw.\nEat.\nDining options near the archaeological complex are very limited. Carry your own snacks and ample drinking water, especially if visiting during warmer months."}
{"id": "219155", "revid": "2298067", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219155", "title": "Station codes", "text": "The names of train and bus stations are often abbreviated by codes that are only a few characters in length. The station codes are intended to be unique within a given country, and knowing them can be useful for booking.\nUnderstand.\nAlthough in many cases you can get by with knowing only the station name, it's nonetheless useful to know the code of the station you are travelling to. Common reasons include:\nUsing the codes can make entering destinations on transportation websites quicker.\nSome stations are known by their code and referenced by local public transport with them, and thus the codes can be useful when communicating with taxi drivers. They may also appear in diverse contexts as a colloquial nickname for the city which the station serves.\nNational codes.\nNational rail carriers (such as Amtrak) have been known to assign their own non-standard codes to train stations on their own lines.\nIn some cases, a single station has different codes for different lines; Mumbai's Dadar Station, for instance, uses DR for the Central Line \u00a0Central\u00a0 and DDR for the Western Line \u00a0Western\u00a0, and the Seoul railway station uses 133 for Line 1 services, 426 for Line 4 services, P313 and 0001 for Korail services (commuter and intercity, respectively), A01 for AREX services and X106 for GTX-A services. In other cases, a single station has different codes for different services; London St Pancras International, for instance, uses STP for domestic and SPX for international services.\nIndia.\nThe Indian Railways (IR) uses station codes whose length varies from one to four letters. Stations with one-letter code include Gondia (G), Jalna (J), Raipur (R), Srirangapatna (S) and Yeliyur (Y).\nStation codes are commonly used not only in booking but also in the short names of railway lines and services. For example, the Howrah\u2013New Jalpaiguri line is often called the \"NJP line\" after the New Jalpaiguri station, and one can find various Visakhapatnam-bound trains named \"VSKP Foobar Exp\".\nWhile IR station codes rarely change, often preserving the stations' former names (like MAS for Chennai Central, formerly Madras Central), they do occasionally change more than once. For example, the station code of Mumbai's Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus was changed from VT to CSTM in 1996 when it was renamed from \"Victoria Terminus\" to \"Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus\". It was changed again to CSMT in 2018 after adding \"Maharaj\" to the station's name.\nSouth Korea.\nSouth Korea's Korail, as well as metro systems in cities like Seoul, use purely numeric station codes. Since the Korean language uses Hangul, most station codes are all-numeric. For example, Korail's Seoul station has 0001 as a station code for ticketing, while Aheyon has a station code of 242.\nIATA.\nAlthough the International Air Transport Association generally assigns three-letter airport codes, they also assign three-letter codes for non-aviation facilities, including train stations, bus stations and ferry terminals. Such stations are generally used in rail air alliances or code-sharing arrangements between airlines and public transport operators. By assigning stations an IATA code, passengers on trips involving those stations can be ticketed all the way through the journey, including being checked straight through to their final destination, without the bother of having to claim their baggage and check-in again when changing between the surface and air portions of a trip. At other places, passengers have to carry their baggage onto the train, but need no separate train booking process.\nOther codes.\nOther identifiers include:"}
{"id": "219164", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219164", "title": "Roero", "text": "Roero is a hilly wine region in the southeast of Piedmont, Italy, on the north (left) bank of the river Tanaro, mostly in the province of Cuneo between Alba and Asti. With the neighbouring Langhe and Monferrato it forms the UNESCO World Heritage Site \"Vineyard Landscape of Piedmont: Langhe-Roero and Monferrato\".\nThe Roero is the quietest and least-visited corner of the three, and the most varied: its sandy, fossil-rich soils give lighter, fragrant wines \u2014 above all the white Roero Arneis \u2014 and the hills are broken by the Rocche, dramatic eroded sandstone cliffs cloaked in woodland. Between the vines the region also grows peaches, strawberries and hazelnuts.\nUnderstand.\nThe Roero is named after the Roero (Roreto) family, who held these lands in the Middle Ages and left castles at Guarene, Govone, Monteu Roero and elsewhere. Geologically it is younger and sandier than the Langhe across the river, which is why its reds are lighter and its whites \u2014 Arneis above all \u2014 so distinctive.\nIt is thoroughly rural and sees few foreign tourists, which is much of its charm. Autumn (harvest and truffle season) and late spring are the best times to visit; the peach and strawberry harvests fill the summer markets.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Turin Caselle ('), about an hour away by car. Milano Malpensa Airport (') and Genoa () are also within reach for international connections.\nBy train.\nBra and Alba are the rail gateways, reached from Turin via the regional lines; from either it is a short drive into the hills. There is no rail line through the heart of the Roero itself.\nBy car.\nEasiest by far. The region sits just off the Alba\u2013Asti road (SP7) and is reached from the A6 (Turin\u2013Savona, exit Marene/Bra) or the A33/Alba. The hill roads between villages are narrow and winding.\nGet around.\nA car is all but essential \u2014 villages are close together but public transport between them is minimal. The quiet roads and the Rocche paths are excellent for cycling, e-biking and walking; local tourist offices signpost vineyard and Rocche routes.\nEat.\nRoero cooking is shared with the wider Langhe\u2013Monferrato table: tajarin and agnolotti pasta, carne cruda (raw veal), bagna cauda in the cold months, and white truffle in autumn. The region's own produce \u2014 peaches, strawberries, hazelnuts and honey \u2014 turns up in markets and desserts, and nearby Bra gives its name to a well-known sausage and cheese.\nDrink.\nThe Roero's signature wine is Roero Arneis DOCG, a fragrant dry white from the native Arneis grape \u2014 one of Piedmont's few notable whites. The red Roero DOCG is made from Nebbiolo and is lighter and earlier-drinking than the Barolo and Barbaresco across the river. Barbera and Favorita are also grown. Most producers are small and welcome visitors by appointment.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is mostly agriturismi and bed &amp; breakfasts among the vineyards, with a few country hotels and the castle hotel at Guarene. Bra and Alba (across the river) have the widest choice of town hotels. Book ahead in autumn.\nStay safe.\nThe Roero is very safe. The main hazards are narrow rural roads and, for walkers, steep and sometimes crumbly edges along the Rocche \u2014 keep to marked paths."}
