{"id": "219175", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219175", "title": "Nizza Monferrato", "text": "Nizza Monferrato is an arcaded market town of about 10,000 people (2017) in the province of Asti, in the Monferrato wine hills of Piedmont, Italy. It is the capital of the Nizza DOCG, the most ambitious expression of the Barbera grape, and a good, unpretentious base for exploring the surrounding vineyards.\nUnderstand.\nNizza sits in the Belbo valley where the Monferrato hills meet the northern edge of the Langhe. It grew as an agricultural market town \u2014 its arcaded streets and the grand former cattle market still fill with stalls on Saturdays \u2014 and remains centred on wine and food rather than tourism. Its best-known local product after Barbera is the \"Cardo Gobbo\", a \"hunchbacked\" cardoon grown by bending the plants under earth, eaten raw with the warm anchovy-and-garlic dip \"bagna cauda\".\nGet in.\nBy train.\nNizza Monferrato is on the regional railway between Asti and Acqui Terme; Asti (about 30 minutes) connects to the fast Turin\u2013Genoa and Turin\u2013Milan lines.\nBy car.\nFrom the A21 (Turin\u2013Piacenza) leave at Asti Est and follow the SP456 south, or reach Nizza on the valley roads from Canelli and Acqui Terme. There is parking around the Foro Boario and the town edges.\nGet around.\nThe compact historic centre is easily walked. A car (or a rented e-bike) is useful for reaching wineries in the surrounding hills.\nConnect.\nMobile coverage is good; many caf\u00e9s and hotels offer Wi-Fi."}
{"id": "219183", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219183", "title": "Right Bank (Paris)", "text": "The Right Bank in travellers' terms generally refers to the \"Right Bank\" (French: \"Rive Droite\") of the River Seine in the French capital city of Paris.\nThe Right Bank defies precise definition but generally refers to those \"arrondissements\" (districts) on the northern bank of the river as it flows through central Paris:\nThe Right Bank is mirrored by the term Left Bank, referring to \"arrondissements\" south of the river within central Paris, the general meaning of which contrasts strongly with that of the Right Bank."}
{"id": "219185", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219185", "title": "Maisons-Alfort", "text": "Maisons-Alfort is a suburb southeast of Paris, in Val-de-Marne."}
{"id": "219197", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219197", "title": "Stuart Town", "text": "Stuart Town is a small railway town in the Central West region of New South Wales.\nGet in.\nBy train.\n is the main port of entry into town. It receives one daily train from Sydney in the mornings and one from Dubbo in the early afternoon.\nBy car.\nStuart Town is 34 km south of Wellington and 60 km north of Orange, both via the Burrendong Way.\nGet around.\nThe town is small enough to be navigable on foot.\nSee.\nThe railway station is heritage-listed and is perhaps the only notable sight in town."}
{"id": "219207", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219207", "title": "Bentiu", "text": "Bentiu is the capital of Unity State in South Sudan, on the southern bank of the Bahr el Ghazal River in the Greater Upper Nile region. It is a frontier oil town rebuilding after repeated cycles of war, ethnic violence, and catastrophic flooding, and it sits at the heart of one of the most severe ongoing humanitarian crises in the world. This is not a conventional tourist destination \u2014 travel here is realistically limited to aid workers, journalists, oil-industry staff, UN personnel, and the rare very experienced overland traveller.\nUnderstand.\nBentiu lies on flat, low-lying grassland and floodplain roughly 654\u00a0km (406\u00a0mi) by road northwest of Juba, near the border with Sudan. The town sits opposite Rubkona across the Bahr el Ghazal River, the two linked by the El Salaam (\"Peace\") Bridge; Bentiu Airport lies just north of Rubkona.\nThe wider Bentiu area has been at the center of South Sudan's conflicts since independence in 2011. It saw one of the worst massacres of the 2013\u20132018 civil war in April 2014, when the town changed hands amid mass killings of civilians. Since 2014 the population has also been repeatedly hit by severe seasonal flooding \u2014 Unity State has experienced near-continuous flooding since 2019 \u2014 displacing tens of thousands of people into a large Internally Displaced Persons (IDP) site adjacent to the UN peacekeeping base (formerly a UN \"Protection of Civilians,\" or POC, site). Population counts of the IDP camp complex (Sites A\u2013E) have fluctuated between roughly 60,000 and 160,000 people depending on flooding, returns, and new arrivals \u2014 including, in in the 2020s, South Sudanese returnees fleeing the war in neighboring Sudan.\nBentiu town (outside the camp) has a much smaller population, historically only several thousand, though this fluctuates with displacement and resettlement.\nAs of early-to-mid 2026, South Sudan's peace process remains fragile: fighting between government (SSPDF) forces and opposition/allied armed groups has been reported across most of the country's states, First Vice President Riek Machar has been under house arrest since March 2025 facing treason-related charges, and the transitional government has formally requested closure of the UNMISS bases in Bentiu and Wau \u2014 a move the UN Security Council has warned could seriously hamper civilian protection and humanitarian operations in the area. Travelers should treat any information here as provisional and verify current conditions before making plans.\nGet in.\nBy air: There is no scheduled commercial passenger service comparable to major hubs, but domestic carriers (historically including Kush Air) have flown Juba\u2013Bentiu on an irregular commercial basis, with one-way fares around US$180 when operating. UNMISS and humanitarian air services (UNHAS) run more frequent flights between Juba and Bentiu, but seats are normally reserved for UN, embassy, and NGO staff \u2014 a spare seat is occasionally available if you have the right contacts. Charter flights can be arranged through tour/travel operators in Juba at additional cost. Bentiu Airport (airstrip) is just north of Rubkona, across the river from town.\nBy road: Overland travel from Juba is a long, rough journey over largely unpaved roads (South Sudan has only around 400\u00a0km of paved road nationwide) and is strongly discouraged for independent travelers given both road conditions and security risks along the route, particularly during the rainy season when roads can become impassable.\nBy river boat: Given seasonal flooding, boats are a common means of local transport into and around the Bentiu area, especially for people arriving at the IDP sites.\nGet around.\nMovement within Bentiu and to Rubkona is mainly on foot, by motorbike, or by vehicle on unpaved tracks; the El Salaam Bridge connects the two towns across the Bahr el Ghazal River. During the wet season (roughly April\u2013October) large parts of the surrounding area flood, and access can be cut off or severely limited. The UNMISS compound area has separately controlled access.\nSee.\nBentiu has essentially no formal tourist sites or infrastructure. What there is to \"see\" is the raw reality of a frontier town shaped by oil, conflict, and climate displacement:\nDo.\nThere is little in the way of organized activity for visitors. The annual Bentiu Cultural Festival (dates vary) has featured Dinka and Nuer folk music, wrestling, and traditional dance, drawing local youth, diaspora returnees, and the occasional traveller; entry fees have historically been in the US$10\u201320 range. Confirm whether the festival is running before planning around it, as timing and viability depend on the security and humanitarian situation.\nBuy.\nBentiu has no ATMs, and mobile money coverage is unreliable. Bring sufficient US dollars in small denominations to cover your entire stay. South Sudanese pounds are widely accepted but subject to significant value fluctuation, so budgeting in dollars is more predictable.\nEat.\nFormal restaurants are essentially nonexistent; food options are limited to basic local market fare and whatever catering exists at guesthouses or aid-agency compounds. As elsewhere in South Sudan, staples include dishes such as \"asida\" (a porridge) and \"kisra\" (a flatbread), alongside grains, dried fish, and meat where available.\nDrink.\nNo notable bar or drinking scene; options are informal and extremely limited. Bottled water and boiled/treated water are essential \u2014 do not assume tap or river water is safe.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is very basic, consisting mainly of guesthouses and compounds catering to aid organizations, UN staff, and oil-industry personnel. There is no tourist hotel infrastructure. Anyone travelling to Bentiu without institutional backing (NGO, UN, media, or oil-company logistics) will likely struggle to find secure lodging.\nConnect.\nCommunication infrastructure is fragile. Mobile networks exist but coverage is patchy and can fail without warning; the UNMISS compound area has more reliable power and water/communications infrastructure, but access there is tightly controlled. Satellite communication devices are advisable for anyone traveling independently in the area."}
{"id": "219227", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219227", "title": "Br\u010dko", "text": "Br\u010dko (\u0411\u0440\u0447\u043a\u043e) is a self-governing condominium in Bosnia and Herzegovina between the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina (FBiH) and Republika Srpska. For travel purposes, Wikivoyage covers it under Posavina, but it's not part of FBiH's Posavina Canton. \"Br\u010dko\" on its own usually refers to the city, but as the district centres around the city, the entire district is covered under this article. \nGet in.\nBy plane.\nTuzla International Airport () is the closest airport to Br\u010dko. \nBy coach (bus).\nFlixbus has two daily services from Zagreb, one leaving at \"&gt; arriving at \"&gt;, and the other leaving at \"&gt; arriving at \"&gt; the following day. Both services are operated by Transturist (meaning no bright green bus). You'll also need to pay a 3 KM/\u20ac1.50 luggage fee to the driver on board. \nServices from Tuzla are operated by Transturist directly.\nBy train.\nBr\u010dko is on the railway line between Vinkovci (HR) and Tuzla (BiH); however, railway services on the Bosnian side have been indefinitely suspended. From the Croatian side, Gunja, just across the border, gets thrice-daily services from Vinkovci, where you can then cross into BiH.\nBuy.\nTrg Mladih is a major pedestrianised street through the centre of Br\u010dko with many shops and dining options."}
