{"id": "219241", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219241", "title": "Una National Park", "text": "Una National Park (Serbo-Croatian: \u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0438 \u043f\u0430\u0440\u043a \u0423\u043d\u0430, \"Nacionalni park Una\") is Bosnia and Herzegovina's largest park in Bosanska Krajina. It protects parts of the Una River and some of its tributaries. It's perhaps most known for the waterfalls around Martin Brod, which have been on the tentative list for UNESCO World Heritage since 2019.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe park is a short drive away from Biha\u0107, southeast via the M-5 highway.\nBy train.\nZFBH operate a twice-daily weekend train from Biha\u0107 to Bosanska Otoka, passing through the park, leaving Biha\u0107 at 09:00 and 16:00, returning 11:45 and 18:20. Tickets are a very affordable 2 KM/person.\nFees and permits.\nPark entrance fees can be found on the park website (in English). Prices vary based on season and which section you're visiting; a single adult price can vary anywhere from 5.50 KM to 25 KM."}
{"id": "219248", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219248", "title": "Pateley Bridge", "text": "Pateley Bridge is a town in North Yorkshire. Here the River Nidd emerges from the karstic valleys of the Yorkshire Dales into the broad plain of the Vale of York, so the town is a good base for exploring both areas. In 2024 the population was 1300. \nGet in.\nFrom Harrogate follow B6165 northwest, 14 miles. From Ripon follow B6265 west: this continues into the Dales towards Grassington. \nHarrogate Bus 24 runs from Harrogate (the nearest railway station), taking a hour via Darley and Summerbridge. It's every 2 hours M-Sa with only 3 on Sunday.\nDales Bus 820 / 821 runs four times on Sundays May-Oct from Otley, taking an hour. One starts from Keighley, and two continue to Fountains Abbey and Ripon.\nDales Bus 822 runs four times on Sundays Apr-Oct from Ripon, taking 40 min via Fountains Abbey. One starts from York, and two continue to Stump Cross Caverns and Grassington.\n is the bus turn-around in Pateley Bridge.\nGet around.\nTown is small and walkable, but you need your own wheels to explore the district.\nConnect.\nAs of July 2026, Pateley Bridge and its approach roads have 4G from O2, Three and Vodafone, and 5G from EE."}
{"id": "219254", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219254", "title": "Ilambazar", "text": "Ilambazar or Illambazar (\u0987\u09b2\u09be\u09ae\u09ac\u09be\u099c\u09be\u09b0 \"Il\u0101mb\u0101j\u0101r\") is a lesser-known town in the Birbhum district of West Bengal, on the way from Durgapur to Santiniketan.\nUnderstand.\nIlambazar (\"EE-lahm-BAH-jahr\") was a major trade centre on the Ajay River because to its strategic location at the road junction. Both the British and the French established factories at Ilambazar in the late 18th century. However, it was the British who assumed control over the whole Birbhum in 1786.\nIn the mid-19th century, John Erskine of Ilambazar was one of the leading sugar manufacturers and exporters in the area. Ilambazar was once a significant area for indigo cultivation, traces of which can still be found. The Erskine family also had indigo factories.\nSee.\nTemples.\nThere are three temples in Ilambazar with beautiful terracotta designs."}
{"id": "219256", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219256", "title": "Rajnagar (West Bengal)", "text": "Rajnagar (\u09b0\u09be\u099c\u09a8\u0997\u09b0 \"R\u0101jnogor\") is a historical town in the Birbhum district of West Bengal.\nUnderstand.\nFounded by Bir Singh, a local Hindu ruler, Rajnagar later fell under the control of Muhammad Shiran and Ali Mardan in the early 13th century. It then came under the Mughal Empire, with the Nagar Raj zamindars of Rajnagar being the feudatories of the Mughals.\nThe Nagar Raj was established by Jonad Khan in 1600. It witnessed a period of autonomy during the rule of Asadullah Khan. His son Badiuzzaman tried to shake off the allegiance to the Nawab of Murshidabad. He was awarded the title of \"Raja\" by Nawab Murshid Quli Khan. During his reign Birbhum was ravaged by the Marathas.\nThe Nagar Raj witnessed the zenith of its power during the rule of his son Asaduzzaman. Although he remained faithful to the Nawab, the British and Nawab Mir Qasim later defeated him in 1761, and again in 1765. Although his estate was restored by a treaty, his autonomy was curbed significantly. Eventually, the Nagar Raj was overshadowed by the Hetampur Raj in Dubrajpur. After the capture of Birbhum in 1786, the British set up the headquarters at nearby Suri. This led many to leave Rajnagar for Suri and Dubrajpur."}
{"id": "219257", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219257", "title": "Ugljan", "text": "Ugljan is an island in Northern Dalmatia, Croatia. It is the closest of the Zadar Archipelago islands to the mainland, lying just across the Zadar Channel from the city of Zadar, and is sometimes called the \"Olive Island\" for the centuries-old olive groves that cover its hills.\nUnderstand.\nUgljan is a green, hilly island about 22 km long and up to roughly 4 km wide, with its highest point, \u0160\u0107ah, reaching 286 m. It is one of the more densely populated Croatian islands, with around 6,000 residents spread across a string of villages along its northeastern, Zadar-facing coast. The southern tip is joined to the neighbouring island of Pa\u0161man by a short bridge, so the two islands share a single road running their whole length.\nOlives have been cultivated here for more than 2,000 years, and olive oil, wine, figs and fishing still shape island life. Compared with better-known Croatian islands, Ugljan sees relatively few tourists and keeps a quiet, authentic feel, which makes it popular with hikers, cyclists and travellers looking for a calm base close to Zadar. In 2016 it was named \"Best European Hidden Gem\" by the Best European Destinations portal, and locals sometimes call it the \"garden of Zadar\".\nVillages.\nThe settlements line up along the coast facing Zadar. From north to south:\nClimate.\nUgljan has a typical Mediterranean climate: hot, dry summers and mild, wetter winters. Summer (June\u2013September) is the main travel season; spring and autumn are excellent for walking and cycling.\nGet in.\nBy boat.\nRegular car ferries run between Zadar (Ga\u017eenica) and Preko, taking roughly 20\u201325 minutes, operated by Jadrolinija. There is also a faster passenger boat from Zadar's old-town waterfront to Preko. Sailings are frequent in summer and reduced off-season.\nBy car.\nFrom the south you can drive onto Ugljan across the bridge from Pa\u0161man. Pa\u0161man in turn connects to the mainland via the Tkon\u2013Biograd ferry, making a road-and-ferry loop possible via Biograd na Moru.\nGet around.\nA single main road runs the length of the island linking all the villages and continues over the bridge onto Pa\u0161man. A car or scooter is the easiest way to explore end to end, but the island is a favourite with cyclists thanks to its quiet roads and network of trails; several campsites, hotels and rental outlets hire out bikes (including e-bikes). A limited local bus connects the villages \u2014 check schedules locally. In summer, taxi-boats from Preko run to the islet of Galevac and to nearby beaches.\nBuy.\nSmall supermarkets, bakeries and local shops are found in Preko, Kali and Kukljica; larger stocking-up is easier in Zadar. Look for locally produced olive oil, wine and dried figs, sold direct by producers in several villages.\nEat.\nFamily-run taverns (\"konoba\") across the villages serve fresh Adriatic fish and shellfish, grilled meats, pasta and local olive oil. Kali and Kukljica have the best concentration of restaurants.\nDrink.\nSeasonal caf\u00e9s, gelaterias and beach bars operate in Preko, Kukljica and along the waterfronts in summer. Try local wines and \"rakija\". The beach bar at Camping Ugljan (see Sleep) is open to non-guests and has a good sea view.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is mostly private apartments, rooms and small guesthouses rather than large hotels, concentrated in Preko, Kukljica, Kali and Lukoran. There is also a campsite and a hotel in Kali. Book well ahead for July and August.\nConnect.\nMobile coverage is good across the island, and most accommodation offers Wi-Fi.\nStay safe.\nUgljan is very safe. The usual coastal precautions apply: wear water shoes on rocky and pebble beaches, take care with sun exposure in summer, and carry water on longer hikes, as trails can be exposed and shadeless. The nearest hospital is in Zadar."}
{"id": "219263", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219263", "title": "Aalto Works", "text": "Alvar Aalto (1898-1976) was one of Finland's foremost architects and designers. His works span much of the 20th century and include modernist buildings of many different styles from functionalist buildings in the interwar era to the grand Finlandia Hall that was finished in 1971, a few years before his death. In 2026, thirteen of Aalto's works were listed as the UNESCO World Heritage Site Aalto Works."}
