{"id": "219270", "revid": "265484", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219270", "title": "Skive", "text": "Skive is a town in West Jutland on the south side of the Limfjord Strait. In the summer, the town attracts tourists to the marina and town centre, but Skive is not a tourist magnet. While Skive definitely presents interesting sees and dos for tourists, prepare for a laid back experience, or use the town as a stopover on your way to and from other destinations in northwest Jutland.\nUnderstand.\nAt just a little more than 20,000 residents, Skive is the urban centre for the rural peninsula of Salling, and the smaller peninsula of Nord-Fjends. Skive is a very old settlement with a long history; the site has been inhabited continuously since at least the late Stone Age, with several ancient stone settings in the area. The town was largely built since the 1800s, when the industrial harbour and the railway was constructed.\nIn 2026, Skive celebrates its 700-year anniversary as a market town, with several events throughout the year.\nSkive is home to the largest military barracks in Denmark, which leaves a significant imprint on the town. \nSkive is the gateway to the large hinterland comprising the Salling peninsula, Nordfjends peninsula, and the two islands of Mors and Fur, all part of the Limfjord region. Mors and Fur became a UNESCO world heritage site in 2026, due to their important fossil sites from the Eocene epoch.\nTourist information.\nSkive doesn't have its own tourist information office, but maintains the Skive Portalen web-site with programmes, schedules, and information on what's going on. \nDestination Limfjorden is the tourism authority in the Skive region.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nSkive and the Salling region is served by the Midttrafik bus company, as the rest of West Jutland (and East Jutland), and is well-connected by bus lines. \nTake a blue regional bus to Skive Central Station from any larger town in West or East Jutland.\nFor local transport in and around Skive, take one of the local bus lines here. They are also run by Midttrafik. Remember that you can use your train tickets on the buslines as well, if you onboard within the time limit. Skive used to have its own city-bus system, but it closed permanently in June 2026. For a complete overview of collective transport in and around Skive, check the local government website here.\nSkive is an important bus stop on \"Prim\u00e6rrute 26\" (Primary Route 26), connecting Aarhus to Hanstholm. This is the quickest route you can take to Thisted, Hanstholm, and the whole region of Thy, in North Jutland.\nBy train.\nArrive by train via Viborg or Struer. \nThe train from Viborg takes half an hour, and from Aarhus is 1\u00bd hour.\nThe Struer Central Station connects to the rest of West Jutland and also the northwestern part of North Jutland, beyond the Limfjord, known as Thy. The rail line is called \"Thybanen\" (Thy Line) and traverse a railway bridge to Thyholm and Hurup Thy, and onwards to the town of Thisted further north. If you are going to the northwest, by the Salling peninsula, take a bus, or drive your own car.\nRemember you can use your train tickets on buslines as well, if you onboard within the time limit.\nBy bicycle.\nNational Bicycle Route N12 pass through Skive from Nordfjends and trace the coasts of Salling on its way to Struer. The route is also known as \"Limfjordsruten\" (The Limfjord Route) and runs along the entire Limfjord for a total of 630 km.\nNational Bicycle Route N2 pass through Skive from Viborg on its way northwest towards Mors and Hanstholm in the Thy region. It is also known as The Hanstholm-Copenhagen Route.\nGet around.\nWalking is a great (and the only) way to experience Skives old pedestrianised town centre. You can also easily walk to all other town destinations.\nBy car.\nThere are many parking lots around the town centre. Most have charging stations, and a few have gas stations. You can easily drive to most spots in Skive, and a car is the best, quickest, and most comfortable option, if you want to explore the hinterlands of Salling and Nordfjends. \nSee.\nCountryside.\nWhen you include the hinterland tegion around Skive, there are many points of interest. See the Go next section or have a look at these sites:\nDo.\nHiking.\nThere are several interesting hiking trails in and around Skive.\nDrink.\nBars and pubs in Skive:\nRegional breweries and distilleries:\nConnect.\nAs in the rest of Denmark, hotels and camping sites offers free Wi-Fi for their guests.\nStay healthy.\nThere are several pharmacies in Skive, but they all seems to be closed on Sundays.\nIn case of emergency, call +45 70 11 31 31. The regional hospital in Skive serves the entire Skive region, and this is also where you will find the accident and emergency center:\nAlways call in advance! And have your ID and health insurance cards ready upon arrival. In a rare and extreme life and death situation, in Denmark, you call 112.\nGo next.\nSkive is the gateway to the rural Salling peninsula, the small Nordfjends peninsula, and the islands of Mors and Fur. As part of that, Skive is an important stop on the interregional road Primary Route 26; the quickest route to Thisted, Hanstholm, and the whole region of Thy in North Jutland.\nSalling and Nordfjends.\nSalling is the large peninsula north and west of Skive.\nBeautiful lake area west of Skive.\nNordfjends.\nNordfjends is the smaller peninsula northeast of Skive.\nMors and Fur.\nMors is a large island northwest of Salling and Fur is a smaller island north of Salling, both in the Limfjord. \nFur and Mors have been a geological hotspot for many years, with important fossil sites from the Eocene specifically (33-55 million years ago), when this part of the world was a subtropical ocean with sea turtles and sea snakes. In 2026, both islands became listed by UNESCO as a natural world heritage site named \"The Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord \u2013 Evolution and Climate Adaptation in the Earliest Eocene\".\nFur.\nThere is no bridge to Fur, so you will have to take the ferry or sail your own leisure boat.\nMors.\nMors, occasionally referred to as \"Mors\u00f8\", is a farmer's island with an important traffic connection to the western parts of North Jutland; the regions also known as Thy and Han Herred. The main town is Nyk\u00f8bing Mors, and this is also where you will find the official tourist office for the island.\nAt the northern tip of Mors, you can take a ferry across the narrow strait of Feggesund to reach the regions of Han Herred and Thy. \nThere are plenty of opportunities for good nature hiking on Mors. "}
{"id": "219279", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219279", "title": "Riola Sardo", "text": "Riola Sardo is a town in Oristano province. Agritourism is a very important economic activity in the town, which is just a few kilometers from the most beautiful beaches of the Sinis Peninsula. Its territory is rich in birdlife, especially aquatic species. In addition to the Cabras and Riola Sardo ponds, the area includes several perennial marshes and part of the Sale Porcus salt pan, home to the greater flamingo.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe SS 292 Bosa-Oristano allows you to reach Riola Sardo.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 bus line stops in Riola Sardo."}
{"id": "219281", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219281", "title": "Condominio theatres in Central Italy", "text": "The system of Italian-style condominio theatres of the 18th and 19th centuries in Central Italy is a UNESCO World Heritage Site consisting of ten theater buildings.\nUnderstand.\nDuring the 18th and 19th centuries several theatre buildings were built in smaller towns in Central Italy. They were financed by municipalities and individuals through a system named \"condominio\". \nBuilt in Neoclassicist and Rococo styles, these beautiful theatre buildings became the centre of cultural life in these smaller towns as venues for theatre performances and for other public events. \nIn 2026 eight such theatre buildings in Marche and one each in Emilia-Romagna and Umbria were listed as a World Heritage Site."}
