{"id": "219320", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219320", "title": "Hanumangarh", "text": "Hanumangarh is a town in the Bagar region of Rajasthan. It is home to the 1700-year-old Bhatner Fort, considered to be one of the oldest forts of India.\nUnderstand.\nHanumangarh is actually a pair of adjacent towns in northwestern Rajasthan, across the Ghaggar River. The Bhatner Fort is at \"Hanumangarh Town\", whereas the other is called \"Hanumangarh Junction\" after the town's main station."}
{"id": "219342", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219342", "title": "Lermoos", "text": "Lermoos is a village in the Au\u00dferfern, Northwest Tyrol in Austria. It sits at 1,004 m on a sunny shelf above a wide flat moor basin, staring straight across at the Zugspitze. It is one of seven villages in the Tiroler Zugspitz Arena, and it is the one most people picture when they picture an Austrian ski village: small, wooden, clustered around a lift station, with a mountain view that does most of the work.\nUnderstand.\nLermoos has roughly 1,150 residents and belongs to the district of Reutte. The village splits into Unterdorf and Oberdorf, plus the smaller quarters of Gries, Obergarten, Untergarten and Widum. Unterdorf is the main street and where most of the shops and restaurants are; Kirchplatz sits on a small rise above it with the church and a couple of the older hotels.\nThe view is the reason to come. Lermoos faces the Wetterstein massif and the Zugspitze (2,962 m, Germany's highest), with the pyramid of the Ehrwalder Sonnenspitze (2,417 m) off to the east and the Mieminger chain behind it. Its own mountain, the Grubigstein (around 2,230 m), rises directly behind the village and carries the ski area and bike park. Between Lermoos and the mountains lies the Lermooser Moos, a flat former marshland of hay meadows and drainage ditches shared with Ehrwald and Biberwier. It is unusual scenery for the Alps and it is what gives the village its wide-open foreground.\nPeople have been passing through here for a very long time. The Roman road Via Claudia Augusta crossed the basin on its way over the Fernpass, and the first written mentions of the settlement date to the 1070s, though some sources push it back a decade or so earlier. The location on the Fernpass route made Lermoos a trading and staging post, and after the Second World War it grew into the biggest tourism destination in the Au\u00dferfern. The village now offers something like 4,200 beds including campsites and racks up over half a million overnight stays a year, weighted heavily towards winter.\nThere is a nice bit of history attached to the old Drei Mohren inn, now the MOHR life resort. In 1813 the Bavarian crown sold the Blindsee, Mittersee and Weissensee to the Mohren innkeeper, and King Ludwig II used to stop here on his way between Hohenschwangau and the Fernpass or the Schachen. He apparently liked being rowed out onto the Weissensee on full moon nights. His room is still preserved.\nWorth being clear about the comparison with Garmisch-Partenkirchen, 24 km north: Garmisch is a town, Lermoos is a village. Garmisch sits at the foot of the Zugspitze, Lermoos looks at it from across a valley, which is arguably the better deal if you want the mountain in your photos. Lermoos is quieter, cheaper to sleep in, and has maybe a dozen restaurants in total. If you want nightlife beyond a handful of ski bars, you are in the wrong valley.\nGet in.\nRoad distances from Lermoos: Ehrwald 4.6 km, Fernpass 10 km, Reutte 20 km, Nassereith 20 km, Garmisch-Partenkirchen 24 km, Imst 31 km, Innsbruck 72 km, Kempten 74 km, Munich 115 km.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Innsbruck, about 75 km by road over the Fernpass. Munich is the bigger option at roughly 140 km, and has far more connections plus a direct train link most of the way. Memmingen also works if you are flying budget, though you will want a car from there.\nBy train.\nLermoos has a station on the Au\u00dferfernbahn, a single-track line running from Garmisch-Partenkirchen to Reutte and on to Pfronten and Kempten, crossing the border twice. Some services run through from Innsbruck. Trains are roughly hourly and stop at six stations inside the Tiroler Zugspitz Arena: Ehrwald, Lermoos, L\u00e4hn bei Bichlbach, Bichlbach-Berwang, Bichlbach Almkopfbahn and Heiterwang.\nFrom Munich, take the regional train to Garmisch and change; the whole trip is around three hours. From Innsbruck you can either go over the Fernpass by bus or ride the train the long way round via Garmisch.\nOne useful quirk if you are coming from Germany: the Deutschlandticket is valid on this line from Pfronten via Reutte to all six Arena stations, and also from Munich via Garmisch and Griesen. That makes getting here absurdly cheap. It does not cover Austrian local buses once you are here, so do not assume it works for the ski bus.\nBy bus.\nRegional buses connect Lermoos with Reutte and, over the Fernpass, with Imst and Innsbruck. Local lines link the villages inside the Arena and the lift valley stations. Check current timetables through VVT Smartride, since services thin out considerably outside the main seasons.\nBy car.\nThe Fernpass is the problem. It is one of the main Alpine crossings for German holiday traffic and it jams badly on summer weekends and at the start and end of German school holidays. Tyrol runs anti-rat-run driving bans on minor roads through the Reutte and Imst districts on weekends and public holidays from roughly May to November, 07:00-19:00, specifically to stop satnavs routing people through villages. The B179 itself is not covered by those bans, but it does occasionally get closed outright for protests connected to the proposed Fernpass tunnel. Check the Tyrol traffic info before you set off on a summer Saturday, and if you can travel midweek, do.\nParking at the Grubigsteinbahn valley station is free.\nGet around.\nThe village is small enough that you can walk from one end to the other in about fifteen minutes, and the lift base station, most hotels and all the restaurants are inside that radius.\nThe guest card is the thing to know about. Every overnight guest gets the Tiroler Zugspitz Arena guest card from their host, either as a plastic card or digitally. It gives free travel on the regional buses (not trains), free entry to a list of guided hikes and events, and discounts on a long list of other things. Ask for it at check-in if nobody offers.\nBuses run roughly hourly between Lermoos, Ehrwald, Biberwier and the valley stations of the Tiroler Zugspitzbahn and the Ehrwalder Almbahn, which is what you want for the big hikes. In winter the same network runs as a ski bus.\nThe Z-Ticket is the summer add-on and it is good value if you plan to use the lifts more than a couple of times. Valid 3 to 13 days between mid-May and early November, it covers one ride on the Tiroler Zugspitzbahn, one round trip daily on the Ehrwalder Almbahn and the Grubigsteinbahnen, the Marienbergbahn and Biberwier summer toboggan run once a day, the Berwang and Bichlbach lifts, the Postbus and Rundbus daily, one boat trip on the Heiterwanger See and Plansee, and daily entry to the Lermoos panorama pool, plus 20% off various sports providers. There is a reduced rate in the shoulder seasons if you are booked for at least seven nights and show your guest card when buying.\nBikes can be rented in the village and several hotels lend them out. Lermoos sits on the Via Claudia Augusta cycle route, which follows the valley floor and is flat and easy.\nSee.\nGrubigstein.\nThe local mountain and the reason the lifts exist. In summer the Hochmoosexpress runs up to the Brettlalm mid-station at the Gschwandtkreuz, and the ten-person Grubig II gondola continues to the Grubigh\u00fctte at around 2,050 m. From the top station the panorama takes in the whole Wetterstein wall, the Ehrwald basin and the Mieminger chain. The summit itself is about an hour's walk from the Grubigh\u00fctte.\nTuftlalm.\nThe Tuftlalm (also called the Lermooser Alm, about 1,495 m) is the classic short walk from the village and probably the single best thing to do here if you only have a morning. The path starts near the train station and takes 1\u00bc to 1\u00bd hours with roughly 500 m of climbing. The hut runs its own farm and serves a short menu of local food, and there is a viewing platform a few minutes further on that looks straight down over Lermoos with the Zugspitze behind.\nThe Moos.\nThe flat moor between Lermoos, Ehrwald and Biberwier is criss-crossed with gravel tracks and it is the best place in the region to just walk on the level. It is also where locals go to watch the Bergfeuer in June, and where the cross-country tracks are laid in winter. Early morning and late evening light are worth getting up for, since the whole Wetterstein wall catches it.\nZugspitze.\nNot in Lermoos, but it is the thing on the skyline and you will want to go up. The Tiroler Zugspitzbahn leaves from Ehrwald, 4.6 km away and reachable by bus, and climbs 1,725 m to in about ten minutes in a single panoramic cabin. It turns 100 in 2026. The summer season usually runs mid-May to early November; adult return tickets are somewhere in the region of \u20ac43-66 depending on the option. The Austrian side is generally less crowded than the Bavarian approach from Garmisch and the Eibsee, and you can walk between the two summit terraces once you are up there.\nSeebensee and Drachensee.\nTwo of the most photographed mountain lakes in Tyrol, reached from Ehrwald rather than Lermoos, but close enough to count as a day out from here. Take the bus to the Ehrwalder Almbahn valley station (free parking there too if you drive) and ride up. From the top station it is about 1\u00bd to 2 hours each way to the Seebensee at 1,657 m, on a wide track that turns into a proper path, and it is an easy walk by alpine standards. Adding the Coburger H\u00fctte (1,917 m) and the Drachensee above it means another 45 minutes up a steep, narrow, rocky series of switchbacks, and that section needs sure feet and decent boots.\nThe full loop back over the Tajat\u00f6rl takes 5-6 hours and dodges most of the crowds. Do go early. This is one of the busiest trails in Tyrol on a fine summer weekend and the path between the two lakes can feel like a queue.\nDo.\nSkiing.\nThe Grubigstein ski area runs from the village at 1,004 m up to about 2,100 m with 8 lifts and roughly 27 km of pistes plus a couple of kilometres of marked ski routes. Around 10.8 km is graded easy and 12.6 km intermediate, with a bit over 3 km of black runs and some mogul terrain for anyone who wants it. It is not a big area and strong skiers will exhaust it in a couple of days, but it is more varied than the numbers suggest and the views across to the Zugspitze from the top are hard to beat. There is a natural snowpark below the Gamsjet chair, a children's park down in the valley area, and the Blueline XXL family run from top to bottom. Night skiing runs Wednesday evenings roughly 18:00-20:30 on the floodlit run at the Familyjet.\nDay tickets cover all 37 lifts across Ehrwald, Lermoos and Biberwier. Buy any multi-day ticket and you get the Top Snow Card, valid across the whole Tiroler Zugspitz Arena plus Garmisch-Classic, Grainau, Mittenwald and the Zugspitze glacier: ten areas, roughly 205 km of piste, 89 lifts. That is the real argument for basing yourself here, since you can ski a different mountain most days and the glacier extends the season at both ends. Children up to 5 ski free on presentation of ID.\nTobogganing.\nThe 2.8-km natural run from the Brettlalm mid-station down to the Hochmoosexpress base is the longest in the Arena. It is floodlit on Wednesday evenings, usually 18:00-21:30, with sledge rental on site. There is a second run at the Grubigstein.\nCross-country.\nFive easy tracks run through the Moos, part of about 120 km of free trails across the wider region. Flat, scenic and about as gentle an introduction to the sport as you will find. There is also an ice rink near the lift station, and snowshoe hikes and torchlight walks run free with the guest card on set dates.\nMountain biking.\nThe Bikepark Lermoos-Biberwier, now branded the Bike Arena Zugspitz, is one of the better ones in Tyrol if you like natural, rooty, rocky trails rather than manicured flow lines. Two gondolas take you to 2,030 m and there are over 20 trails with more than 1,000 m of descent available. Season is roughly mid-May to early November, 08:30-17:00, though Grubig II runs shorter hours and some trails are only reachable via the Grubigalm chair in the shoulder season. Helmet and protectors are compulsory and riding is only permitted on marked trails. There is a skill area right next to the valley station for warming up.\nHiking.\nBeyond the Tuftlalm and the lakes above Ehrwald:\nGuided walks run through the summer and several are free with the guest card, including a full-moon hike and a herb and orchid walk. Register at the tourist office a day or two ahead.\nBuy.\nA small supermarket and a bakery cover daily needs, and there are two banks with ATMs on Unterdorf. For a proper shop, go to Ehrwald or Reutte. Some huts, including the Brettlalm, are cash only, so carry some. There is one petrol station in the village.\nEat.\nThe village has maybe a dozen places, and in high season you should book. Several close one day a week and the day varies, so it is worth a phone call rather than a walk.\nDrink.\nNightlife here means apr\u00e8s-ski and then bed. In summer several of these close or keep short hours.\nSleep.\nLermoos has around 4,200 beds and the bulk of them are apartments and small pensions rather than hotels. Winter is the peak and prices reflect that; late spring and late autumn are cheap but a lot of lifts and restaurants shut.\nOther.\nApartments and holiday flats far outnumber hotel rooms here and are usually the best value for a week or more, especially for groups. The tourist office and the regional booking site both list them.\nStay safe.\nStandard alpine caution applies. Weather in this basin turns fast and afternoon thunderstorms in summer are common, so start early and be off the ridges by early afternoon. The Daniel circuit and the Coburger H\u00fctte approach both have sections where a slip matters, and both get slick in rain. Boots, not trainers, however easy the gondola makes the start of the walk look.\nIn winter, the off-piste around the Grubigstein is avalanche terrain and the ski area sits low enough that snow conditions vary a lot; check the report before committing to a multi-day pass.\nThe emergency number is 112. Alpine rescue in Austria is not free, so travel insurance covering mountain rescue is a good idea."}
{"id": "219349", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219349", "title": "Pandhurna", "text": "Pandhurna (\u092a\u093e\u0902\u0922\u0941\u0930\u0928\u093e \"P\u0101\u1e47\u1e0dhurn\u0101\") is a small town on the banks of the Jam River in Madhya Pradesh, India. It's not a place that shows up on the usual India tourist trail, and honestly, most of the year there's not a whole lot happening here. But once a year, for one single evening, this quiet orange-farming town turns into the site of one of the strangest and most talked-about festivals in the country."}
{"id": "219351", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219351", "title": "Nieuwegein", "text": "Nieuwegein (population 68,000) is a city in Utrecht province in the Netherlands, about 5 km south of Utrecht and just north of the river Lek. Most of Nieuwegein was built as a planned city since the 1970s but it does have its pretty sides, too.\nUnderstand.\nDespite its sizable population, Nieuwegein can essentially be considered a suburb of Utrecht. It was founded in 1971 mainly to accommodate Utrecht's rapidly growing population. The city was developed around the older villages of Jutphaas and Vreeswijk, whose character has been at least partly preserved. Therefore, somewhat unusually for a planned city, Nieuwegein still features around 100 listed monuments.\nGet in.\nNieuwegein is bounded by three motorways, the A2, A12 and A27, which all have a (different) exit named \"Nieuwegein\". The exits \"Utrecht-Lunetten\" and \"Utrecht-Zuid\" on the A12 and \"Nieuwegein Zuid\" on the A2 also serve the city. All these motorways can get quite congested during rush hour.\nNieuwegein does not have a train station and instead relies on Utrecht for connections to the rest of the country. There are frequent connections to Utrecht's main station, Utrecht Centraal, either by tram (lines 20 and 21) or bus (65, 66, 74, 77). All these buses serve slightly different routes within Nieuwegein, so make sure to double-check where exactly the bus is going when you board it at Utrecht Centraal.\nTram line 21 continues to IJsselstein and bus line 48 connects Nieuwegein with the smaller train stations in Houten and Maarssen. There is also a small ferry (pedestrians/cyclists \u20ac1.25/\u20ac1.75) across the river Lek to Vianen. Most other destinations are best reached with a change in Utrecht or by car or bike.\nGet around.\nMany amenities are within short walking distance from the \"Nieuwegein City\" tram stop, but otherwise distances within the city can be quite long. One of the aforementioned bus or tram lines might provide a connection, otherwise use a bike or car. \nEat.\nA growing number of food options can be found in and around Cityplaza, but there are some hidden gems to be found around town, especially in the older neighbourhoods of Jutphaas and Vreeswijk."}
